const DATA=[{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"I","loc":"Athens. A room in the Palace of Theseus.","id":0},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter THESEUS, HIPPOLYTA, PHILOSTRATE and Attendants","id":1},{"t":"sp","who":"THESEUS","lines":["Now, fair Hippolyta, our nuptial hour","Draws on apace; four happy days bring in","Another moon; but oh, methinks, how slow","This old moon wanes! She lingers my desires,","Like to a step-dame or a dowager,","Long withering out a young man's revenue."],"id":2,"m":"Now, beautiful Hippolyta, our wedding day is coming fast. In four happy days there will be a new moon. But oh, how slowly this old moon seems to fade! She keeps me waiting for what I want, like a stepmother or a widow who lives on and on, eating up the money a young heir is waiting to inherit."},{"t":"sp","who":"HIPPOLYTA","lines":["Four days will quickly steep themselves in night;","Four nights will quickly dream away the time;","And then the moon, like to a silver bow","New bent in heaven, shall behold the night","Of our solemnities."],"id":3,"m":"Four days will quickly sink into night, and four nights will quickly pass in dreams. Then the new moon, like a silver bow freshly bent in the sky, will look down on the night of our wedding."},{"t":"sp","who":"THESEUS","lines":["Go, Philostrate,","Stir up the Athenian youth to merriments;","Awake the pert and nimble spirit of mirth;","Turn melancholy forth to funerals;","The pale companion is not for our pomp."],"id":4,"m":"Go, Philostrate, get the young people of Athens ready to celebrate. Wake up the lively, quick spirit of fun. Send sadness off to funerals, where it belongs. That pale-faced companion has no place at our festivities."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit PHILOSTRATE","id":5},{"t":"sp","who":"THESEUS","cont":true,"lines":["Hippolyta, I woo'd thee with my sword,","And won thy love doing thee injuries;","But I will wed thee in another key,","With pomp, with triumph, and with revelling."],"id":6,"m":"Hippolyta, I courted you with my sword, and won your love by doing you harm in battle. But I will marry you in a different style: with grand shows, with public celebrations, and with partying."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter EGEUS, HERMIA, LYSANDER and DEMETRIUS","id":7},{"t":"sp","who":"EGEUS","lines":["Happy be Theseus, our renownèd Duke!"],"id":8,"m":"Long life and happiness to Theseus, our famous Duke!"},{"t":"sp","who":"THESEUS","lines":["Thanks, good Egeus. What's the news with thee?"],"id":9,"m":"Thank you, good Egeus. What news do you bring?"},{"t":"sp","who":"EGEUS","lines":["Full of vexation come I, with complaint","Against my child, my daughter Hermia.","Stand forth, Demetrius. My noble lord,","This man hath my consent to marry her.","Stand forth, Lysander. And, my gracious Duke,","This man hath bewitch'd the bosom of my child.","Thou, thou, Lysander, thou hast given her rhymes,","And interchang'd love-tokens with my child.","Thou hast by moonlight at her window sung,","With feigning voice, verses of feigning love;","And stol'n the impression of her fantasy","With bracelets of thy hair, rings, gauds, conceits,","Knacks, trifles, nosegays, sweetmeats (messengers","Of strong prevailment in unharden'd youth)","With cunning hast thou filch'd my daughter's heart,","Turn'd her obedience (which is due to me)","To stubborn harshness. And, my gracious Duke,","Be it so she will not here before your grace","Consent to marry with Demetrius,","I beg the ancient privilege of Athens:","As she is mine I may dispose of her;","Which shall be either to this gentleman","Or to her death, according to our law","Immediately provided in that case."],"id":10,"m":"I come here full of anger, to complain about my own child, my daughter Hermia. Step forward, Demetrius. My noble lord, this man has my permission to marry her. Step forward, Lysander. And, my gracious Duke, this man has put a spell on my child's heart. You, you, Lysander, you have given her poems and swapped love-tokens with her. You have sung at her window by moonlight in a fake, sweet voice, singing verses of fake love. You have stolen her imagination with bracelets made of your hair, rings, cheap toys, trinkets, little gifts, flowers and sweets, which are powerful persuaders for a young girl who knows no better. With your tricks you have stolen my daughter's heart, and turned the obedience she owes me into stubbornness. So, my gracious Duke, if she will not agree here, in front of you, to marry Demetrius, I ask for the old right of every Athenian father. Since she belongs to me, I may do what I like with her: either give her to this gentleman or have her put to death, as our law clearly lays down for this case."},{"t":"sp","who":"THESEUS","lines":["What say you, Hermia? Be advis'd, fair maid.","To you your father should be as a god;","One that compos'd your beauties, yea, and one","To whom you are but as a form in wax","By him imprinted, and within his power","To leave the figure, or disfigure it.","Demetrius is a worthy gentleman."],"id":11,"m":"What do you say, Hermia? Think carefully, pretty girl. Your father should be like a god to you. He made your beauty. To him you are like a wax figure he has shaped, and he has the power to leave the figure as it is or to destroy it. Demetrius is a fine gentleman."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIA","lines":["So is Lysander."],"id":12,"m":"So is Lysander."},{"t":"sp","who":"THESEUS","lines":["In himself he is.","But in this kind, wanting your father's voice,","The other must be held the worthier."],"id":13,"m":"As a man, he is. But in this matter, since he does not have your father's support, the other man must be considered the better one."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIA","lines":["I would my father look'd but with my eyes."],"id":14,"m":"I wish my father could see with my eyes."},{"t":"sp","who":"THESEUS","lines":["Rather your eyes must with his judgment look."],"id":15,"m":"Instead, your eyes must see with his judgement."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIA","lines":["I do entreat your Grace to pardon me.","I know not by what power I am made bold,","Nor how it may concern my modesty","In such a presence here to plead my thoughts:","But I beseech your Grace that I may know","The worst that may befall me in this case,","If I refuse to wed Demetrius."],"id":16,"m":"I beg your Grace to forgive me. I don't know what has made me so bold, or whether it is proper for a girl to speak her mind in front of such great people. But please tell me the worst that can happen to me if I refuse to marry Demetrius."},{"t":"sp","who":"THESEUS","lines":["Either to die the death, or to abjure","For ever the society of men.","Therefore, fair Hermia, question your desires,","Know of your youth, examine well your blood,","Whether, if you yield not to your father's choice,","You can endure the livery of a nun,","For aye to be in shady cloister mew'd,","To live a barren sister all your life,","Chanting faint hymns to the cold fruitless moon.","Thrice-blessèd they that master so their blood","To undergo such maiden pilgrimage,","But earthlier happy is the rose distill'd","Than that which, withering on the virgin thorn,","Grows, lives, and dies, in single blessedness."],"id":17,"m":"Either you will be put to death, or you must give up the company of men for ever. So, beautiful Hermia, think hard about what you want. Remember how young you are, and test your feelings, to decide whether, if you refuse your father's choice, you can bear to wear a nun's robes, to be shut up for ever in a gloomy convent, to live your whole life as a childless sister, singing weak hymns to the cold, barren moon. Those who can control their passions enough to live like that, as pure as pilgrims, are blessed three times over. But on earth a rose that is picked and made into perfume is happier than one that withers on its untouched stem, and grows, lives and dies alone."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIA","lines":["So will I grow, so live, so die, my lord,","Ere I will yield my virgin patent up","Unto his lordship, whose unwishèd yoke","My soul consents not to give sovereignty."],"id":18,"m":"Then I will grow, live and die that way, my lord, before I give up my virginity, and the right to keep it, to a man whose rule over me I never wanted, and whom my soul refuses to accept as master."},{"t":"sp","who":"THESEUS","lines":["Take time to pause; and by the next new moon","The sealing-day betwixt my love and me","For everlasting bond of fellowship,","Upon that day either prepare to die","For disobedience to your father's will,","Or else to wed Demetrius, as he would,","Or on Diana's altar to protest","For aye austerity and single life."],"id":19,"m":"Take time to think. By the next new moon, the day my love and I are joined together for ever, on that day you must either prepare to die for disobeying your father, or marry Demetrius as he wishes, or swear at the altar of Diana, goddess of chastity, to live a strict and single life for ever."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["Relent, sweet Hermia; and, Lysander, yield","Thy crazèd title to my certain right."],"id":20,"m":"Give in, sweet Hermia. And, Lysander, give up your crazy claim to what is rightfully mine."},{"t":"sp","who":"LYSANDER","lines":["You have her father's love, Demetrius.","Let me have Hermia's. Do you marry him."],"id":21,"m":"You have her father's love, Demetrius. Let me have Hermia's. Why don't you marry him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"EGEUS","lines":["Scornful Lysander, true, he hath my love;","And what is mine my love shall render him;","And she is mine, and all my right of her","I do estate unto Demetrius."],"id":22,"m":"Rude Lysander! It's true he has my love, and because I love him I will give him what is mine. She is mine, and I hand over all my rights in her to Demetrius."},{"t":"sp","who":"LYSANDER","lines":["I am, my lord, as well deriv'd as he,","As well possess'd; my love is more than his;","My fortunes every way as fairly rank'd,","If not with vantage, as Demetrius';","And, which is more than all these boasts can be,","I am belov'd of beauteous Hermia.","Why should not I then prosecute my right?","Demetrius, I'll avouch it to his head,","Made love to Nedar's daughter, Helena,","And won her soul; and she, sweet lady, dotes,","Devoutly dotes, dotes in idolatry,","Upon this spotted and inconstant man."],"id":23,"m":"My lord, I come from as good a family as he does, and I am as rich. I love her more than he does. In every way my prospects are as good as his, if not better. And, more important than any of these boasts, beautiful Hermia loves me. So why shouldn't I claim what is mine? I'll say it to his face: Demetrius courted Nedar's daughter, Helena, and won her heart. And she, sweet lady, adores him, worships him like an idol, this stained and unfaithful man."},{"t":"sp","who":"THESEUS","lines":["I must confess that I have heard so much,","And with Demetrius thought to have spoke thereof;","But, being over-full of self-affairs,","My mind did lose it.—But, Demetrius, come,","And come, Egeus; you shall go with me.","I have some private schooling for you both.—","For you, fair Hermia, look you arm yourself","To fit your fancies to your father's will,","Or else the law of Athens yields you up","(Which by no means we may extenuate)","To death, or to a vow of single life.","Come, my Hippolyta. What cheer, my love?","Demetrius and Egeus, go along;","I must employ you in some business","Against our nuptial, and confer with you","Of something nearly that concerns yourselves."],"id":24,"m":"I must admit I have heard as much, and meant to speak to Demetrius about it, but I was so busy with my own affairs that I forgot. But Demetrius, come, and Egeus, come too. You will come with me. I have some private advice for you both. As for you, beautiful Hermia, make sure you bring your wishes into line with your father's will, or else the law of Athens, which I have no power to soften, will condemn you to death or to a life alone. Come, my Hippolyta. Are you all right, my love? Demetrius and Egeus, come along. I need your help with some arrangements for our wedding, and I want to talk to you about something that closely concerns you both."},{"t":"sp","who":"EGEUS","lines":["With duty and desire we follow you."],"id":25,"m":"We follow you out of duty and gladly."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt all but LYSANDER and HERMIA","id":26},{"t":"sp","who":"LYSANDER","lines":["How now, my love? Why is your cheek so pale?","How chance the roses there do fade so fast?"],"id":27,"m":"What is it, my love? Why are your cheeks so pale? How is it that the roses there are fading so fast?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIA","lines":["Belike for want of rain, which I could well","Beteem them from the tempest of my eyes."],"id":28,"m":"Probably because they need rain, which I could easily give them from the storm in my eyes."},{"t":"sp","who":"LYSANDER","lines":["Ay me! For aught that I could ever read,","Could ever hear by tale or history,","The course of true love never did run smooth.","But either it was different in blood—"],"id":29,"m":"Oh dear! From everything I have ever read, or heard in stories or history, the course of true love never ran smoothly. Either the lovers were from different social classes..."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIA","lines":["O cross! Too high to be enthrall'd to low."],"id":30,"m":"What a misery! To be too high-born to be tied to someone low!"},{"t":"sp","who":"LYSANDER","lines":["Or else misgraffèd in respect of years—"],"id":31,"m":"Or they were badly matched in age..."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIA","lines":["O spite! Too old to be engag'd to young."],"id":32,"m":"How cruel! To be too old to be engaged to someone young!"},{"t":"sp","who":"LYSANDER","lines":["Or else it stood upon the choice of friends—"],"id":33,"m":"Or else it depended on the choice of family and friends..."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIA","lines":["O hell! to choose love by another's eyes!"],"id":34,"m":"Oh, hell! To choose a lover through someone else's eyes!"},{"t":"sp","who":"LYSANDER","lines":["Or, if there were a sympathy in choice,","War, death, or sickness did lay siege to it,","Making it momentany as a sound,","Swift as a shadow, short as any dream,","Brief as the lightning in the collied night","That, in a spleen, unfolds both heaven and earth,","And, ere a man hath power to say, 'Behold!'","The jaws of darkness do devour it up:","So quick bright things come to confusion."],"id":35,"m":"Or, if the lovers did suit each other, war, death or illness attacked their love and made it last only a moment, like a sound, as quick as a shadow, as short as a dream, as brief as lightning on a pitch-black night, which in a flash lights up heaven and earth, and before anyone can say 'Look!' the jaws of darkness swallow it up. That's how quickly bright things are destroyed."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIA","lines":["If then true lovers have ever cross'd,","It stands as an edict in destiny.","Then let us teach our trial patience,","Because it is a customary cross,","As due to love as thoughts and dreams and sighs,","Wishes and tears, poor fancy's followers."],"id":36,"m":"If true lovers have always been thwarted, then it must be a law of fate. So let us learn to be patient in our trouble, because it is a normal part of love, as much a part of it as thoughts and dreams and sighs, wishes and tears, the usual companions of love."},{"t":"sp","who":"LYSANDER","lines":["A good persuasion; therefore, hear me, Hermia.","I have a widow aunt, a dowager","Of great revenue, and she hath no child.","From Athens is her house remote seven leagues,","And she respects me as her only son.","There, gentle Hermia, may I marry thee,","And to that place the sharp Athenian law","Cannot pursue us. If thou lovest me then,","Steal forth thy father's house tomorrow night;","And in the wood, a league without the town","(Where I did meet thee once with Helena","To do observance to a morn of May),","There will I stay for thee."],"id":37,"m":"That is the right attitude. So listen to me, Hermia. I have an aunt, a rich widow with no children. Her house is twenty miles from Athens, and she treats me like her own son. There, sweet Hermia, I can marry you, and the harsh Athenian law cannot follow us there. So, if you love me, slip out of your father's house tomorrow night. In the wood three miles outside the town, where I once met you with Helena to celebrate a May morning, I will wait for you."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIA","lines":["My good Lysander!","I swear to thee by Cupid's strongest bow,","By his best arrow with the golden head,","By the simplicity of Venus' doves,","By that which knitteth souls and prospers loves,","And by that fire which burn'd the Carthage queen","When the false Trojan under sail was seen,","By all the vows that ever men have broke","(In number more than ever women spoke),","In that same place thou hast appointed me,","Tomorrow truly will I meet with thee."],"id":38,"m":"My dear Lysander! I swear to you by Cupid's strongest bow, by his best arrow with the golden tip, by the innocence of Venus's doves, by everything that binds souls together and makes love grow, by the fire that burned Dido, the Queen of Carthage, when she saw her lover Aeneas sail away, and by every promise men have ever broken (more promises than women have ever made): tomorrow I will meet you without fail in the place you have chosen."},{"t":"sp","who":"LYSANDER","lines":["Keep promise, love. Look, here comes Helena."],"id":39,"m":"Keep your promise, my love. Look, here comes Helena."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter HELENA","id":40},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIA","lines":["God speed fair Helena! Whither away?"],"id":41,"m":"Hello, beautiful Helena! Where are you going?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["Call you me fair? That fair again unsay.","Demetrius loves your fair. O happy fair!","Your eyes are lode-stars and your tongue's sweet air","More tuneable than lark to shepherd's ear,","When wheat is green, when hawthorn buds appear.","Sickness is catching. O were favour so,","Yours would I catch, fair Hermia, ere I go.","My ear should catch your voice, my eye your eye,","My tongue should catch your tongue's sweet melody.","Were the world mine, Demetrius being bated,","The rest I'd give to be to you translated.","O, teach me how you look, and with what art","You sway the motion of Demetrius' heart!"],"id":42,"m":"Did you call me beautiful? Take that back. Demetrius loves your beauty. Lucky beauty! Your eyes are guiding stars, and your sweet voice is more musical than a lark's song is to a shepherd in spring, when the wheat is green and the hawthorn comes into bud. Illness is catching. If only good looks were catching too! I would catch yours, beautiful Hermia, before I left. My ear would catch your voice, my eye your eye, my tongue your sweet way of speaking. If the world were mine, I'd give it all, except Demetrius, to be changed into you. Oh, teach me how you look at him, and what trick you use to control Demetrius's heart!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIA","lines":["I frown upon him, yet he loves me still."],"id":43,"m":"I frown at him, but he still loves me."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["O that your frowns would teach my smiles such skill!"],"id":44,"m":"If only my smiles could learn to work as well as your frowns!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIA","lines":["I give him curses, yet he gives me love."],"id":45,"m":"I curse him, but he gives me love."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["O that my prayers could such affection move!"],"id":46,"m":"If only my prayers could make him feel that way!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIA","lines":["The more I hate, the more he follows me."],"id":47,"m":"The more I hate him, the more he follows me."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["The more I love, the more he hateth me."],"id":48,"m":"The more I love him, the more he hates me."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIA","lines":["His folly, Helena, is no fault of mine."],"id":49,"m":"His foolishness isn't my fault, Helena."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["None but your beauty; would that fault were mine!"],"id":50,"m":"No, it's your beauty's fault. I wish I had that fault!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIA","lines":["Take comfort: he no more shall see my face;","Lysander and myself will fly this place.","Before the time I did Lysander see,","Seem'd Athens as a paradise to me.","O, then, what graces in my love do dwell,","That he hath turn'd a heaven into hell!"],"id":51,"m":"Cheer up. He won't see my face again. Lysander and I are going to run away from here. Before I met Lysander, Athens seemed like paradise to me. So what powers my love must have, to turn heaven into hell!"},{"t":"sp","who":"LYSANDER","lines":["Helen, to you our minds we will unfold:","Tomorrow night, when Phoebe doth behold","Her silver visage in the watery glass,","Decking with liquid pearl the bladed grass","(A time that lovers' flights doth still conceal),","Through Athens' gates have we devis'd to steal."],"id":52,"m":"Helena, we'll tell you our plans. Tomorrow night, when the moon sees her silver face reflected in the water and decorates the blades of grass with drops of dew like pearls (a time that always hides lovers who run away), we plan to slip out through the gates of Athens."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIA","lines":["And in the wood where often you and I","Upon faint primrose beds were wont to lie,","Emptying our bosoms of their counsel sweet,","There my Lysander and myself shall meet,","And thence from Athens turn away our eyes,","To seek new friends and stranger companies.","Farewell, sweet playfellow. Pray thou for us,","And good luck grant thee thy Demetrius!","Keep word, Lysander. We must starve our sight","From lovers' food, till morrow deep midnight."],"id":53,"m":"And in the wood, where you and I used to lie on beds of pale primroses telling each other our sweet secrets, Lysander and I will meet. From there we will turn our backs on Athens and look for new friends among strangers. Goodbye, sweet friend of my childhood. Pray for us, and good luck in winning your Demetrius! Keep your word, Lysander. We must starve our eyes of the sight of each other, which is lovers' food, until midnight tomorrow."},{"t":"sp","who":"LYSANDER","lines":["I will, my Hermia."],"id":54,"m":"I will, my Hermia."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit HERMIA","id":55},{"t":"sp","who":"LYSANDER","cont":true,"lines":["Helena, adieu.","As you on him, Demetrius dote on you!"],"id":56,"m":"Goodbye, Helena. I hope Demetrius comes to love you as much as you love him!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit LYSANDER","id":57},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["How happy some o'er other some can be!","Through Athens I am thought as fair as she.","But what of that? Demetrius thinks not so;","He will not know what all but he do know.","And as he errs, doting on Hermia's eyes,","So I, admiring of his qualities.","Things base and vile, holding no quantity,","Love can transpose to form and dignity.","Love looks not with the eyes, but with the mind;","And therefore is wing'd Cupid painted blind.","Nor hath love's mind of any judgment taste.","Wings, and no eyes, figure unheedy haste.","And therefore is love said to be a child,","Because in choice he is so oft beguil'd.","As waggish boys in game themselves forswear,","So the boy Love is perjur'd everywhere.","For, ere Demetrius look'd on Hermia's eyne,","He hail'd down oaths that he was only mine;","And when this hail some heat from Hermia felt,","So he dissolv'd, and showers of oaths did melt.","I will go tell him of fair Hermia's flight.","Then to the wood will he tomorrow night","Pursue her; and for this intelligence","If I have thanks, it is a dear expense.","But herein mean I to enrich my pain,","To have his sight thither and back again."],"id":58,"m":"How much happier some people are than others! All over Athens I'm thought to be as beautiful as she is. But so what? Demetrius doesn't think so. He refuses to see what everyone except him can see. And just as he is wrong to be obsessed with Hermia's eyes, so am I wrong to admire everything about him. Love can turn things that are low and worthless into something fine and dignified. Love doesn't see with the eyes but with the mind, and that is why winged Cupid is painted blind. Love's mind has no judgement at all: wings and no eyes stand for reckless hurry. That's why Love is said to be a child: because he is so often fooled in his choices. Just as naughty boys break their promises in games, so the boy Love tells lies everywhere. Before Demetrius saw Hermia's eyes, he showered me with promises that he was mine alone. But when that hail of promises felt some heat from Hermia, he melted, and his promises melted away too. I will go and tell him that Hermia is running away. Then tomorrow night he will follow her to the wood. If he thanks me for this news, it will cost me dearly. But I mean to make my pain worth it by seeing him on the way there and back again."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit HELENA","id":59},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"II","loc":"The Same. A Room in a Cottage.","id":60},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter QUINCE, SNUG, BOTTOM, FLUTE, SNOUT and STARVELING","id":61},{"t":"sp","who":"QUINCE","lines":["Is all our company here?"],"id":62,"m":"Is everyone here?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOTTOM","lines":["You were best to call them generally, man by man, according to the","scrip."],"id":63,"m":"You'd better call them all one by one, man by man, from the list."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUINCE","lines":["Here is the scroll of every man's name, which is thought fit through","all Athens, to play in our interlude before the Duke and Duchess, on","his wedding-day at night."],"id":64,"m":"Here's the list of names of every man in Athens who is thought good enough to act in our play before the Duke and Duchess on the night of his wedding."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOTTOM","lines":["First, good Peter Quince, say what the play treats on; then read the","names of the actors; and so grow to a point."],"id":65,"m":"First, good Peter Quince, tell us what the play is about, then read out the actors' names, and so get to the point."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUINCE","lines":["Marry, our play is The most lamentable comedy and most cruel death of","Pyramus and Thisbe."],"id":66,"m":"Well, our play is The Most Sad Comedy and Most Cruel Death of Pyramus and Thisbe."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOTTOM","lines":["A very good piece of work, I assure you, and a merry. Now, good Peter","Quince, call forth your actors by the scroll. Masters, spread","yourselves."],"id":67,"m":"A very good piece of work, I promise you, and a cheerful one. Now, good Peter Quince, call out your actors from the list. Gentlemen, spread out."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUINCE","lines":["Answer, as I call you. Nick Bottom, the weaver."],"id":68,"m":"Answer when I call your name. Nick Bottom, the weaver."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOTTOM","lines":["Ready. Name what part I am for, and proceed."],"id":69,"m":"Here. Tell me which part I'm playing, and carry on."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUINCE","lines":["You, Nick Bottom, are set down for Pyramus."],"id":70,"m":"You, Nick Bottom, are down to play Pyramus."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOTTOM","lines":["What is Pyramus—a lover, or a tyrant?"],"id":71,"m":"What is Pyramus? A lover or a tyrant?"},{"t":"sp","who":"QUINCE","lines":["A lover, that kills himself most gallantly for love."],"id":72,"m":"A lover, who kills himself very bravely for love."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOTTOM","lines":["That will ask some tears in the true performing of it. If I do it, let","the audience look to their eyes. I will move storms; I will condole in","some measure. To the rest—yet my chief humour is for a tyrant. I could","play Ercles rarely, or a part to tear a cat in, to make all split.","~The raging rocks","~And shivering shocks","~Shall break the locks","~Of prison gates,","~And Phibbus' car","~Shall shine from far,","~And make and mar","~The foolish Fates.","This was lofty. Now name the rest of the players. This is Ercles' vein,","a tyrant's vein; a lover is more condoling."],"id":73,"m":"That will need some tears to do it properly. If I play it, the audience had better watch out for their eyes. I'll whip up storms of weeping; I'll lament in style. Now the rest. But what I'm really best at is a tyrant. I could play Hercules wonderfully, or a part where I rant and rave and bring the house down. 'The raging rocks and shivering shocks shall break the locks of prison gates, and Phoebus's chariot shall shine from far, and make and mar the foolish Fates.' That was magnificent! Now name the rest of the actors. That's the Hercules style, the tyrant style. A lover is more full of sorrow."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUINCE","lines":["Francis Flute, the bellows-mender."],"id":74,"m":"Francis Flute, the bellows-mender."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUTE","lines":["Here, Peter Quince."],"id":75,"m":"Here, Peter Quince."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUINCE","lines":["Flute, you must take Thisbe on you."],"id":76,"m":"Flute, you must take on Thisbe."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUTE","lines":["What is Thisbe? A wandering knight?"],"id":77,"m":"Who is Thisbe? A knight on a quest?"},{"t":"sp","who":"QUINCE","lines":["It is the lady that Pyramus must love."],"id":78,"m":"She's the lady Pyramus has to love."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUTE","lines":["Nay, faith, let not me play a woman. I have a beard coming."],"id":79,"m":"No, honestly, don't make me play a woman. My beard is starting to grow."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUINCE","lines":["That's all one. You shall play it in a mask, and you may speak as small","as you will."],"id":80,"m":"That doesn't matter. You'll wear a mask, and you can speak in as high a voice as you like."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOTTOM","lines":["And I may hide my face, let me play Thisbe too. I'll speak in a","monstrous little voice; 'Thisne, Thisne!'—'Ah, Pyramus, my lover dear!","thy Thisbe dear! and lady dear!'"],"id":81,"m":"If I can hide my face, let me play Thisbe too. I'll speak in an amazingly tiny voice: 'Thisne, Thisne!' 'Ah, Pyramus, my dear lover! Your dear Thisbe, your dear lady!'"},{"t":"sp","who":"QUINCE","lines":["No, no, you must play Pyramus; and, Flute, you Thisbe."],"id":82,"m":"No, no, you must play Pyramus; and Flute, you play Thisbe."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOTTOM","lines":["Well, proceed."],"id":83,"m":"All right, carry on."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUINCE","lines":["Robin Starveling, the tailor."],"id":84,"m":"Robin Starveling, the tailor."},{"t":"sp","who":"STARVELING","lines":["Here, Peter Quince."],"id":85,"m":"Here, Peter Quince."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUINCE","lines":["Robin Starveling, you must play Thisbe's mother.","Tom Snout, the tinker."],"id":86,"m":"Robin Starveling, you must play Thisbe's mother. Tom Snout, the tinker."},{"t":"sp","who":"SNOUT","lines":["Here, Peter Quince."],"id":87,"m":"Here, Peter Quince."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUINCE","lines":["You, Pyramus' father; myself, Thisbe's father;","Snug, the joiner, you, the lion's part. And, I hope here is a play","fitted."],"id":88,"m":"You'll play Pyramus's father. I'll be Thisbe's father. Snug, the joiner, you have the lion's part. And there, I hope, we have a well-cast play."},{"t":"sp","who":"SNUG","lines":["Have you the lion's part written? Pray you, if it be, give it me, for I","am slow of study."],"id":89,"m":"Have you got the lion's part written out? If you have, please give it to me, because I'm slow at learning lines."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUINCE","lines":["You may do it extempore, for it is nothing but roaring."],"id":90,"m":"You can make it up as you go along, because it's nothing but roaring."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOTTOM","lines":["Let me play the lion too. I will roar that I will do any man's heart","good to hear me. I will roar that I will make the Duke say 'Let him","roar again, let him roar again.'"],"id":91,"m":"Let me play the lion too. I'll roar so well that it will do everyone's heart good to hear me. I'll roar so well that the Duke will say, 'Let him roar again, let him roar again!'"},{"t":"sp","who":"QUINCE","lines":["If you should do it too terribly, you would fright the Duchess and the","ladies, that they would shriek; and that were enough to hang us all."],"id":92,"m":"If you did it too fiercely, you'd frighten the Duchess and the ladies so much that they'd scream, and that would be enough to get us all hanged."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALL","lines":["That would hang us every mother's son."],"id":93,"m":"That would get every one of us hanged."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOTTOM","lines":["I grant you, friends, if you should fright the ladies out of their","wits, they would have no more discretion but to hang us. But I will","aggravate my voice so, that I will roar you as gently as any sucking","dove; I will roar you an 'twere any nightingale."],"id":94,"m":"I agree, friends, that if you frightened the ladies out of their minds, they'd have no choice but to hang us. But I'll 'aggravate' my voice so that I roar as gently as a baby dove. I'll roar for you as sweetly as a nightingale."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUINCE","lines":["You can play no part but Pyramus, for Pyramus is a sweet-faced man; a","proper man as one shall see in a summer's day; a most lovely","gentleman-like man. Therefore you must needs play Pyramus."],"id":95,"m":"You can't play any part except Pyramus, because Pyramus is a sweet-faced man, as handsome a man as you'll see on a summer's day, a most lovely, gentlemanly man. So you simply have to play Pyramus."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOTTOM","lines":["Well, I will undertake it. What beard were I best to play it in?"],"id":96,"m":"Well, I'll take it on. What beard would be best to play it in?"},{"t":"sp","who":"QUINCE","lines":["Why, what you will."],"id":97,"m":"Whatever you like."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOTTOM","lines":["I will discharge it in either your straw-colour beard, your","orange-tawny beard, your purple-in-grain beard, or your","French-crown-colour beard, your perfect yellow."],"id":98,"m":"I'll perform it in either the straw-coloured beard, the orange-brown beard, the deep purple beard, or the beard the colour of a French gold coin, a perfect yellow."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUINCE","lines":["Some of your French crowns have no hair at all, and then you will play","bare-faced. But, masters, here are your parts, and I am to entreat you,","request you, and desire you, to con them by tomorrow night; and meet me","in the palace wood, a mile without the town, by moonlight; there will","we rehearse, for if we meet in the city, we shall be dogg'd with","company, and our devices known. In the meantime I will draw a bill of","properties, such as our play wants. I pray you fail me not."],"id":99,"m":"Some French heads have no hair at all, so then you'd be playing bare-faced. But, gentlemen, here are your parts, and I beg you, ask you and want you to learn them by tomorrow night. Meet me in the palace wood, a mile outside the town, by moonlight. We'll rehearse there, because if we meet in the city we'll be followed by crowds and our plans will be found out. Meanwhile I'll make a list of the props our play needs. Please don't let me down."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOTTOM","lines":["We will meet, and there we may rehearse most obscenely and","courageously. Take pains, be perfect; adieu."],"id":100,"m":"We'll meet, and there we can rehearse 'obscenely' and bravely. Work hard, know your lines perfectly. Goodbye."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUINCE","lines":["At the Duke's oak we meet."],"id":101,"m":"We'll meet at the Duke's oak."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOTTOM","lines":["Enough. Hold, or cut bow-strings."],"id":102,"m":"That's settled. Be there, or else."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt","id":103},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"I","loc":"A wood near Athens.","id":104},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Fairy at one door, and PUCK at another","id":105},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCK","lines":["How now, spirit! Whither wander you?"],"id":106,"m":"Hello there, spirit! Where are you off to?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FAIRY","lines":["~Over hill, over dale,","~Thorough bush, thorough brier,","~Over park, over pale,","~Thorough flood, thorough fire,","~I do wander everywhere,","~Swifter than the moon's sphere;","~And I serve the Fairy Queen,","~To dew her orbs upon the green.","~The cowslips tall her pensioners be,","~In their gold coats spots you see;","~Those be rubies, fairy favours,","~In those freckles live their savours.","I must go seek some dew-drops here,","And hang a pearl in every cowslip's ear.","Farewell, thou lob of spirits; I'll be gone.","Our Queen and all her elves come here anon."],"id":107,"m":"Over hills, over valleys, through bushes, through briars, over parks, over fences, through floods, through fire, I wander everywhere, faster than the moon moves in its orbit. I serve the Fairy Queen and sprinkle dew on her fairy rings in the grass. The tall cowslips are her bodyguards. You can see spots on their golden coats: those are rubies, gifts from the fairies, and their sweet scent lives in those freckles. I have to go and find some dewdrops here and hang a pearl in every cowslip's ear. Goodbye, you clumsy lump of a spirit. I'll be off. Our Queen and all her elves will be here any minute."},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCK","lines":["The King doth keep his revels here tonight;","Take heed the Queen come not within his sight,","For Oberon is passing fell and wrath,","Because that she, as her attendant, hath","A lovely boy, stol'n from an Indian king;","She never had so sweet a changeling.","And jealous Oberon would have the child","Knight of his train, to trace the forests wild:","But she perforce withholds the lovèd boy,","Crowns him with flowers, and makes him all her joy.","And now they never meet in grove or green,","By fountain clear, or spangled starlight sheen,","But they do square; that all their elves for fear","Creep into acorn cups, and hide them there."],"id":108,"m":"The King is holding his party here tonight. Make sure the Queen doesn't come anywhere near him, because Oberon is extremely angry. She has a lovely boy as her servant, stolen from an Indian king; she never had such a sweet stolen child. Jealous Oberon wants the boy to be a knight in his own following, to roam the wild forests. But she refuses to give up the boy she loves, crowns him with flowers and makes him her whole delight. Now whenever they meet, in a wood or a meadow, by a clear spring or under the shining stars, they quarrel, so that all their elves crawl into acorn cups in fear and hide there."},{"t":"sp","who":"FAIRY","lines":["Either I mistake your shape and making quite,","Or else you are that shrewd and knavish sprite","Call'd Robin Goodfellow. Are not you he","That frights the maidens of the villagery,","Skim milk, and sometimes labour in the quern,","And bootless make the breathless housewife churn,","And sometime make the drink to bear no barm,","Mislead night-wanderers, laughing at their harm?","Those that Hobgoblin call you, and sweet Puck,","You do their work, and they shall have good luck.","Are not you he?"],"id":109,"m":"Either I've got your looks completely wrong, or you are that cunning, mischievous spirit called Robin Goodfellow. Aren't you the one who scares the village girls, skims the cream off the milk, sometimes works the hand-mill so the flour comes out wrong, and makes the housewife churn till she's out of breath with no butter to show for it? Who sometimes stops the beer from frothing, and leads night travellers the wrong way, laughing at their trouble? Those who call you Hobgoblin and sweet Puck, you do their work for them, and they have good luck. Aren't you him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCK","lines":["Thou speak'st aright;","I am that merry wanderer of the night.","I jest to Oberon, and make him smile,","When I a fat and bean-fed horse beguile,","Neighing in likeness of a filly foal;","And sometime lurk I in a gossip's bowl","In very likeness of a roasted crab,","And, when she drinks, against her lips I bob,","And on her withered dewlap pour the ale.","The wisest aunt, telling the saddest tale,","Sometime for three-foot stool mistaketh me;","Then slip I from her bum, down topples she,","And 'tailor' cries, and falls into a cough;","And then the whole quire hold their hips and loffe","And waxen in their mirth, and neeze, and swear","A merrier hour was never wasted there.","But room, fairy. Here comes Oberon."],"id":110,"m":"You've got it right. I am that cheerful night wanderer. I tell jokes to Oberon and make him smile, when I trick a fat, well-fed stallion by neighing like a young female horse. Sometimes I hide in an old woman's drink, looking exactly like a roasted crab apple, and when she drinks I bob against her lips and make her spill the ale down her wrinkled neck. The wisest old aunt, telling the saddest story, sometimes mistakes me for a three-legged stool. Then I slip out from under her bum, down she tumbles, shouts 'Ouch, my backside!' and starts coughing. Then the whole group hold their sides and laugh, and get merrier, and sneeze, and swear they never had a better time. But make way, fairy. Here comes Oberon."},{"t":"sp","who":"FAIRY","lines":["And here my mistress. Would that he were gone!"],"id":111,"m":"And here comes my mistress. I wish he would go away!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter OBERON at one door, with his Train, and TITANIA at another, with hers","id":112},{"t":"sp","who":"OBERON","lines":["Ill met by moonlight, proud Titania."],"id":113,"m":"An unlucky meeting by moonlight, proud Titania."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITANIA","lines":["What, jealous Oberon! Fairies, skip hence;","I have forsworn his bed and company."],"id":114,"m":"What, jealous Oberon! Fairies, let's go. I have sworn to stay out of his bed and his company."},{"t":"sp","who":"OBERON","lines":["Tarry, rash wanton; am not I thy lord?"],"id":115,"m":"Wait, you headstrong, unfaithful woman! Aren't I your husband?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TITANIA","lines":["Then I must be thy lady; but I know","When thou hast stol'n away from fairyland,","And in the shape of Corin sat all day","Playing on pipes of corn, and versing love","To amorous Phillida. Why art thou here,","Come from the farthest steep of India,","But that, forsooth, the bouncing Amazon,","Your buskin'd mistress and your warrior love,","To Theseus must be wedded; and you come","To give their bed joy and prosperity?"],"id":116,"m":"Then I must be your wife. But I know about the times you've sneaked off from fairyland, and sat all day disguised as the shepherd Corin, playing on straw pipes and reciting love poems to the lovesick shepherdess Phillida. Why have you come here, all the way from the furthest mountains of India? It's only because, of course, that strapping Amazon, your mistress in her hunting boots, your warrior love, is going to marry Theseus, and you've come to bless their marriage bed with joy and good fortune."},{"t":"sp","who":"OBERON","lines":["How canst thou thus, for shame, Titania,","Glance at my credit with Hippolyta,","Knowing I know thy love to Theseus?","Didst not thou lead him through the glimmering night","From Perigenia, whom he ravished?","And make him with fair Aegles break his faith,","With Ariadne and Antiopa?"],"id":117,"m":"How can you, shameless Titania, hint at my friendship with Hippolyta, when you know I know about your love for Theseus? Didn't you lead him through the starlit night away from Perigenia, whom he had raped? And make him break his promises to beautiful Aegles, to Ariadne and to Antiopa?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TITANIA","lines":["These are the forgeries of jealousy:","And never, since the middle summer's spring,","Met we on hill, in dale, forest, or mead,","By pavèd fountain, or by rushy brook,","Or on the beachèd margent of the sea,","To dance our ringlets to the whistling wind,","But with thy brawls thou hast disturb'd our sport.","Therefore the winds, piping to us in vain,","As in revenge, have suck'd up from the sea","Contagious fogs; which, falling in the land,","Hath every pelting river made so proud","That they have overborne their continents.","The ox hath therefore stretch'd his yoke in vain,","The ploughman lost his sweat, and the green corn","Hath rotted ere his youth attain'd a beard.","The fold stands empty in the drownèd field,","And crows are fatted with the murrion flock;","The nine-men's-morris is fill'd up with mud,","And the quaint mazes in the wanton green,","For lack of tread, are undistinguishable.","The human mortals want their winter here.","No night is now with hymn or carol blest.","Therefore the moon, the governess of floods,","Pale in her anger, washes all the air,","That rheumatic diseases do abound.","And thorough this distemperature we see","The seasons alter: hoary-headed frosts","Fall in the fresh lap of the crimson rose;","And on old Hiems' thin and icy crown","An odorous chaplet of sweet summer buds","Is, as in mockery, set. The spring, the summer,","The childing autumn, angry winter, change","Their wonted liveries; and the mazed world,","By their increase, now knows not which is which.","And this same progeny of evils comes","From our debate, from our dissension;","We are their parents and original."],"id":118,"m":"These are lies made up by jealousy. Ever since the start of midsummer, we have never met on a hill, in a valley, forest or meadow, by a stone-lined spring or a reedy stream, or on the sandy edge of the sea, to dance our circles to the whistling wind, without you spoiling our fun with your quarrelling. So the winds, piping to us for nothing, have taken revenge by sucking up poisonous fogs from the sea. These have fallen on the land and made every little river so swollen that they have burst their banks. So the ox has pulled his plough for nothing, the ploughman has wasted his sweat, and the green corn has rotted before it could ripen. The sheep pens stand empty in the flooded fields, and crows grow fat on the dead, diseased flocks. The outdoor game boards are filled with mud, and the intricate paths in the lush grass can't be seen, because no one walks on them. The humans are missing their winter festivities. No night is blessed with hymns or carols now. So the moon, which rules the tides, pale with anger, soaks the air with damp, and colds and fevers are everywhere. Because of this disorder, the seasons are changing. White frosts fall on the fresh red rose, and on old Winter's thin, icy head a sweet-smelling crown of summer buds is placed, as if to mock him. Spring, summer, fruitful autumn and angry winter swap their usual clothes, and the confused world can't tell which is which from what they produce. And this whole family of disasters comes from our argument, our quarrel. We are their parents and their origin."},{"t":"sp","who":"OBERON","lines":["Do you amend it, then. It lies in you.","Why should Titania cross her Oberon?","I do but beg a little changeling boy","To be my henchman."],"id":119,"m":"Then you put it right. It's in your power. Why should Titania go against her Oberon? All I'm asking for is a little stolen boy to be my page."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITANIA","lines":["Set your heart at rest;","The fairyland buys not the child of me.","His mother was a vot'ress of my order,","And in the spicèd Indian air, by night,","Full often hath she gossip'd by my side;","And sat with me on Neptune's yellow sands,","Marking th' embarkèd traders on the flood,","When we have laugh'd to see the sails conceive,","And grow big-bellied with the wanton wind;","Which she, with pretty and with swimming gait","Following (her womb then rich with my young squire),","Would imitate, and sail upon the land,","To fetch me trifles, and return again,","As from a voyage, rich with merchandise.","But she, being mortal, of that boy did die;","And for her sake do I rear up her boy,","And for her sake I will not part with him."],"id":120,"m":"Forget it. You couldn't buy the child from me with all of fairyland. His mother was a follower of mine, and on many nights in the spicy Indian air she sat chatting beside me, and sat with me on the yellow sands by the sea, watching the merchant ships on the waves. We laughed to see the sails swell up as if they were pregnant, puffed out by the playful wind. She would copy them, walking with a pretty, swaying step (her womb was carrying my young page then), and sail across the land to fetch me little presents, and come back as if from a voyage, loaded with goods. But she was mortal, and she died giving birth to that boy. For her sake I am bringing up her son, and for her sake I won't give him up."},{"t":"sp","who":"OBERON","lines":["How long within this wood intend you stay?"],"id":121,"m":"How long do you plan to stay in this wood?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TITANIA","lines":["Perchance till after Theseus' wedding-day.","If you will patiently dance in our round,","And see our moonlight revels, go with us;","If not, shun me, and I will spare your haunts."],"id":122,"m":"Maybe until after Theseus's wedding day. If you will dance calmly in our circle and watch our moonlit celebrations, come with us. If not, keep away from me, and I'll stay away from the places you go."},{"t":"sp","who":"OBERON","lines":["Give me that boy and I will go with thee."],"id":123,"m":"Give me that boy and I'll come with you."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITANIA","lines":["Not for thy fairy kingdom. Fairies, away.","We shall chide downright if I longer stay."],"id":124,"m":"Not for your whole fairy kingdom. Fairies, let's go. We'll have a real fight if I stay any longer."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit TITANIA with her Train","id":125},{"t":"sp","who":"OBERON","lines":["Well, go thy way. Thou shalt not from this grove","Till I torment thee for this injury.—","My gentle Puck, come hither. Thou rememb'rest","Since once I sat upon a promontory,","And heard a mermaid on a dolphin's back","Uttering such dulcet and harmonious breath","That the rude sea grew civil at her song","And certain stars shot madly from their spheres","To hear the sea-maid's music."],"id":126,"m":"All right, go your way. You won't leave this wood until I've punished you for this insult. My gentle Puck, come here. Do you remember when I sat on a headland and heard a mermaid on a dolphin's back singing so sweetly and harmoniously that the rough sea grew calm at her song, and some stars shot wildly out of their orbits to hear the sea-girl's music?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCK","lines":["I remember."],"id":127,"m":"I remember."},{"t":"sp","who":"OBERON","lines":["That very time I saw, (but thou couldst not),","Flying between the cold moon and the earth,","Cupid all arm'd: a certain aim he took","At a fair vestal, thronèd by the west,","And loos'd his love-shaft smartly from his bow","As it should pierce a hundred thousand hearts.","But I might see young Cupid's fiery shaft","Quench'd in the chaste beams of the watery moon;","And the imperial votress passed on,","In maiden meditation, fancy-free.","Yet mark'd I where the bolt of Cupid fell:","It fell upon a little western flower,","Before milk-white, now purple with love's wound,","And maidens call it love-in-idleness.","Fetch me that flower, the herb I showed thee once:","The juice of it on sleeping eyelids laid","Will make or man or woman madly dote","Upon the next live creature that it sees.","Fetch me this herb, and be thou here again","Ere the leviathan can swim a league."],"id":128,"m":"At that very moment I saw (though you couldn't) Cupid, fully armed, flying between the cold moon and the earth. He took careful aim at a beautiful virgin queen, enthroned in the west, and shot his love-arrow sharply from his bow, as if it could pierce a hundred thousand hearts. But I saw young Cupid's fiery arrow put out by the pure rays of the watery moon, and the royal virgin walked on, lost in innocent thoughts, free of love. But I noticed where Cupid's arrow fell. It fell on a little western flower, which used to be milk-white but is now purple from love's wound. Girls call it love-in-idleness. Fetch me that flower, the herb I once showed you. Its juice, put on sleeping eyelids, will make a man or woman fall madly in love with the next living creature they see. Fetch me this herb, and be back before a whale can swim three miles."},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCK","lines":["I'll put a girdle round about the earth","In forty minutes."],"id":129,"m":"I'll fly a belt around the whole earth in forty minutes."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit PUCK","id":130},{"t":"sp","who":"OBERON","lines":["Having once this juice,","I'll watch Titania when she is asleep,","And drop the liquor of it in her eyes:","The next thing then she waking looks upon","(Be it on lion, bear, or wolf, or bull,","On meddling monkey, or on busy ape)","She shall pursue it with the soul of love.","And ere I take this charm from off her sight","(As I can take it with another herb)","I'll make her render up her page to me.","But who comes here? I am invisible;","And I will overhear their conference."],"id":131,"m":"Once I have this juice, I'll watch Titania until she is asleep and drop the liquid in her eyes. The next thing she sees when she wakes up, whether it's a lion, a bear, a wolf or a bull, an interfering monkey or a busy ape, she will chase it with all her soul in love. And before I take this spell off her eyes (which I can do with another herb), I'll make her hand over her page to me. But who's coming? I am invisible, and I'll listen to their conversation."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter DEMETRIUS, HELENA following him","id":132},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["I love thee not, therefore pursue me not.","Where is Lysander and fair Hermia?","The one I'll slay, the other slayeth me.","Thou told'st me they were stol'n into this wood,","And here am I, and wode within this wood","Because I cannot meet with Hermia.","Hence, get thee gone, and follow me no more."],"id":133,"m":"I don't love you, so stop following me. Where are Lysander and beautiful Hermia? I'll kill the one, and the other is killing me. You told me they had slipped away into this wood, and here I am, going wild in this wood because I can't find Hermia. Go away, get out, and stop following me."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["You draw me, you hard-hearted adamant,","But yet you draw not iron, for my heart","Is true as steel. Leave you your power to draw,","And I shall have no power to follow you."],"id":134,"m":"You pull me to you, you hard-hearted magnet. But it isn't iron you attract, because my heart is as true as steel. Give up your power to attract me, and I'll have no power to follow you."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["Do I entice you? Do I speak you fair?","Or rather do I not in plainest truth","Tell you I do not, nor I cannot love you?"],"id":135,"m":"Do I lead you on? Do I speak kindly to you? Don't I tell you, as plainly as I can, that I don't love you and can't love you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["And even for that do I love you the more.","I am your spaniel; and, Demetrius,","The more you beat me, I will fawn on you.","Use me but as your spaniel, spurn me, strike me,","Neglect me, lose me; only give me leave,","Unworthy as I am, to follow you.","What worser place can I beg in your love,","(And yet a place of high respect with me)","Than to be usèd as you use your dog?"],"id":136,"m":"And that's exactly why I love you more. I am your spaniel, and, Demetrius, the more you beat me, the more I'll fawn over you. Just treat me as your spaniel: kick me, hit me, ignore me, lose me. Only let me follow you, unworthy as I am. What lower place could I ask for in your love (and yet to me it's a place of great honour) than to be treated the way you treat your dog?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["Tempt not too much the hatred of my spirit;","For I am sick when I do look on thee."],"id":137,"m":"Don't push my hatred too far. It makes me sick to look at you."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["And I am sick when I look not on you."],"id":138,"m":"And I am sick when I can't look at you."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["You do impeach your modesty too much","To leave the city and commit yourself","Into the hands of one that loves you not,","To trust the opportunity of night","And the ill counsel of a desert place,","With the rich worth of your virginity."],"id":139,"m":"You are putting your reputation at great risk by leaving the city and handing yourself over to a man who doesn't love you, trusting your precious virginity to the chances of the night and the temptations of a lonely place."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["Your virtue is my privilege: for that","It is not night when I do see your face,","Therefore I think I am not in the night;","Nor doth this wood lack worlds of company,","For you, in my respect, are all the world.","Then how can it be said I am alone","When all the world is here to look on me?"],"id":140,"m":"Your good character is my protection. And because it is never night when I see your face, I don't feel I'm in the dark. Nor is this wood short of company, because to me you are the whole world. So how can anyone say I'm alone, when the whole world is here looking at me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["I'll run from thee and hide me in the brakes,","And leave thee to the mercy of wild beasts."],"id":141,"m":"I'll run away from you and hide in the bushes, and leave you to the mercy of wild animals."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["The wildest hath not such a heart as you.","Run when you will, the story shall be chang'd;","Apollo flies, and Daphne holds the chase;","The dove pursues the griffin, the mild hind","Makes speed to catch the tiger. Bootless speed,","When cowardice pursues and valour flies!"],"id":142,"m":"The wildest animal doesn't have a heart as cruel as yours. Run whenever you like; the old story will be turned round. Apollo will run away and Daphne will chase him. The dove will chase the griffin, the gentle deer will hurry to catch the tiger. But speeding is useless when the coward chases and the brave one runs away!"},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["I will not stay thy questions. Let me go,","Or if thou follow me, do not believe","But I shall do thee mischief in the wood."],"id":143,"m":"I won't stay to listen to you. Let me go. Or if you follow me, you can be sure I'll do you some harm in the wood."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["Ay, in the temple, in the town, the field,","You do me mischief. Fie, Demetrius!","Your wrongs do set a scandal on my sex.","We cannot fight for love as men may do.","We should be woo'd, and were not made to woo."],"id":144,"m":"Yes, in the temple, in the town, in the fields, you do me harm. Shame on you, Demetrius! The wrongs you do me make my whole sex look disgraceful. We women can't fight for love the way men can. We are supposed to be courted, not to do the courting."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit DEMETRIUS","id":145},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","cont":true,"lines":["I'll follow thee, and make a heaven of hell,","To die upon the hand I love so well."],"id":146,"m":"I'll follow you and turn this hell into heaven, if I can die by the hand of the man I love so much."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit HELENA","id":147},{"t":"sp","who":"OBERON","lines":["Fare thee well, nymph. Ere he do leave this grove,","Thou shalt fly him, and he shall seek thy love."],"id":148,"m":"Goodbye, nymph. Before he leaves this wood, you will be the one running away, and he will be chasing your love."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter PUCK","id":149},{"t":"sp","who":"OBERON","cont":true,"lines":["Hast thou the flower there? Welcome, wanderer."],"id":150,"m":"Have you got the flower? Welcome, wanderer."},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCK","lines":["Ay, there it is."],"id":151,"m":"Yes, here it is."},{"t":"sp","who":"OBERON","lines":["I pray thee give it me.","I know a bank where the wild thyme blows,","Where oxlips and the nodding violet grows,","Quite over-canopied with luscious woodbine,","With sweet musk-roses, and with eglantine.","There sleeps Titania sometime of the night,","Lull'd in these flowers with dances and delight;","And there the snake throws her enamell'd skin,","Weed wide enough to wrap a fairy in.","And with the juice of this I'll streak her eyes,","And make her full of hateful fantasies.","Take thou some of it, and seek through this grove:","A sweet Athenian lady is in love","With a disdainful youth. Anoint his eyes;","But do it when the next thing he espies","May be the lady. Thou shalt know the man","By the Athenian garments he hath on.","Effect it with some care, that he may prove","More fond on her than she upon her love:","And look thou meet me ere the first cock crow."],"id":152,"m":"Please give it to me. I know a bank where wild thyme blooms, where oxlips and nodding violets grow, all roofed over with rich honeysuckle, sweet musk roses and wild roses. Titania sleeps there for part of the night, lulled to sleep among these flowers by dancing and pleasure. There the snake sheds its shiny skin, a garment wide enough to wrap a fairy in. I'll smear her eyes with the juice of this flower and fill her head with horrible fantasies. Take some of it and search through this wood. A sweet Athenian lady is in love with a young man who scorns her. Put some on his eyes, but do it when the next thing he sees will be the lady. You'll recognise the man by his Athenian clothes. Do it carefully, so that he ends up more in love with her than she is with him. And make sure you meet me before the first cock crows."},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCK","lines":["Fear not, my lord, your servant shall do so."],"id":153,"m":"Don't worry, my lord. Your servant will do it."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt","id":154},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"II","loc":"Another part of the wood.","id":155},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter TITANIA with her Train","id":156},{"t":"sp","who":"TITANIA","lines":["Come, now a roundel and a fairy song;","Then for the third part of a minute, hence;","Some to kill cankers in the musk-rose buds;","Some war with reremice for their leathern wings,","To make my small elves coats; and some keep back","The clamorous owl, that nightly hoots and wonders","At our quaint spirits. Sing me now asleep;","Then to your offices, and let me rest.","[Fairies sing]"],"id":157,"m":"Come on, now, a dance in a ring and a fairy song. Then, for a third of a minute, off you go. Some of you, kill the grubs in the musk-rose buds. Some of you, fight the bats for their leathery wings, to make coats for my little elves. And some of you, keep away the noisy owl that hoots all night, amazed at our dainty spirits. Now sing me to sleep. Then off to your jobs, and let me rest. [The fairies sing]"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST FAIRY","lines":["~You spotted snakes with double tongue,","~Thorny hedgehogs, be not seen;","~Newts and blind-worms do no wrong,","~Come not near our Fairy Queen:"],"id":158,"m":"You spotted snakes with forked tongues, prickly hedgehogs, keep out of sight. Newts and slow-worms, do no harm. Don't come near our Fairy Queen."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHORUS","lines":["~Philomel, with melody,","~Sing in our sweet lullaby:","Lulla, lulla, lullaby; lulla, lulla, lullaby.","~Never harm, nor spell, nor charm,","~Come our lovely lady nigh;","~So good night, with lullaby."],"id":159,"m":"Nightingale, sing with your melody in our sweet lullaby: lulla, lulla, lullaby; lulla, lulla, lullaby. Let no harm, no spell and no charm come near our lovely lady. So good night, with a lullaby."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST FAIRY","lines":["~Weaving spiders, come not here;","~Hence, you long-legg'd spinners, hence.","~Beetles black, approach not near;","~Worm nor snail do no offence."],"id":160,"m":"Weaving spiders, don't come here. Away with you, long-legged spinners, away! Black beetles, don't come near. Worms and snails, do no harm."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHORUS","lines":["~Philomel with melody, &c."],"id":161,"m":"Nightingale, with melody, and so on."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND FAIRY","lines":["Hence away! Now all is well.","One aloof stand sentinel."],"id":162,"m":"Away now! All is well. One of you stay at a distance and keep guard."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Fairies. TITANIA sleeps","id":163},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter OBERON","id":164},{"t":"sp","who":"OBERON","lines":["What thou seest when thou dost wake,"],"id":165,"m":"Whatever you see when you wake,"},{"t":"sd","text":"Squeezes the flower on TITANIA's eyelids","id":166},{"t":"sp","who":"OBERON","cont":true,"lines":["Do it for thy true love take;","Love and languish for his sake.","Be it ounce, or cat, or bear,","Pard, or boar with bristled hair,","In thy eye that shall appear","When thou wak'st, it is thy dear.","Wake when some vile thing is near."],"id":167,"m":"take it to be your true love. Love it and long for it. Whether it's a lynx, or a cat, or a bear, a leopard or a boar with bristly hair, whatever appears before your eyes when you wake will be your darling. Wake up when some horrible thing is near."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit","id":168},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter LYSANDER and HERMIA","id":169},{"t":"sp","who":"LYSANDER","lines":["Fair love, you faint with wand'ring in the wood.","And, to speak troth, I have forgot our way.","We'll rest us, Hermia, if you think it good,","And tarry for the comfort of the day."],"id":170,"m":"My lovely one, you're exhausted from wandering in the wood. And to tell you the truth, I've lost our way. We'll rest, Hermia, if you think it's a good idea, and wait for daylight to help us."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIA","lines":["Be it so, Lysander: find you out a bed,","For I upon this bank will rest my head."],"id":171,"m":"All right, Lysander. Find yourself a bed, because I'm going to rest my head on this bank."},{"t":"sp","who":"LYSANDER","lines":["One turf shall serve as pillow for us both;","One heart, one bed, two bosoms, and one troth."],"id":172,"m":"One piece of turf will do as a pillow for us both: one heart, one bed, two bodies and one promise."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIA","lines":["Nay, good Lysander; for my sake, my dear,","Lie further off yet, do not lie so near."],"id":173,"m":"No, good Lysander. For my sake, my dear, lie further away. Don't lie so close."},{"t":"sp","who":"LYSANDER","lines":["O take the sense, sweet, of my innocence!","Love takes the meaning in love's conference.","I mean that my heart unto yours is knit,","So that but one heart we can make of it:","Two bosoms interchainèd with an oath,","So then two bosoms and a single troth.","Then by your side no bed-room me deny;","For lying so, Hermia, I do not lie."],"id":174,"m":"Oh, sweetheart, understand that I mean nothing wrong! Lovers should understand each other's words in a loving way. I mean that my heart is so joined to yours that we can make one heart out of them. Our two hearts are chained together by a promise, so that makes two bodies and one faithful promise. So don't refuse me a place to lie beside you, because if I lie like this, Hermia, I am not lying."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIA","lines":["Lysander riddles very prettily.","Now much beshrew my manners and my pride,","If Hermia meant to say Lysander lied!","But, gentle friend, for love and courtesy","Lie further off, in human modesty,","Such separation as may well be said","Becomes a virtuous bachelor and a maid,","So far be distant; and good night, sweet friend:","Thy love ne'er alter till thy sweet life end!"],"id":175,"m":"Lysander plays with words very nicely. Curse my manners and my pride if I meant to say that Lysander was lying! But, gentle friend, out of love and politeness, lie further away, as decency requires. A distance that is right for a respectable unmarried man and a young girl: keep that far away. And good night, sweet friend. May your love never change until your sweet life ends!"},{"t":"sp","who":"LYSANDER","lines":["Amen, amen, to that fair prayer say I;","And then end life when I end loyalty!","Here is my bed. Sleep give thee all his rest!"],"id":176,"m":"Amen, amen to that lovely prayer, I say. And may my life end when my loyalty ends! Here is my bed. May sleep give you all its rest!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIA","lines":["With half that wish the wisher's eyes be pressed!"],"id":177,"m":"May half that wish close your own eyes in rest!"},{"t":"sd","text":"They sleep","id":178},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter PUCK","id":179},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCK","lines":["Through the forest have I gone,","But Athenian found I none,","On whose eyes I might approve","This flower's force in stirring love.","Night and silence! Who is here?","Weeds of Athens he doth wear:","This is he, my master said,","Despisèd the Athenian maid;","And here the maiden, sleeping sound,","On the dank and dirty ground.","Pretty soul, she durst not lie","Near this lack-love, this kill-courtesy.","Churl, upon thy eyes I throw","All the power this charm doth owe;","When thou wak'st let love forbid","Sleep his seat on thy eyelid.","So awake when I am gone;","For I must now to Oberon."],"id":180,"m":"I've been through the whole forest, but I haven't found any Athenian on whose eyes I could test this flower's power to stir up love. Night and silence! Who's this? He's wearing Athenian clothes. This is the one my master said scorned the Athenian girl. And here is the girl, fast asleep on the damp, dirty ground. Pretty thing, she didn't dare lie near this loveless, rude man. You lout, on your eyes I pour all the power this charm has. When you wake, let love keep sleep from ever settling on your eyelids again. So wake up when I've gone, because I must go back to Oberon now."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit","id":181},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter DEMETRIUS and HELENA, running","id":182},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["Stay, though thou kill me, sweet Demetrius."],"id":183,"m":"Stop, even if you kill me, sweet Demetrius."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["I charge thee, hence, and do not haunt me thus."],"id":184,"m":"I order you to go away. Stop haunting me like this."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["O, wilt thou darkling leave me? Do not so."],"id":185,"m":"Oh, will you leave me here in the dark? Don't do that."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["Stay, on thy peril; I alone will go."],"id":186,"m":"Stay here, or you'll be in danger. I'm going on alone."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit DEMETRIUS","id":187},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["O, I am out of breath in this fond chase!","The more my prayer, the lesser is my grace.","Happy is Hermia, wheresoe'er she lies,","For she hath blessèd and attractive eyes.","How came her eyes so bright? Not with salt tears.","If so, my eyes are oftener wash'd than hers.","No, no, I am as ugly as a bear,","For beasts that meet me run away for fear:","Therefore no marvel though Demetrius","Do, as a monster, fly my presence thus.","What wicked and dissembling glass of mine","Made me compare with Hermia's sphery eyne?","But who is here? Lysander, on the ground!","Dead or asleep? I see no blood, no wound.","Lysander, if you live, good sir, awake."],"id":188,"m":"Oh, I'm out of breath from this foolish chase! The more I beg, the less I get. Hermia is lucky, wherever she is, because she has beautiful, attractive eyes. How did her eyes become so bright? Not with salty tears. If they did, my eyes are washed more often than hers. No, no, I'm as ugly as a bear, because animals that meet me run away in fear. So it's no wonder Demetrius runs from me as if I were a monster. What lying, wicked mirror made me think I could compare with Hermia's starry eyes? But who's this? Lysander, on the ground! Dead or asleep? I can't see any blood or wound. Lysander, if you're alive, good sir, wake up."},{"t":"sp","who":"LYSANDER","lines":["[Waking] And run through fire I will for thy sweet sake.","Transparent Helena! Nature shows art,","That through thy bosom makes me see thy heart.","Where is Demetrius? O, how fit a word","Is that vile name to perish on my sword!"],"id":189,"m":"And I'd run through fire for your sweet sake! Radiant Helena! Nature works magic: I can see your heart through your breast. Where is Demetrius? Oh, how right it would be for that vile name to die on my sword!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["Do not say so, Lysander, say not so.","What though he love your Hermia? Lord, what though?","Yet Hermia still loves you. Then be content."],"id":190,"m":"Don't say that, Lysander, don't say it. What does it matter if he loves your Hermia? Lord, what does it matter? Hermia still loves you, so be happy."},{"t":"sp","who":"LYSANDER","lines":["Content with Hermia? No, I do repent","The tedious minutes I with her have spent.","Not Hermia, but Helena I love.","Who will not change a raven for a dove?","The will of man is by his reason sway'd,","And reason says you are the worthier maid.","Things growing are not ripe until their season;","So I, being young, till now ripe not to reason;","And touching now the point of human skill,","Reason becomes the marshal to my will,","And leads me to your eyes, where I o'erlook","Love's stories, written in love's richest book."],"id":191,"m":"Happy with Hermia? No, I regret the boring minutes I've spent with her. It's not Hermia but Helena I love. Who wouldn't swap a raven for a dove? A man's desires are ruled by his reason, and reason says you are the better woman. Things that grow aren't ripe until their season comes, so I, being young, wasn't ripe for reason until now. Now that I've reached the peak of human judgement, reason commands my desire and leads me to your eyes, where I read love's stories, written in love's richest book."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["Wherefore was I to this keen mockery born?","When at your hands did I deserve this scorn?","Is't not enough, is't not enough, young man,","That I did never, no, nor never can","Deserve a sweet look from Demetrius' eye,","But you must flout my insufficiency?","Good troth, you do me wrong, good sooth, you do,","In such disdainful manner me to woo.","But fare you well; perforce I must confess,","I thought you lord of more true gentleness.","O, that a lady of one man refus'd,","Should of another therefore be abus'd!"],"id":192,"m":"Why was I born to be mocked so cruelly? What have I ever done to deserve such contempt from you? Isn't it enough, isn't it enough, young man, that I never have and never can earn one kind look from Demetrius, without you making fun of how I fall short? Honestly, you do me wrong, truly you do, to court me in such a sneering way. But goodbye. I have to admit I thought you were a more genuine gentleman. Oh, that a lady rejected by one man should be insulted by another because of it!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit","id":193},{"t":"sp","who":"LYSANDER","lines":["She sees not Hermia. Hermia, sleep thou there,","And never mayst thou come Lysander near!","For, as a surfeit of the sweetest things","The deepest loathing to the stomach brings;","Or as the heresies that men do leave","Are hated most of those they did deceive;","So thou, my surfeit and my heresy,","Of all be hated, but the most of me!","And, all my powers, address your love and might","To honour Helen, and to be her knight!"],"id":194,"m":"She hasn't seen Hermia. Hermia, sleep there, and may you never come near Lysander again! Just as eating too many sweet things makes the stomach feel sick of them, or as men who give up false beliefs hate them most because they were fooled by them, so you, my sickness and my false belief, be hated by everyone, but most of all by me! And all my powers, use your love and strength to honour Helena and be her knight!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit","id":195},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIA","lines":["[Starting] Help me, Lysander, help me! Do thy best","To pluck this crawling serpent from my breast!","Ay me, for pity! What a dream was here!","Lysander, look how I do quake with fear.","Methought a serpent eat my heart away,","And you sat smiling at his cruel prey.","Lysander! What, removed? Lysander! lord!","What, out of hearing? Gone? No sound, no word?","Alack, where are you? Speak, and if you hear;","Speak, of all loves! I swoon almost with fear.","No? Then I well perceive you are not nigh.","Either death or you I'll find immediately."],"id":196,"m":"Help me, Lysander, help me! Do your best to pull this crawling snake off my breast! Oh, have pity on me! What a dream that was! Lysander, look how I'm shaking with fear. I dreamed a snake was eating my heart, and you sat smiling while it attacked me. Lysander! What, gone? Lysander! My lord! What, out of earshot? Gone? No sound, no word? Oh no, where are you? Speak, if you can hear me. Speak, for love's sake! I'm almost fainting with fear. No? Then I can see you're not nearby. I'll find either you or my death straight away."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit","id":197},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"I","loc":"The Wood.","id":198},{"t":"sd","text":"The Queen of Fairies still lying asleep","id":199},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter BOTTOM, QUINCE, SNOUT, STARVELING, SNUG and FLUTE","id":200},{"t":"sp","who":"BOTTOM","lines":["Are we all met?"],"id":201,"m":"Are we all here?"},{"t":"sp","who":"QUINCE","lines":["Pat, pat; and here's a marvellous convenient place for our rehearsal.","This green plot shall be our stage, this hawthorn brake our","tiring-house; and we will do it in action, as we will do it before the","Duke."],"id":202,"m":"Right on time. And here's a wonderfully handy place for our rehearsal. This patch of grass will be our stage, this hawthorn bush our dressing room, and we'll act it out properly, just as we'll do it in front of the Duke."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOTTOM","lines":["Peter Quince?"],"id":203,"m":"Peter Quince?"},{"t":"sp","who":"QUINCE","lines":["What sayest thou, bully Bottom?"],"id":204,"m":"What is it, good old Bottom?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOTTOM","lines":["There are things in this comedy of Pyramus and Thisbe that will never","please. First, Pyramus must draw a sword to kill himself; which the","ladies cannot abide. How answer you that?"],"id":205,"m":"There are things in this comedy of Pyramus and Thisbe that will never go down well. First, Pyramus has to draw a sword to kill himself, which the ladies won't be able to stand. What do you say to that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SNOUT","lines":["By'r lakin, a parlous fear."],"id":206,"m":"By our Lady, that's a terrible worry."},{"t":"sp","who":"STARVELING","lines":["I believe we must leave the killing out, when all is done."],"id":207,"m":"I think we'll have to leave the killing out, in the end."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOTTOM","lines":["Not a whit; I have a device to make all well. Write me a prologue, and","let the prologue seem to say we will do no harm with our swords, and","that Pyramus is not killed indeed; and for the more better assurance,","tell them that I Pyramus am not Pyramus but Bottom the weaver. This","will put them out of fear."],"id":208,"m":"Not at all. I have a plan to make everything all right. Write me a prologue, and have the prologue seem to say that we will do no harm with our swords, and that Pyramus isn't really killed. And to reassure them even more, tell them that I, Pyramus, am not Pyramus but Bottom the weaver. That will stop them being afraid."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUINCE","lines":["Well, we will have such a prologue; and it shall be written in eight","and six."],"id":209,"m":"All right, we'll have a prologue like that, and it will be written in lines of eight and six syllables."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOTTOM","lines":["No, make it two more; let it be written in eight and eight."],"id":210,"m":"No, add two more. Let it be written in eight and eight."},{"t":"sp","who":"SNOUT","lines":["Will not the ladies be afeard of the lion?"],"id":211,"m":"Won't the ladies be afraid of the lion?"},{"t":"sp","who":"STARVELING","lines":["I fear it, I promise you."],"id":212,"m":"I'm afraid of it myself, I promise you."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOTTOM","lines":["Masters, you ought to consider with yourselves, to bring in (God shield","us!) a lion among ladies is a most dreadful thing. For there is not a","more fearful wild-fowl than your lion living; and we ought to look to","it."],"id":213,"m":"Gentlemen, you should think carefully about this. To bring in, God help us, a lion among ladies is a most terrible thing. There's no more frightening 'wild bird' alive than your lion, and we ought to watch out."},{"t":"sp","who":"SNOUT","lines":["Therefore another prologue must tell he is not a lion."],"id":214,"m":"Then another prologue must tell them he isn't a lion."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOTTOM","lines":["Nay, you must name his name, and half his face must be seen through the","lion's neck; and he himself must speak through, saying thus, or to the","same defect: 'Ladies,' or, 'Fair ladies, I would wish you,' or, 'I","would request you,' or, 'I would entreat you, not to fear, not to","tremble: my life for yours. If you think I come hither as a lion, it","were pity of my life. No, I am no such thing; I am a man as other men","are': and there, indeed, let him name his name, and tell them plainly","he is Snug the joiner."],"id":215,"m":"No, you must say his name, and half his face must show through the lion's neck, and he himself must speak through it, saying this, or something to the same 'defect': 'Ladies,' or 'Fair ladies, I would wish you,' or 'I would ask you,' or 'I would beg you, not to be afraid, not to tremble. I'd stake my life on your safety. If you think I have come here as a lion, I'd be sorry for my life. No, I am nothing of the kind. I am a man like other men.' And then, right there, let him say his name and tell them plainly that he is Snug the joiner."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUINCE","lines":["Well, it shall be so. But there is two hard things: that is, to bring","the moonlight into a chamber, for you know, Pyramus and Thisbe meet by","moonlight."],"id":216,"m":"All right, that's what we'll do. But there are two difficult things. One is to bring the moonlight into a room, because, you know, Pyramus and Thisbe meet by moonlight."},{"t":"sp","who":"SNOUT","lines":["Doth the moon shine that night we play our play?"],"id":217,"m":"Does the moon shine on the night we do our play?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOTTOM","lines":["A calendar, a calendar! Look in the almanack; find out moonshine, find","out moonshine."],"id":218,"m":"A calendar, a calendar! Look in the almanac. Find out about moonshine, find out about moonshine!"},{"t":"sp","who":"QUINCE","lines":["Yes, it doth shine that night."],"id":219,"m":"Yes, it does shine that night."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOTTOM","lines":["Why, then may you leave a casement of the great chamber window, where","we play, open; and the moon may shine in at the casement."],"id":220,"m":"Well then, you could leave one of the windows open in the great hall where we're performing, and the moon can shine in through the window."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUINCE","lines":["Ay; or else one must come in with a bush of thorns and a lantern, and","say he comes to disfigure or to present the person of Moonshine. Then","there is another thing: we must have a wall in the great chamber; for","Pyramus and Thisbe, says the story, did talk through the chink of a","wall."],"id":221,"m":"Yes, or else someone could come in with a bundle of sticks and a lantern and say he has come to 'disfigure', or to play, the character of Moonshine. Then there's another thing. We must have a wall in the great hall, because Pyramus and Thisbe, the story says, talked through a crack in a wall."},{"t":"sp","who":"SNOUT","lines":["You can never bring in a wall. What say you, Bottom?"],"id":222,"m":"You can never bring in a wall. What do you say, Bottom?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOTTOM","lines":["Some man or other must present Wall. And let him have some plaster, or","some loam, or some rough-cast about him, to signify wall; and let him","hold his fingers thus, and through that cranny shall Pyramus and Thisbe","whisper."],"id":223,"m":"Somebody or other must play Wall. Let him wear some plaster, or some clay, or some rough lime and gravel, to show he's a wall. And let him hold his fingers like this, and Pyramus and Thisbe can whisper through that crack."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUINCE","lines":["If that may be, then all is well. Come, sit down, every mother's son,","and rehearse your parts. Pyramus, you begin: when you have spoken your","speech, enter into that brake; and so everyone according to his cue."],"id":224,"m":"If we can do that, everything's fine. Come on, sit down, every one of you, and rehearse your parts. Pyramus, you start. When you've said your speech, go into that bush, and so on, everyone when it's his cue."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter PUCK behind","id":225},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCK","lines":["What hempen homespuns have we swaggering here,","So near the cradle of the Fairy Queen?","What, a play toward? I'll be an auditor;","An actor too perhaps, if I see cause."],"id":226,"m":"Who are these rough peasants strutting about here, so near the Fairy Queen's bed? What, a play coming up? I'll be in the audience. Maybe an actor too, if I see a reason."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUINCE","lines":["Speak, Pyramus.—Thisbe, stand forth."],"id":227,"m":"Speak, Pyramus. Thisbe, step forward."},{"t":"sp","who":"PYRAMUS","lines":["Thisbe, the flowers of odious savours sweet"],"id":228,"m":"Thisbe, the flowers of hateful smells are sweet..."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUINCE","lines":["Odours, odours."],"id":229,"m":"Odours, odours!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PYRAMUS","lines":[". . . odours savours sweet.","So hath thy breath, my dearest Thisbe dear.","But hark, a voice! Stay thou but here awhile,","And by and by I will to thee appear."],"id":230,"m":"...odours smell sweet. So does your breath, my dearest Thisbe dear. But listen, a voice! Wait here a while, and soon I will appear to you."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit","id":231},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCK","lines":["A stranger Pyramus than e'er played here!"],"id":232,"m":"The strangest Pyramus that's ever been played here!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit","id":233},{"t":"sp","who":"THISBE","lines":["Must I speak now?"],"id":234,"m":"Do I speak now?"},{"t":"sp","who":"QUINCE","lines":["Ay, marry, must you, For you must understand he goes but to see a noise","that he heard, and is to come again."],"id":235,"m":"Yes, of course you do. You see, he's only gone to see about a noise he heard, and he's coming back."},{"t":"sp","who":"THISBE","lines":["Most radiant Pyramus, most lily-white of hue,","Of colour like the red rose on triumphant brier,","Most brisky juvenal, and eke most lovely Jew,","As true as truest horse, that yet would never tire,","I'll meet thee, Pyramus, at Ninny's tomb."],"id":236,"m":"Most radiant Pyramus, as white as a lily, coloured like a red rose on a splendid briar, most lively youth and also most lovely 'Jew', as faithful as the most faithful horse that never gets tired, I'll meet you, Pyramus, at Ninny's tomb."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUINCE","lines":["Ninus' tomb, man! Why, you must not speak that yet. That you answer to","Pyramus. You speak all your part at once, cues, and all.—Pyramus enter!","Your cue is past; it is 'never tire.'"],"id":237,"m":"Ninus's tomb, man! And you mustn't say that yet. That's your answer to Pyramus. You're saying your whole part at once, cues and all. Pyramus, come in! You've missed your cue. It's 'never tire'."},{"t":"sp","who":"THISBE","lines":["O, As true as truest horse, that yet would never tire."],"id":238,"m":"Oh: as faithful as the most faithful horse that never gets tired."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter PUCK and BOTTOM with an ass's head","id":239},{"t":"sp","who":"PYRAMUS","lines":["If I were fair, Thisbe, I were only thine."],"id":240,"m":"If I were handsome, Thisbe, I would be yours alone."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUINCE","lines":["O monstrous! O strange! We are haunted. Pray, masters, fly, masters!","Help!"],"id":241,"m":"How monstrous! How strange! We're haunted! Run, gentlemen, run! Help!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Clowns","id":242},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCK","lines":["I'll follow you. I'll lead you about a round,","Through bog, through bush, through brake, through brier;","Sometime a horse I'll be, sometime a hound,","A hog, a headless bear, sometime a fire;","And neigh, and bark, and grunt, and roar, and burn,","Like horse, hound, hog, bear, fire, at every turn."],"id":243,"m":"I'll follow you. I'll lead you round in circles, through bog, through bush, through thicket, through briar. Sometimes I'll be a horse, sometimes a hound, a pig, a headless bear, sometimes a fire. And I'll neigh and bark and grunt and roar and burn, like a horse, hound, pig, bear and fire, at every turn."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit","id":244},{"t":"sp","who":"BOTTOM","lines":["Why do they run away? This is a knavery of them to make me afeard."],"id":245,"m":"Why are they running away? This is a trick of theirs to scare me."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter SNOUT","id":246},{"t":"sp","who":"SNOUT","lines":["O Bottom, thou art changed! What do I see on thee?"],"id":247,"m":"Oh, Bottom, you've changed! What's that I see on you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOTTOM","lines":["What do you see? You see an ass-head of your own, do you?"],"id":248,"m":"What do you see? You see an ass's head of your own, do you?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit SNOUT","id":249},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter QUINCE","id":250},{"t":"sp","who":"QUINCE","lines":["Bless thee, Bottom! bless thee! Thou art translated."],"id":251,"m":"God bless you, Bottom! God bless you! You've been transformed."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit","id":252},{"t":"sp","who":"BOTTOM","lines":["I see their knavery. This is to make an ass of me, to fright me, if","they could. But I will not stir from this place, do what they can. I","will walk up and down here, and I will sing, that they shall hear I am","not afraid.","[Sings]","~The ousel cock, so black of hue,","~With orange-tawny bill,","~The throstle with his note so true,","~The wren with little quill."],"id":253,"m":"I see their trick. This is to make an ass of me, to frighten me if they could. But I won't move from this spot, whatever they do. I'll walk up and down here and I'll sing, so they'll hear I'm not afraid. [He sings] The blackbird, so black in colour, with its orange-brown beak, the thrush with its song so true, the wren with its little quill."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITANIA","lines":["[Waking] What angel wakes me from my flowery bed?"],"id":254,"m":"What angel wakes me from my bed of flowers?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOTTOM","lines":["[Sings]","~The finch, the sparrow, and the lark,","~The plain-song cuckoo gray,","~Whose note full many a man doth mark,","~And dares not answer nay.","for, indeed, who would set his wit to so foolish a bird? Who would give","a bird the lie, though he cry 'cuckoo' never so?"],"id":255,"m":"The finch, the sparrow and the lark, the grey cuckoo with its simple song, whose call many a man hears and dares not deny. Because, honestly, who would waste his brains answering such a foolish bird? Who would call a bird a liar, however often it cries 'cuckoo'?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TITANIA","lines":["I pray thee, gentle mortal, sing again.","Mine ear is much enamour'd of thy note.","So is mine eye enthrallèd to thy shape;","And thy fair virtue's force perforce doth move me,","On the first view, to say, to swear, I love thee."],"id":256,"m":"Please, gentle mortal, sing again. My ear is in love with your song. And my eye is enchanted by your shape, and the power of your fine qualities forces me, at first sight, to say, to swear, that I love you."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOTTOM","lines":["Methinks, mistress, you should have little reason for that. And yet, to","say the truth, reason and love keep little company together nowadays.","The more the pity that some honest neighbours will not make them","friends. Nay, I can gleek upon occasion."],"id":257,"m":"I think, madam, you have very little reason for that. And yet, to tell the truth, reason and love don't spend much time together these days. It's a pity some honest neighbour doesn't make them friends. There, I can make a clever joke when I want to."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITANIA","lines":["Thou art as wise as thou art beautiful."],"id":258,"m":"You are as wise as you are beautiful."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOTTOM","lines":["Not so, neither; but if I had wit enough to get out of this wood, I","have enough to serve mine own turn."],"id":259,"m":"No, I'm not. But if I had the brains to get out of this wood, I'd have enough for what I need."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITANIA","lines":["Out of this wood do not desire to go.","Thou shalt remain here whether thou wilt or no.","I am a spirit of no common rate.","The summer still doth tend upon my state;","And I do love thee: therefore, go with me.","I'll give thee fairies to attend on thee;","And they shall fetch thee jewels from the deep,","And sing, while thou on pressèd flowers dost sleep.","And I will purge thy mortal grossness so","That thou shalt like an airy spirit go.—","Peaseblossom! Cobweb! Moth! and Mustardseed!"],"id":260,"m":"Don't wish to leave this wood. You will stay here whether you want to or not. I am no ordinary spirit. Summer itself always waits on me as its queen, and I love you. So come with me. I'll give you fairies to serve you, and they will fetch you jewels from the bottom of the sea and sing while you sleep on a bed of flowers. And I will clean away your mortal heaviness so that you can move like an airy spirit. Peaseblossom! Cobweb! Moth! And Mustardseed!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter four Fairies","id":261},{"t":"sp","who":"PEASEBLOSSOM","lines":["Ready."],"id":262,"m":"Here."},{"t":"sp","who":"COBWEB","lines":["And I."],"id":263,"m":"And me."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["And I."],"id":264,"m":"And me."},{"t":"sp","who":"MUSTARDSEED","lines":["And I."],"id":265,"m":"And me."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALL","lines":["Where shall we go?"],"id":266,"m":"Where shall we go?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TITANIA","lines":["Be kind and courteous to this gentleman;","Hop in his walks and gambol in his eyes;","Feed him with apricocks and dewberries,","With purple grapes, green figs, and mulberries;","The honey-bags steal from the humble-bees,","And for night-tapers, crop their waxen thighs,","And light them at the fiery glow-worm's eyes,","To have my love to bed and to arise;","And pluck the wings from painted butterflies,","To fan the moonbeams from his sleeping eyes.","Nod to him, elves, and do him courtesies."],"id":267,"m":"Be kind and polite to this gentleman. Hop where he walks and dance in front of his eyes. Feed him with apricots and blackberries, purple grapes, green figs and mulberries. Steal the honey-sacs from the bumblebees, and for candles, take the wax from their legs and light them from the fiery eyes of glow-worms, to light my love to bed and to wake him. And pull the wings off painted butterflies to fan the moonlight away from his sleeping eyes. Bow to him, elves, and pay him your respects."},{"t":"sp","who":"PEASEBLOSSOM","lines":["Hail, mortal!"],"id":268,"m":"Greetings, mortal!"},{"t":"sp","who":"COBWEB","lines":["Hail!"],"id":269,"m":"Greetings!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["Hail!"],"id":270,"m":"Greetings!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MUSTARDSEED","lines":["Hail!"],"id":271,"m":"Greetings!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOTTOM","lines":["I cry your worships mercy, heartily.—I beseech your worship's name."],"id":272,"m":"I humbly beg your worships' pardon. Please may I ask your worship's name?"},{"t":"sp","who":"COBWEB","lines":["Cobweb."],"id":273,"m":"Cobweb."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOTTOM","lines":["I shall desire you of more acquaintance, good Master Cobweb. If I cut","my finger, I shall make bold with you.—Your name, honest gentleman?"],"id":274,"m":"I hope to get to know you better, good Master Cobweb. If I cut my finger, I'll make use of you. Your name, honest gentleman?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PEASEBLOSSOM","lines":["Peaseblossom."],"id":275,"m":"Peaseblossom."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOTTOM","lines":["I pray you, commend me to Mistress Squash, your mother, and to Master","Peascod, your father. Good Master Peaseblossom, I shall desire you of","more acquaintance too.—Your name, I beseech you, sir?"],"id":276,"m":"Please give my regards to Mrs Squash, your mother, and Mr Peascod, your father. Good Master Peaseblossom, I hope to know you better too. Your name, please, sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MUSTARDSEED","lines":["Mustardseed."],"id":277,"m":"Mustardseed."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOTTOM","lines":["Good Master Mustardseed, I know your patience well. That same cowardly","giant-like ox-beef hath devoured many a gentleman of your house. I","promise you, your kindred hath made my eyes water ere now. I desire you","of more acquaintance, good Master Mustardseed."],"id":278,"m":"Good Master Mustardseed, I know all about your suffering. That cowardly, giant-sized roast beef has eaten many gentlemen of your family. I promise you, your relatives have made my eyes water before now. I hope to know you better, good Master Mustardseed."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITANIA","lines":["Come, wait upon him; lead him to my bower.","The moon, methinks, looks with a watery eye,","And when she weeps, weeps every little flower,","Lamenting some enforced chastity.","Tie up my love's tongue, bring him silently."],"id":279,"m":"Come, attend to him. Lead him to my bower. The moon looks to me as if her eyes are wet, and when she cries, every little flower cries too, mourning some girl's lost virginity. Tie up my love's tongue. Bring him quietly."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt","id":280},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"II","loc":"Another part of the wood.","id":281},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter OBERON","id":282},{"t":"sp","who":"OBERON","lines":["I wonder if Titania be awak'd;","Then, what it was that next came in her eye,","Which she must dote on in extremity."],"id":283,"m":"I wonder if Titania has woken up, and what was the first thing she saw, which she must now love to distraction."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter PUCK","id":284},{"t":"sp","who":"OBERON","cont":true,"lines":["Here comes my messenger. How now, mad spirit?","What night-rule now about this haunted grove?"],"id":285,"m":"Here comes my messenger. Well, crazy spirit? What night-time mischief is going on in this haunted wood?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCK","lines":["My mistress with a monster is in love.","Near to her close and consecrated bower,","While she was in her dull and sleeping hour,","A crew of patches, rude mechanicals,","That work for bread upon Athenian stalls,","Were met together to rehearse a play","Intended for great Theseus' nuptial day.","The shallowest thick-skin of that barren sort","Who Pyramus presented in their sport,","Forsook his scene and enter'd in a brake.","When I did him at this advantage take,","An ass's nole I fixed on his head.","Anon, his Thisbe must be answerèd,","And forth my mimic comes. When they him spy,","As wild geese that the creeping fowler eye,","Or russet-pated choughs, many in sort,","Rising and cawing at the gun's report,","Sever themselves and madly sweep the sky,","So at his sight away his fellows fly,","And at our stamp, here o'er and o'er one falls;","He murder cries, and help from Athens calls.","Their sense thus weak, lost with their fears, thus strong,","Made senseless things begin to do them wrong;","For briers and thorns at their apparel snatch;","Some sleeves, some hats, from yielders all things catch.","I led them on in this distracted fear,","And left sweet Pyramus translated there.","When in that moment, so it came to pass,","Titania wak'd, and straightway lov'd an ass."],"id":286,"m":"My mistress is in love with a monster. Near her private, sacred bower, while she was sleeping, a gang of fools, rough workmen who earn their bread in the Athenian market, met to rehearse a play they are planning for great Theseus's wedding day. The stupidest blockhead of that brainless lot, who played Pyramus in their show, left his scene and went into a thicket. When I caught him there, I fixed an ass's head on him. Soon he had to answer his Thisbe, and out comes my actor. When they saw him, like wild geese that spot the hunter creeping up, or a big flock of grey-headed jackdaws rising and cawing at the sound of a gun and scattering wildly across the sky, his friends ran away at the sight of him. And when I stamped my foot, one after another fell over. One shouted 'Murder!' and called to Athens for help. Their wits were so weak, and their fears so strong, that lifeless things began to attack them: briars and thorns snatched at their clothes, a sleeve here, a hat there. Everything grabs from a man who gives in. I led them on in this crazy fear, and left sweet Pyramus, transformed, behind. At that very moment, as it happened, Titania woke and immediately fell in love with an ass."},{"t":"sp","who":"OBERON","lines":["This falls out better than I could devise.","But hast thou yet latch'd the Athenian's eyes","With the love-juice, as I did bid thee do?"],"id":287,"m":"This has turned out better than I could have planned. But have you put the love-juice on the Athenian's eyes, as I told you to?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCK","lines":["I took him sleeping—that is finish'd too—","And the Athenian woman by his side,","That, when he wak'd, of force she must be ey'd."],"id":288,"m":"I caught him sleeping. That's done too. And the Athenian woman was beside him, so that when he wakes, he's bound to see her."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter DEMETRIUS and HERMIA","id":289},{"t":"sp","who":"OBERON","lines":["Stand close. This is the same Athenian."],"id":290,"m":"Keep out of sight. This is the same Athenian."},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCK","lines":["This is the woman, but not this the man."],"id":291,"m":"This is the woman, but that's not the man."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["O why rebuke you him that loves you so?","Lay breath so bitter on your bitter foe."],"id":292,"m":"Oh, why do you scold a man who loves you so much? Save such bitter words for your bitter enemy."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIA","lines":["Now I but chide, but I should use thee worse,","For thou, I fear, hast given me cause to curse.","If thou hast slain Lysander in his sleep,","Being o'er shoes in blood, plunge in the deep,","And kill me too.","The sun was not so true unto the day","As he to me. Would he have stol'n away","From sleeping Hermia? I'll believe as soon","This whole earth may be bor'd, and that the moon","May through the centre creep and so displease","Her brother's noontide with th' Antipodes.","It cannot be but thou hast murder'd him.","So should a murderer look, so dead, so grim."],"id":293,"m":"For now I'm only scolding you, but I ought to treat you worse, because I'm afraid you've given me reason to curse you. If you've killed Lysander in his sleep, you're already ankle-deep in blood, so wade in deeper and kill me too. The sun was never as faithful to the day as he was to me. Would he have crept away from Hermia while she was asleep? I'd sooner believe that a hole could be drilled through the earth, and that the moon could creep through it and annoy her brother the sun at noon on the other side of the world. You must have murdered him. That's how a murderer looks, so deathly pale, so grim."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["So should the murder'd look, and so should I,","Pierc'd through the heart with your stern cruelty.","Yet you, the murderer, look as bright, as clear,","As yonder Venus in her glimmering sphere."],"id":294,"m":"That's how the murdered man looks, and that's how I look, stabbed through the heart by your harsh cruelty. Yet you, the murderer, look as bright and clear as Venus, shining up there in her orbit."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIA","lines":["What's this to my Lysander? Where is he?","Ah, good Demetrius, wilt thou give him me?"],"id":295,"m":"What has this got to do with my Lysander? Where is he? Oh, good Demetrius, will you give him back to me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["I had rather give his carcass to my hounds."],"id":296,"m":"I'd rather give his dead body to my dogs."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIA","lines":["Out, dog! Out, cur! Thou driv'st me past the bounds","Of maiden's patience. Hast thou slain him, then?","Henceforth be never number'd among men!","O once tell true; tell true, even for my sake!","Durst thou have look'd upon him, being awake,","And hast thou kill'd him sleeping? O brave touch!","Could not a worm, an adder, do so much?","An adder did it; for with doubler tongue","Than thine, thou serpent, never adder stung."],"id":297,"m":"Get out, dog! Get out, cur! You're driving me past the limits of a girl's patience. So have you killed him? From now on, don't ever count yourself a man! Oh, tell the truth for once, tell the truth, even if only for my sake! Would you have dared to look at him while he was awake, and did you kill him while he slept? Oh, how brave! Couldn't a worm or a snake do as much? A snake did do it, because no snake ever stung with a more forked tongue than yours, you serpent."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["You spend your passion on a mispris'd mood:","I am not guilty of Lysander's blood;","Nor is he dead, for aught that I can tell."],"id":298,"m":"You're wasting your anger on a mistake. I'm not guilty of Lysander's blood, and he isn't dead, as far as I know."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIA","lines":["I pray thee, tell me then that he is well."],"id":299,"m":"Then please tell me he's all right."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["And if I could, what should I get therefore?"],"id":300,"m":"And if I could, what would I get for it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIA","lines":["A privilege never to see me more.","And from thy hated presence part I so:","See me no more, whether he be dead or no."],"id":301,"m":"The privilege of never seeing me again. And so I leave your hateful company. Don't see me again, whether he's dead or not."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit","id":302},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["There is no following her in this fierce vein.","Here, therefore, for a while I will remain.","So sorrow's heaviness doth heavier grow","For debt that bankrupt sleep doth sorrow owe;","Which now in some slight measure it will pay,","If for his tender here I make some stay."],"id":303,"m":"There's no point following her while she's in this furious mood. So I'll stay here for a while. Sorrow gets heavier when sleep, like a bankrupt, can't pay what it owes. Now sleep will pay back a little of its debt, if I stay here and accept its offer."},{"t":"sd","text":"Lies down","id":304},{"t":"sp","who":"OBERON","lines":["What hast thou done? Thou hast mistaken quite,","And laid the love-juice on some true-love's sight.","Of thy misprision must perforce ensue","Some true love turn'd, and not a false turn'd true."],"id":305,"m":"What have you done? You've made a complete mistake and put the love-juice on the eyes of someone truly in love. Your error is bound to turn a true love false, instead of a false love true."},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCK","lines":["Then fate o'er-rules, that, one man holding troth,","A million fail, confounding oath on oath."],"id":306,"m":"Then fate has taken over: for every man who keeps his promise, a million fail, breaking oath after oath."},{"t":"sp","who":"OBERON","lines":["About the wood go swifter than the wind,","And Helena of Athens look thou find.","All fancy-sick she is, and pale of cheer","With sighs of love, that costs the fresh blood dear.","By some illusion see thou bring her here;","I'll charm his eyes against she do appear."],"id":307,"m":"Go round the wood faster than the wind, and make sure you find Helena of Athens. She is lovesick and pale-faced from the sighs of love, which drain away the fresh blood. Bring her here with some trick. I'll put a spell on his eyes ready for when she appears."},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCK","lines":["I go, I go; look how I go,","Swifter than arrow from the Tartar's bow."],"id":308,"m":"I'm going, I'm going. Look how I go, faster than an arrow from a Tartar's bow."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit","id":309},{"t":"sp","who":"OBERON","lines":["~Flower of this purple dye,","~Hit with Cupid's archery,","~Sink in apple of his eye.","~When his love he doth espy,","~Let her shine as gloriously","~As the Venus of the sky.—","~When thou wak'st, if she be by,","~Beg of her for remedy."],"id":310,"m":"Flower with this purple colour, struck by Cupid's arrow, sink into the pupil of his eye. When he sees his love, let her shine as brilliantly as Venus in the sky. When you wake, if she is near, beg her to cure you."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter PUCK","id":311},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCK","lines":["Captain of our fairy band,","Helena is here at hand,","And the youth mistook by me,","Pleading for a lover's fee.","Shall we their fond pageant see?","Lord, what fools these mortals be!"],"id":312,"m":"Captain of our fairy army, Helena is close by, and the young man I made the mistake with, begging her to love him. Shall we watch their silly show? Lord, what fools these mortals are!"},{"t":"sp","who":"OBERON","lines":["Stand aside. The noise they make","Will cause Demetrius to awake."],"id":313,"m":"Stand aside. The noise they're making will wake Demetrius."},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCK","lines":["Then will two at once woo one.","That must needs be sport alone;","And those things do best please me","That befall prepost'rously."],"id":314,"m":"Then two will be courting one at the same time. That will be fun all on its own. And the things that please me best are the ones that turn out all upside down."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter LYSANDER and HELENA","id":315},{"t":"sp","who":"LYSANDER","lines":["Why should you think that I should woo in scorn?","Scorn and derision never come in tears.","Look when I vow, I weep; and vows so born,","In their nativity all truth appears.","How can these things in me seem scorn to you,","Bearing the badge of faith, to prove them true?"],"id":316,"m":"Why should you think I'm courting you to mock you? Mockery and scorn never come with tears. Look, when I make a promise, I cry, and promises born like that show they are true from the moment they're made. How can these things look like mockery to you, when they carry the proof of faithfulness, my tears, to show they're real?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["You do advance your cunning more and more.","When truth kills truth, O devilish-holy fray!","These vows are Hermia's: will you give her o'er?","Weigh oath with oath, and you will nothing weigh:","Your vows to her and me, put in two scales,","Will even weigh; and both as light as tales."],"id":317,"m":"You're showing your cunning more and more. When one truth kills another, what a devilish holy fight! These promises belong to Hermia. Will you give her up? Weigh one promise against the other and you'll find they weigh nothing. Your promises to her and to me, put on two sides of a scale, will balance, and both are as light as fairy tales."},{"t":"sp","who":"LYSANDER","lines":["I had no judgment when to her I swore."],"id":318,"m":"I had no judgement when I made promises to her."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["Nor none, in my mind, now you give her o'er."],"id":319,"m":"And you have none now, I think, when you give her up."},{"t":"sp","who":"LYSANDER","lines":["Demetrius loves her, and he loves not you."],"id":320,"m":"Demetrius loves her, and he doesn't love you."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["[Waking] O Helen, goddess, nymph, perfect, divine!","To what, my love, shall I compare thine eyne?","Crystal is muddy. O how ripe in show","Thy lips, those kissing cherries, tempting grow!","That pure congealèd white, high Taurus' snow,","Fann'd with the eastern wind, turns to a crow","When thou hold'st up thy hand. O, let me kiss","This princess of pure white, this seal of bliss!"],"id":321,"m":"Oh Helena, goddess, nymph, perfect, divine! What can I compare your eyes to, my love? Crystal looks muddy beside them. Oh, how ripe and tempting your lips look, those kissing cherries! That pure, frozen white snow on the high Taurus mountains, blown by the east wind, looks as black as a crow when you hold up your hand. Oh, let me kiss this princess of pure whiteness, this promise of happiness!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["O spite! O hell! I see you all are bent","To set against me for your merriment.","If you were civil, and knew courtesy,","You would not do me thus much injury.","Can you not hate me, as I know you do,","But you must join in souls to mock me too?","If you were men, as men you are in show,","You would not use a gentle lady so;","To vow, and swear, and superpraise my parts,","When I am sure you hate me with your hearts.","You both are rivals, and love Hermia;","And now both rivals, to mock Helena.","A trim exploit, a manly enterprise,","To conjure tears up in a poor maid's eyes","With your derision! None of noble sort","Would so offend a virgin, and extort","A poor soul's patience, all to make you sport."],"id":322,"m":"How cruel! What hell! I can see you're all determined to gang up on me for your entertainment. If you were decent and had any manners, you wouldn't hurt me like this. Isn't it enough to hate me, as I know you do, without joining forces to mock me as well? If you were real men, as you look like men, you wouldn't treat a gentle lady like this: promising, swearing and overpraising my looks, when I'm sure you hate me with all your hearts. You're both rivals in love with Hermia, and now you're rivals in mocking Helena. A fine achievement, a manly deed, to bring tears to a poor girl's eyes with your scorn! No one of noble birth would insult a girl like that and push a poor soul's patience to breaking point, just to amuse yourselves."},{"t":"sp","who":"LYSANDER","lines":["You are unkind, Demetrius; be not so,","For you love Hermia; this you know I know.","And here, with all good will, with all my heart,","In Hermia's love I yield you up my part;","And yours of Helena to me bequeath,","Whom I do love and will do till my death."],"id":323,"m":"You're being unkind, Demetrius. Don't be like that, because you love Hermia; you know I know that. And here, with all my good will, with all my heart, I give you my share of Hermia's love. In return, give me your share of Helena, whom I love and will love until I die."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["Never did mockers waste more idle breath."],"id":324,"m":"Mockers never wasted so much useless breath."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["Lysander, keep thy Hermia; I will none.","If e'er I lov'd her, all that love is gone.","My heart to her but as guest-wise sojourn'd;","And now to Helen is it home return'd,","There to remain."],"id":325,"m":"Lysander, keep your Hermia. I don't want her. If I ever loved her, all that love has gone. My heart only stayed with her like a visitor, and now it has come home to Helena, to stay there."},{"t":"sp","who":"LYSANDER","lines":["Helen, it is not so."],"id":326,"m":"Helena, it isn't true."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["Disparage not the faith thou dost not know,","Lest to thy peril thou aby it dear.","Look where thy love comes; yonder is thy dear."],"id":327,"m":"Don't run down a love you know nothing about, or you'll pay dearly for it. Look, here comes your love. There's your darling."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter HERMIA","id":328},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIA","lines":["Dark night, that from the eye his function takes,","The ear more quick of apprehension makes;","Wherein it doth impair the seeing sense,","It pays the hearing double recompense.","Thou art not by mine eye, Lysander, found;","Mine ear, I thank it, brought me to thy sound.","But why unkindly didst thou leave me so?"],"id":329,"m":"Dark night, which takes away the eye's power to see, makes the ear quicker to hear. While it weakens our sight, it pays the hearing back twice over. My eyes couldn't find you, Lysander. My ear, I thank it, led me to the sound of your voice. But why did you leave me so cruelly?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LYSANDER","lines":["Why should he stay whom love doth press to go?"],"id":330,"m":"Why should someone stay when love urges him to go?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIA","lines":["What love could press Lysander from my side?"],"id":331,"m":"What love could drive Lysander from my side?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LYSANDER","lines":["Lysander's love, that would not let him bide,","Fair Helena, who more engilds the night","Than all yon fiery oes and eyes of light.","Why seek'st thou me? Could not this make thee know","The hate I bare thee made me leave thee so?"],"id":332,"m":"Lysander's love, which wouldn't let him stay: beautiful Helena, who brightens the night more than all those fiery circles and eyes of light up there. Why are you looking for me? Didn't this make you realise that it was because I hate you that I left you like that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIA","lines":["You speak not as you think; it cannot be."],"id":333,"m":"You don't mean what you're saying. It can't be true."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["Lo, she is one of this confederacy!","Now I perceive they have conjoin'd all three","To fashion this false sport in spite of me.","Injurious Hermia, most ungrateful maid!","Have you conspir'd, have you with these contriv'd,","To bait me with this foul derision?","Is all the counsel that we two have shar'd,","The sisters' vows, the hours that we have spent,","When we have chid the hasty-footed time","For parting us—O, is all forgot?","All school-days' friendship, childhood innocence?","We, Hermia, like two artificial gods,","Have with our needles created both one flower,","Both on one sampler, sitting on one cushion,","Both warbling of one song, both in one key,","As if our hands, our sides, voices, and minds,","Had been incorporate. So we grew together,","Like to a double cherry, seeming parted,","But yet a union in partition,","Two lovely berries moulded on one stem;","So, with two seeming bodies, but one heart;","Two of the first, like coats in heraldry,","Due but to one, and crownèd with one crest.","And will you rent our ancient love asunder,","To join with men in scorning your poor friend?","It is not friendly, 'tis not maidenly.","Our sex, as well as I, may chide you for it,","Though I alone do feel the injury."],"id":334,"m":"Look, she's part of this plot too! Now I see they've all three joined together to set up this cruel game to spite me. Hurtful Hermia, most ungrateful girl! Have you plotted, have you schemed with these men, to torment me with this foul mockery? Have all the secrets we two shared, the vows we made as sisters, the hours we spent together, when we scolded time for hurrying to part us, oh, have they all been forgotten? All our schooldays' friendship, our childhood innocence? Hermia, we were like two creative gods. With our needles we both made one flower, both sewing on one sampler, sitting on one cushion, both singing one song, both in the same key, as if our hands, our bodies, voices and minds were all one. So we grew up together like a double cherry, which looks like two but is joined though divided, two lovely berries growing on one stem. So we were two bodies with one heart, like the two halves of a coat of arms that belong to one family and are topped by one crest. And will you tear our old love apart, to join with men in mocking your poor friend? It isn't friendly. It isn't the way a girl should behave. All women, as well as me, could blame you for it, though I am the only one who feels the hurt."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIA","lines":["I am amazèd at your passionate words:","I scorn you not; it seems that you scorn me."],"id":335,"m":"Your passionate words amaze me. I'm not mocking you. It seems that you are mocking me."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["Have you not set Lysander, as in scorn,","To follow me, and praise my eyes and face?","And made your other love, Demetrius,","Who even but now did spurn me with his foot,","To call me goddess, nymph, divine and rare,","Precious, celestial? Wherefore speaks he this","To her he hates? And wherefore doth Lysander","Deny your love, so rich within his soul,","And tender me, forsooth, affection,","But by your setting on, by your consent?","What though I be not so in grace as you,","So hung upon with love, so fortunate,","But miserable most, to love unlov'd?","This you should pity rather than despise."],"id":336,"m":"Haven't you sent Lysander to follow me, to mock me, and praise my eyes and face? And made your other lover, Demetrius, who only just now kicked me away with his foot, call me goddess, nymph, divine, wonderful, precious, heavenly? Why does he say this to a woman he hates? And why does Lysander deny his love for you, which is so rich in his soul, and offer me, of all things, his affection, unless you put him up to it and agreed to it? So what if I'm not as popular as you, not as surrounded by love, not as lucky, but most miserable, loving and not being loved? You should pity this, not despise it."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIA","lines":["I understand not what you mean by this."],"id":337,"m":"I don't understand what you mean by this."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["Ay, do. Persever, counterfeit sad looks,","Make mouths upon me when I turn my back,","Wink each at other; hold the sweet jest up.","This sport, well carried, shall be chronicled.","If you have any pity, grace, or manners,","You would not make me such an argument.","But fare ye well. 'Tis partly my own fault,","Which death, or absence, soon shall remedy."],"id":338,"m":"Oh yes, carry on. Keep pretending to look serious, pull faces at me when I turn my back, wink at each other, keep up your sweet joke. This game, if you play it well, will go down in history. If you had any pity, kindness or manners, you wouldn't make me the butt of your joke like this. But goodbye. It's partly my own fault, and death or going away will soon fix that."},{"t":"sp","who":"LYSANDER","lines":["Stay, gentle Helena; hear my excuse;","My love, my life, my soul, fair Helena!"],"id":339,"m":"Stay, gentle Helena, and hear me explain. My love, my life, my soul, beautiful Helena!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["O excellent!"],"id":340,"m":"Oh, wonderful!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIA","lines":["Sweet, do not scorn her so."],"id":341,"m":"Sweetheart, don't mock her like that."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["If she cannot entreat, I can compel."],"id":342,"m":"If she can't persuade you to stop, I can force you."},{"t":"sp","who":"LYSANDER","lines":["Thou canst compel no more than she entreat;","Thy threats have no more strength than her weak prayers.","Helen, I love thee, by my life I do;","I swear by that which I will lose for thee","To prove him false that says I love thee not."],"id":343,"m":"You can no more force me than she can persuade me. Your threats have no more strength than her weak pleas. Helena, I love you. By my life, I do. I swear by my life, which I'm ready to lose for you, to prove wrong any man who says I don't love you."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["I say I love thee more than he can do."],"id":344,"m":"I say I love you more than he can."},{"t":"sp","who":"LYSANDER","lines":["If thou say so, withdraw, and prove it too."],"id":345,"m":"If you say so, come away and prove it."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["Quick, come."],"id":346,"m":"Quick, come on."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIA","lines":["Lysander, whereto tends all this?"],"id":347,"m":"Lysander, where is all this leading?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LYSANDER","lines":["Away, you Ethiope!"],"id":348,"m":"Get away, you blackamoor!"},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["No, no. He will","Seem to break loose. Take on as you would follow,","But yet come not. You are a tame man, go!"],"id":349,"m":"No, no. He'll pretend to break free. Make a show as if you're going to follow me, but then don't come. You're a coward. Go on!"},{"t":"sp","who":"LYSANDER","lines":["Hang off, thou cat, thou burr! Vile thing, let loose,","Or I will shake thee from me like a serpent."],"id":350,"m":"Let go, you cat, you burr! Vile thing, let go, or I'll shake you off me like a snake."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIA","lines":["Why are you grown so rude? What change is this,","Sweet love?"],"id":351,"m":"Why have you become so rough? What's changed, sweet love?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LYSANDER","lines":["Thy love? Out, tawny Tartar, out!","Out, loathèd medicine! O hated potion, hence!"],"id":352,"m":"Your love? Get away, you dark-skinned Tartar, get away! Get away, disgusting medicine! Hateful poison, get away!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIA","lines":["Do you not jest?"],"id":353,"m":"Are you joking?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["Yes, sooth, and so do you."],"id":354,"m":"Yes, he is, and so are you."},{"t":"sp","who":"LYSANDER","lines":["Demetrius, I will keep my word with thee."],"id":355,"m":"Demetrius, I'll keep my promise to you."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["I would I had your bond; for I perceive","A weak bond holds you; I'll not trust your word."],"id":356,"m":"I wish I had your promise in writing, because I can see a weak bond is holding you. I won't trust your word."},{"t":"sp","who":"LYSANDER","lines":["What, should I hurt her, strike her, kill her dead?","Although I hate her, I'll not harm her so."],"id":357,"m":"What, should I hurt her, hit her, kill her? Although I hate her, I won't harm her like that."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIA","lines":["What, can you do me greater harm than hate?","Hate me? Wherefore? O me! what news, my love?","Am not I Hermia? Are not you Lysander?","I am as fair now as I was erewhile.","Since night you lov'd me; yet since night you left me.","Why then, you left me—O, the gods forbid!—","In earnest, shall I say?"],"id":358,"m":"What, can you do me any greater harm than hating me? Hate me? Why? Oh no! What's happened, my love? Aren't I Hermia? Aren't you Lysander? I'm as beautiful now as I was a while ago. You loved me at nightfall, and yet during the night you left me. So did you leave me (oh, the gods forbid!) in earnest, shall I say?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LYSANDER","lines":["Ay, by my life;","And never did desire to see thee more.","Therefore be out of hope, of question, of doubt;","Be certain, nothing truer; 'tis no jest","That I do hate thee and love Helena."],"id":359,"m":"Yes, I swear on my life, and I never wanted to see you again. So give up hope, give up questions, give up doubt. Be certain, nothing is truer: it's no joke that I hate you and love Helena."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIA","lines":["O me! You juggler! You cankerblossom!","You thief of love! What! have you come by night","And stol'n my love's heart from him?"],"id":360,"m":"Oh no! You trickster! You grub that eats flowers! You thief of love! What, have you come in the night and stolen my love's heart from him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["Fine, i' faith!","Have you no modesty, no maiden shame,","No touch of bashfulness? What, will you tear","Impatient answers from my gentle tongue?","Fie, fie, you counterfeit, you puppet, you!"],"id":361,"m":"That's rich, really! Have you no modesty, no shame, not a trace of shyness? What, are you going to force angry answers out of my gentle mouth? Shame on you, you fake, you puppet, you!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIA","lines":["Puppet! Why so? Ay, that way goes the game.","Now I perceive that she hath made compare","Between our statures; she hath urg'd her height;","And with her personage, her tall personage,","Her height, forsooth, she hath prevail'd with him.","And are you grown so high in his esteem","Because I am so dwarfish and so low?","How low am I, thou painted maypole? Speak,","How low am I? I am not yet so low","But that my nails can reach unto thine eyes."],"id":362,"m":"Puppet? Why puppet? Oh, so that's the game. Now I see she's been comparing our heights. She's made a point of how tall she is, and with her figure, her tall figure, her height, if you please, she has won him over. And have you grown so high in his opinion because I'm so short and dwarfish? How short am I, you painted maypole? Tell me, how short am I? I'm not so short that my nails can't reach your eyes."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["I pray you, though you mock me, gentlemen,","Let her not hurt me. I was never curst;","I have no gift at all in shrewishness;","I am a right maid for my cowardice;","Let her not strike me. You perhaps may think,","Because she is something lower than myself,","That I can match her."],"id":363,"m":"Please, gentlemen, even if you are mocking me, don't let her hurt me. I was never bad-tempered. I have no talent at all for being a shrew. I'm a real girl when it comes to being a coward. Don't let her hit me. You might think, because she's a bit shorter than me, that I can fight her."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIA","lines":["Lower! Hark, again."],"id":364,"m":"Shorter! Listen, she's said it again!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["Good Hermia, do not be so bitter with me.","I evermore did love you, Hermia,","Did ever keep your counsels, never wrong'd you,","Save that, in love unto Demetrius,","I told him of your stealth unto this wood.","He follow'd you; for love I follow'd him;","But he hath chid me hence, and threaten'd me","To strike me, spurn me, nay, to kill me too:","And now, so you will let me quiet go,","To Athens will I bear my folly back,","And follow you no further. Let me go:","You see how simple and how fond I am."],"id":365,"m":"Good Hermia, don't be so bitter with me. I always loved you, Hermia, always kept your secrets, never wronged you, except that, out of love for Demetrius, I told him you were slipping away to this wood. He followed you, and out of love I followed him. But he has scolded me and sent me away, and threatened to hit me, kick me, even to kill me. So now, if you'll let me go quietly, I'll take my foolishness back to Athens and follow you no further. Let me go. You can see how simple and foolish I am."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIA","lines":["Why, get you gone. Who is't that hinders you?"],"id":366,"m":"Well, go then. Who's stopping you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["A foolish heart that I leave here behind."],"id":367,"m":"A foolish heart that I'm leaving behind here."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIA","lines":["What! with Lysander?"],"id":368,"m":"What, with Lysander?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["With Demetrius."],"id":369,"m":"With Demetrius."},{"t":"sp","who":"LYSANDER","lines":["Be not afraid; she shall not harm thee, Helena."],"id":370,"m":"Don't be afraid. She won't hurt you, Helena."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["No, sir, she shall not, though you take her part."],"id":371,"m":"No, sir, she won't, even if you take her side."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["O, when she's angry, she is keen and shrewd.","She was a vixen when she went to school,","And though she be but little, she is fierce."],"id":372,"m":"Oh, when she's angry, she's sharp and vicious. She was a little vixen at school, and though she's only small, she's fierce."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIA","lines":["Little again! Nothing but low and little?","Why will you suffer her to flout me thus?","Let me come to her."],"id":373,"m":"Small again! Nothing but short and small? Why do you let her insult me like this? Let me get at her."},{"t":"sp","who":"LYSANDER","lines":["Get you gone, you dwarf;","You minimus, of hind'ring knot-grass made;","You bead, you acorn."],"id":374,"m":"Get away, you dwarf, you tiny thing, made of knot-grass that stunts growth. You bead, you acorn!"},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["You are too officious","In her behalf that scorns your services.","Let her alone. Speak not of Helena;","Take not her part; for if thou dost intend","Never so little show of love to her,","Thou shalt aby it."],"id":375,"m":"You're too eager to help a woman who scorns your services. Leave her alone. Don't talk about Helena. Don't take her side. Because if you try to show her even the slightest love, you'll pay for it."},{"t":"sp","who":"LYSANDER","lines":["Now she holds me not.","Now follow, if thou dar'st, to try whose right,","Of thine or mine, is most in Helena."],"id":376,"m":"Now she's not holding me. Now follow me, if you dare, to find out whose claim to Helena is stronger, yours or mine."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["Follow! Nay, I'll go with thee, cheek by jole."],"id":377,"m":"Follow you? No, I'll go right beside you, cheek by jowl."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt LYSANDER and DEMETRIUS","id":378},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIA","lines":["You, mistress, all this coil is long of you.","Nay, go not back."],"id":379,"m":"You, madam, all this trouble is because of you. No, don't back away."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["I will not trust you, I,","Nor longer stay in your curst company.","Your hands than mine are quicker for a fray.","My legs are longer though, to run away."],"id":380,"m":"I won't trust you, not me, and I won't stay any longer in your bad-tempered company. Your hands are quicker than mine in a fight, but my legs are longer for running away."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit","id":381},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIA","lines":["I am amaz'd, and know not what to say."],"id":382,"m":"I'm stunned, and I don't know what to say."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit, pursuing HELENA","id":383},{"t":"sp","who":"OBERON","lines":["This is thy negligence: still thou mistak'st,","Or else commit'st thy knaveries willfully."],"id":384,"m":"This is your carelessness. You're always making mistakes, or else you play your tricks on purpose."},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCK","lines":["Believe me, king of shadows, I mistook.","Did not you tell me I should know the man","By the Athenian garments he had on?","And so far blameless proves my enterprise","That I have 'nointed an Athenian's eyes:","And so far am I glad it so did sort,","As this their jangling I esteem a sport."],"id":385,"m":"Believe me, king of shadows, I made a mistake. Didn't you tell me I would recognise the man by the Athenian clothes he was wearing? So far my work has been blameless, because I did put the juice on an Athenian's eyes. And so far I'm glad it turned out this way, because I think their squabbling is great fun."},{"t":"sp","who":"OBERON","lines":["Thou seest these lovers seek a place to fight.","Hie therefore, Robin, overcast the night;","The starry welkin cover thou anon","With drooping fog, as black as Acheron,","And lead these testy rivals so astray","As one come not within another's way.","Like to Lysander sometime frame thy tongue,","Then stir Demetrius up with bitter wrong;","And sometime rail thou like Demetrius.","And from each other look thou lead them thus,","Till o'er their brows death-counterfeiting sleep","With leaden legs and batty wings doth creep.","Then crush this herb into Lysander's eye,","Whose liquor hath this virtuous property,","To take from thence all error with his might","And make his eyeballs roll with wonted sight.","When they next wake, all this derision","Shall seem a dream and fruitless vision;","And back to Athens shall the lovers wend,","With league whose date till death shall never end.","Whiles I in this affair do thee employ,","I'll to my queen, and beg her Indian boy;","And then I will her charmèd eye release","From monster's view, and all things shall be peace."],"id":386,"m":"You can see these lovers are looking for a place to fight. So hurry, Robin, and cloud over the night. Cover the starry sky straight away with a low fog as black as hell's river, and lead these angry rivals so far astray that neither can find the other. Sometimes imitate Lysander's voice and stir Demetrius up with cruel insults, and sometimes rant like Demetrius. Lead them away from each other like this until sleep, which looks like death, creeps over their foreheads with legs as heavy as lead and wings like a bat's. Then squeeze this herb into Lysander's eye. Its juice has the power to take away all his error and make his eyes see as they used to. When they next wake, all this foolishness will seem like a dream, an empty vision, and the lovers will go back to Athens, joined in a bond that will last until death. While I give you this job, I'll go to my queen and ask her for her Indian boy. And then I'll free her bewitched eyes from the sight of that monster, and everything will be peaceful."},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCK","lines":["My fairy lord, this must be done with haste,","For night's swift dragons cut the clouds full fast;","And yonder shines Aurora's harbinger,","At whose approach, ghosts wandering here and there","Troop home to churchyards. Damnèd spirits all,","That in cross-ways and floods have burial,","Already to their wormy beds are gone;","For fear lest day should look their shames upon,","They wilfully themselves exile from light,","And must for aye consort with black-brow'd night."],"id":387,"m":"My fairy lord, this has to be done quickly, because night's swift dragons are cutting through the clouds at full speed, and over there shines the morning star that announces the dawn. When it comes, ghosts wandering here and there flock home to the churchyards. All the damned spirits buried at crossroads or drowned in rivers have already gone to their beds of worms. Afraid that daylight might see their shame, they shut themselves away from the light on purpose, and must keep company for ever with dark-browed night."},{"t":"sp","who":"OBERON","lines":["But we are spirits of another sort:","I with the morning's love have oft made sport;","And, like a forester, the groves may tread","Even till the eastern gate, all fiery-red,","Opening on Neptune with fair blessèd beams,","Turns into yellow gold his salt-green streams.","But, notwithstanding, haste, make no delay.","We may effect this business yet ere day."],"id":388,"m":"But we are a different kind of spirit. I have often played with the lover of the morning, and like a forester I can walk the woods right up until the gates of the east, fiery red, open onto the sea with lovely blessed rays and turn its salty green waves to yellow gold. But all the same, hurry. Don't delay. We can still get this done before daybreak."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit OBERON","id":389},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCK","lines":["Up and down, up and down,","I will lead them up and down.","I am fear'd in field and town.","Goblin, lead them up and down.","Here comes one."],"id":390,"m":"Up and down, up and down, I will lead them up and down. I am feared in country and town. Goblin, lead them up and down. Here comes one."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter LYSANDER","id":391},{"t":"sp","who":"LYSANDER","lines":["Where art thou, proud Demetrius? Speak thou now."],"id":392,"m":"Where are you, proud Demetrius? Speak now."},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCK","lines":["Here, villain, drawn and ready. Where art thou?"],"id":393,"m":"Here, villain, with my sword drawn and ready. Where are you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LYSANDER","lines":["I will be with thee straight."],"id":394,"m":"I'll be with you in a moment."},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCK","lines":["Follow me then to plainer ground."],"id":395,"m":"Follow me, then, to more level ground."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit LYSANDER as following the voice","id":396},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter DEMETRIUS","id":397},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["Lysander, speak again.","Thou runaway, thou coward, art thou fled?","Speak. In some bush? Where dost thou hide thy head?"],"id":398,"m":"Lysander, speak again! You runaway, you coward, have you run off? Speak! In some bush? Where are you hiding your head?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCK","lines":["Thou coward, art thou bragging to the stars,","Telling the bushes that thou look'st for wars,","And wilt not come? Come, recreant, come, thou child!","I'll whip thee with a rod. He is defil'd","That draws a sword on thee."],"id":399,"m":"You coward, are you boasting to the stars, telling the bushes that you're looking for a fight, and won't come? Come on, you traitor, come on, you child! I'll beat you with a stick. It's a disgrace to draw a sword on you."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["Yea, art thou there?"],"id":400,"m":"Yes? Are you there?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCK","lines":["Follow my voice; we'll try no manhood here."],"id":401,"m":"Follow my voice. This is no place to test our courage."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt","id":402},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter LYSANDER","id":403},{"t":"sp","who":"LYSANDER","lines":["He goes before me, and still dares me on;","When I come where he calls, then he is gone.","The villain is much lighter-heel'd than I:","I follow'd fast, but faster he did fly,","That fallen am I in dark uneven way,","And here will rest me. Come, thou gentle day!","[Lies down] For if but once thou show me thy grey light,","I'll find Demetrius, and revenge this spite."],"id":404,"m":"He goes ahead of me and keeps daring me to follow. When I get to where he calls from, he's gone. The villain is much quicker on his feet than I am. I followed fast, but he ran faster, so now I've ended up on dark, rough ground, and I'll rest here. Come, gentle day! [He lies down] As soon as you show me your grey light, I'll find Demetrius and get revenge for this insult."},{"t":"sd","text":"Sleeps","id":405},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter PUCK and DEMETRIUS","id":406},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCK","lines":["Ho, ho, ho! Coward, why com'st thou not?"],"id":407,"m":"Ho, ho, ho! Coward, why don't you come?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["Abide me, if thou dar'st; for well I wot","Thou runn'st before me, shifting every place,","And dar'st not stand, nor look me in the face.","Where art thou?"],"id":408,"m":"Wait for me, if you dare, because I know very well you're running ahead of me, changing places all the time, and you don't dare to stand still or look me in the face. Where are you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCK","lines":["Come hither; I am here."],"id":409,"m":"Come here. I'm here."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["Nay, then, thou mock'st me. Thou shalt buy this dear","If ever I thy face by daylight see:","Now go thy way. Faintness constraineth me","To measure out my length on this cold bed.","By day's approach look to be visited."],"id":410,"m":"No, you're mocking me. You'll pay dearly for this if I ever see your face by daylight. Go your way for now. I'm so tired I have to lie down full length on this cold bed. When day comes, expect a visit from me."},{"t":"sd","text":"Lies down and sleeps","id":411},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter HELENA","id":412},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["O weary night, O long and tedious night,","Abate thy hours! Shine, comforts, from the east,","That I may back to Athens by daylight,","From these that my poor company detest.","And sleep, that sometimes shuts up sorrow's eye,","Steal me awhile from mine own company."],"id":413,"m":"Oh weary night, oh long and boring night, make your hours shorter! Shine, comforting light, from the east, so that I can go back to Athens by daylight, away from these people who hate my poor company. And sleep, which sometimes closes the eyes of sorrow, take me away from myself for a while."},{"t":"sd","text":"Sleeps","id":414},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCK","lines":["Yet but three? Come one more.","Two of both kinds makes up four.","Here she comes, curst and sad.","Cupid is a knavish lad","Thus to make poor females mad."],"id":415,"m":"Only three so far? One more to come. Two of each kind make four. Here she comes, angry and sad. Cupid is a naughty boy to drive poor women mad like this."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter HERMIA","id":416},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIA","lines":["Never so weary, never so in woe,","Bedabbled with the dew, and torn with briers,","I can no further crawl, no further go;","My legs can keep no pace with my desires.","Here will I rest me till the break of day.","Heavens shield Lysander, if they mean a fray!"],"id":417,"m":"I've never been so tired, never so unhappy. I'm soaked with dew and torn by briars. I can't crawl any further, can't walk any further. My legs can't keep up with what I want. I'll rest here till daybreak. Heaven protect Lysander, if they mean to fight!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Lies down","id":418},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCK","lines":["~On the ground","~Sleep sound.","~I'll apply","~To your eye,","~Gentle lover, remedy."],"id":419,"m":"On the ground, sleep soundly. I'll put a cure on your eye, gentle lover."},{"t":"sd","text":"Squeezing the juice on LYSANDER's eye","id":420},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCK","cont":true,"lines":["~When thou wak'st,","~Thou tak'st","~True delight","~In the sight","~Of thy former lady's eye.","~And the country proverb known,","~That every man should take his own,","~In your waking shall be shown:","~Jack shall have Jill;","~Nought shall go ill;","The man shall have his mare again, and all shall be well."],"id":421,"m":"When you wake, you'll take true delight in the sight of your old lady's eyes. And the well-known country saying, that every man should have his own, will be shown when you wake: Jack shall have Jill, nothing will go wrong, the man will get his mare back, and all will be well."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit PUCK","id":422},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"I","loc":"The Wood.","id":423},{"t":"sd","text":"LYSANDER, DEMETRIUS, HELENA and HERMIA still asleep","id":424},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter TITANIA and BOTTOM; PEASEBLOSSOM, COBWEB, MOTH, MUSTARDSEED and other Fairies attending; OBERON behind, unseen","id":425},{"t":"sp","who":"TITANIA","lines":["Come, sit thee down upon this flowery bed,","While I thy amiable cheeks do coy,","And stick musk-roses in thy sleek smooth head,","And kiss thy fair large ears, my gentle joy."],"id":426,"m":"Come, sit down on this bed of flowers, while I stroke your lovely cheeks, and stick musk roses in your sleek, smooth head, and kiss your beautiful big ears, my gentle joy."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOTTOM","lines":["Where's Peaseblossom?"],"id":427,"m":"Where's Peaseblossom?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PEASEBLOSSOM","lines":["Ready."],"id":428,"m":"Here."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOTTOM","lines":["Scratch my head, Peaseblossom. Where's Monsieur Cobweb?"],"id":429,"m":"Scratch my head, Peaseblossom. Where's Monsieur Cobweb?"},{"t":"sp","who":"COBWEB","lines":["Ready."],"id":430,"m":"Here."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOTTOM","lines":["Monsieur Cobweb; good monsieur, get you your weapons in your hand and","kill me a red-hipped humble-bee on the top of a thistle; and, good","monsieur, bring me the honey-bag. Do not fret yourself too much in the","action, monsieur; and, good monsieur, have a care the honey-bag break","not; I would be loath to have you overflown with a honey-bag, signior.","Where's Monsieur Mustardseed?"],"id":431,"m":"Monsieur Cobweb, good monsieur, take your weapons in your hand and kill me a red-bottomed bumblebee on top of a thistle. And, good monsieur, bring me the honey-sac. Don't wear yourself out doing it, monsieur, and, good monsieur, take care the honey-sac doesn't burst. I'd hate to see you drowned in honey, signor. Where's Monsieur Mustardseed?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MUSTARDSEED","lines":["Ready."],"id":432,"m":"Here."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOTTOM","lines":["Give me your neaf, Monsieur Mustardseed. Pray you, leave your courtesy,","good monsieur."],"id":433,"m":"Give me your fist, Monsieur Mustardseed. Please, stop bowing, good monsieur."},{"t":"sp","who":"MUSTARDSEED","lines":["What's your will?"],"id":434,"m":"What would you like?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOTTOM","lines":["Nothing, good monsieur, but to help Cavalery Cobweb to scratch. I must","to the barber's, monsieur, for methinks I am marvellous hairy about the","face; and I am such a tender ass, if my hair do but tickle me, I must","scratch."],"id":435,"m":"Nothing, good monsieur, except to help Cavalier Cobweb with the scratching. I must go to the barber's, monsieur, because I think I'm amazingly hairy about the face. And I'm such a sensitive ass that if my hair tickles me even a little, I have to scratch."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITANIA","lines":["What, wilt thou hear some music, my sweet love?"],"id":436,"m":"Would you like to hear some music, my sweet love?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOTTOM","lines":["I have a reasonable good ear in music. Let us have the tongs and the","bones."],"id":437,"m":"I've got a pretty good ear for music. Let's have the tongs and the bones."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITANIA","lines":["Or say, sweet love, what thou desirest to eat."],"id":438,"m":"Or tell me, sweet love, what you would like to eat."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOTTOM","lines":["Truly, a peck of provender; I could munch your good dry oats. Methinks","I have a great desire to a bottle of hay: good hay, sweet hay, hath no","fellow."],"id":439,"m":"Honestly, a bucket of fodder. I could munch some of your good dry oats. I think I'd really love a bundle of hay. Good hay, sweet hay, there's nothing like it."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITANIA","lines":["I have a venturous fairy that shall seek","The squirrel's hoard, and fetch thee new nuts."],"id":440,"m":"I have an adventurous fairy who will find the squirrel's store and bring you fresh nuts."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOTTOM","lines":["I had rather have a handful or two of dried peas. But, I pray you, let","none of your people stir me; I have an exposition of sleep come upon","me."],"id":441,"m":"I'd rather have a handful or two of dried peas. But please don't let any of your people disturb me. I feel an 'exposition' of sleep coming over me."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITANIA","lines":["Sleep thou, and I will wind thee in my arms.","Fairies, be gone, and be all ways away.","So doth the woodbine the sweet honeysuckle","Gently entwist, the female ivy so","Enrings the barky fingers of the elm.","O, how I love thee! How I dote on thee!"],"id":442,"m":"Sleep, and I'll wrap you in my arms. Fairies, go away, go in every direction. This is how the bindweed gently twines round the sweet honeysuckle, and how the clinging ivy wraps round the bark-covered fingers of the elm. Oh, how I love you! How I adore you!"},{"t":"sd","text":"They sleep","id":443},{"t":"sd","text":"OBERON advances. Enter PUCK","id":444},{"t":"sp","who":"OBERON","lines":["Welcome, good Robin. Seest thou this sweet sight?","Her dotage now I do begin to pity.","For, meeting her of late behind the wood,","Seeking sweet favours for this hateful fool,","I did upbraid her and fall out with her:","For she his hairy temples then had rounded","With coronet of fresh and fragrant flowers;","And that same dew, which sometime on the buds","Was wont to swell like round and orient pearls,","Stood now within the pretty flouriets' eyes,","Like tears that did their own disgrace bewail.","When I had at my pleasure taunted her,","And she in mild terms begg'd my patience,","I then did ask of her her changeling child;","Which straight she gave me, and her fairy sent","To bear him to my bower in fairyland.","And now I have the boy, I will undo","This hateful imperfection of her eyes.","And, gentle Puck, take this transformèd scalp","From off the head of this Athenian swain,","That he awaking when the other do,","May all to Athens back again repair,","And think no more of this night's accidents","But as the fierce vexation of a dream.","But first I will release the Fairy Queen."],"id":445,"m":"Welcome, good Robin. Can you see this sweet sight? I'm beginning to pity her foolish love now. Because when I met her just now behind the wood, looking for sweet presents for this hateful fool, I told her off and quarrelled with her. She had put a crown of fresh, sweet-smelling flowers round his hairy head, and the dew that used to swell on the buds like round, shining pearls was sitting in the eyes of the pretty little flowers like tears crying over their own disgrace. When I had teased her as much as I liked, and she had gently begged me to be patient, I asked her for the stolen child. She gave him to me straight away, and sent her fairy to carry him to my bower in fairyland. And now that I have the boy, I'll undo this hateful fault in her eyes. And, gentle Puck, take this transformed head off this Athenian peasant, so that when he wakes up with the others, they can all go back to Athens and think of this night's events as nothing more than the wild torment of a dream. But first I'll free the Fairy Queen."},{"t":"sd","text":"Touching her eyes with an herb","id":446},{"t":"sp","who":"OBERON","cont":true,"lines":["~Be as thou wast wont to be;","~See as thou was wont to see.","~Dian's bud o'er Cupid's flower","~Hath such force and blessed power.","Now, my Titania, wake you, my sweet queen."],"id":447,"m":"Be as you used to be. See as you used to see. Diana's flower has such strength and blessed power over Cupid's flower. Now, my Titania, wake up, my sweet queen."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITANIA","lines":["My Oberon, what visions have I seen!","Methought I was enamour'd of an ass."],"id":448,"m":"My Oberon, what visions I've seen! I dreamed I was in love with an ass."},{"t":"sp","who":"OBERON","lines":["There lies your love."],"id":449,"m":"There lies your love."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITANIA","lines":["How came these things to pass?","O, how mine eyes do loathe his visage now!"],"id":450,"m":"How did this happen? Oh, how my eyes hate the sight of his face now!"},{"t":"sp","who":"OBERON","lines":["Silence awhile.—Robin, take off this head.","Titania, music call; and strike more dead","Than common sleep, of all these five the sense."],"id":451,"m":"Be quiet for a while. Robin, take off this head. Titania, call for music, and make these five sleep more deeply than ordinary sleep."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITANIA","lines":["Music, ho, music, such as charmeth sleep."],"id":452,"m":"Music, come, music, the kind that brings sleep!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCK","lines":["Now when thou wak'st, with thine own fool's eyes peep."],"id":453,"m":"Now, when you wake, see with your own fool's eyes."},{"t":"sp","who":"OBERON","lines":["Sound, music."],"id":454,"m":"Play, music!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Still music","id":455},{"t":"sp","who":"OBERON","cont":true,"lines":["Come, my queen, take hands with me,","And rock the ground whereon these sleepers be.","Now thou and I are new in amity,","And will tomorrow midnight solemnly","Dance in Duke Theseus' house triumphantly,","And bless it to all fair prosperity:","There shall the pairs of faithful lovers be","Wedded, with Theseus, all in jollity."],"id":456,"m":"Come, my queen, take my hand, and let's dance and rock the ground where these sleepers lie. Now you and I are friends again, and tomorrow at midnight we will dance in Duke Theseus's house in celebration, and bless it with good fortune. There the two pairs of faithful lovers will be married, at the same time as Theseus, all in happiness."},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCK","lines":["Fairy king, attend and mark.","I do hear the morning lark."],"id":457,"m":"Fairy king, listen and pay attention. I can hear the morning lark."},{"t":"sp","who":"OBERON","lines":["Then, my queen, in silence sad,","Trip we after night's shade.","We the globe can compass soon,","Swifter than the wand'ring moon."],"id":458,"m":"Then, my queen, quietly and seriously, let's follow the night's shadow. We can go round the world quickly, faster than the wandering moon."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITANIA","lines":["Come, my lord, and in our flight,","Tell me how it came this night","That I sleeping here was found","With these mortals on the ground."],"id":459,"m":"Come, my lord, and as we fly, tell me how it happened that tonight I was found sleeping here on the ground with these mortals."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt. Horns sound within","id":460},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter THESEUS, HIPPOLYTA, EGEUS and Train","id":461},{"t":"sp","who":"THESEUS","lines":["Go, one of you, find out the forester;","For now our observation is perform'd;","And since we have the vaward of the day,","My love shall hear the music of my hounds.","Uncouple in the western valley; let them go.","Dispatch I say, and find the forester."],"id":462,"m":"One of you, go and find the forester. Now we have finished our May Day ceremonies, and since it is still early in the day, my love will hear the music of my hounds. Let them loose in the western valley. Let them go. Hurry, I say, and find the forester."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit an Attendant","id":463},{"t":"sp","who":"THESEUS","cont":true,"lines":["We will, fair queen, up to the mountain's top,","And mark the musical confusion","Of hounds and echo in conjunction."],"id":464,"m":"Fair queen, we'll go up to the top of the mountain and listen to the musical confusion of the hounds and their echoes together."},{"t":"sp","who":"HIPPOLYTA","lines":["I was with Hercules and Cadmus once,","When in a wood of Crete they bay'd the bear","With hounds of Sparta. Never did I hear","Such gallant chiding; for, besides the groves,","The skies, the fountains, every region near","Seem'd all one mutual cry. I never heard","So musical a discord, such sweet thunder."],"id":465,"m":"I was with Hercules and Cadmus once, when they hunted a bear in a wood in Crete with Spartan hounds. I never heard such splendid barking, because the woods, the skies, the springs, the whole area round about seemed to join in one cry. I never heard such musical discord, such sweet thunder."},{"t":"sp","who":"THESEUS","lines":["My hounds are bred out of the Spartan kind,","So flew'd, so sanded; and their heads are hung","With ears that sweep away the morning dew;","Crook-knee'd and dewlap'd like Thessalian bulls;","Slow in pursuit, but match'd in mouth like bells,","Each under each. A cry more tuneable","Was never holla'd to, nor cheer'd with horn,","In Crete, in Sparta, nor in Thessaly.","Judge when you hear.—But, soft, what nymphs are these?"],"id":466,"m":"My hounds are bred from the Spartan kind: they have the same hanging jowls and sandy colour, and their heads are hung with ears that sweep the morning dew. They are bow-legged and have folds of skin at the throat like the bulls of Thessaly. They are slow in the chase, but their voices are matched like bells, each one a note lower than the next. A more tuneful pack was never called to or cheered on with a horn in Crete, in Sparta or in Thessaly. Judge for yourself when you hear them. But wait, who are these nymphs?"},{"t":"sp","who":"EGEUS","lines":["My lord, this is my daughter here asleep,","And this Lysander; this Demetrius is;","This Helena, old Nedar's Helena:","I wonder of their being here together."],"id":467,"m":"My lord, this is my daughter here, asleep, and this is Lysander. This is Demetrius, and this is Helena, old Nedar's Helena. I'm amazed to find them here together."},{"t":"sp","who":"THESEUS","lines":["No doubt they rose up early to observe","The rite of May; and, hearing our intent,","Came here in grace of our solemnity.","But speak, Egeus; is not this the day","That Hermia should give answer of her choice?"],"id":468,"m":"No doubt they got up early to celebrate May Day and, hearing what we planned, came here to honour our celebrations. But tell me, Egeus, isn't this the day when Hermia has to give her answer about her choice?"},{"t":"sp","who":"EGEUS","lines":["It is, my lord."],"id":469,"m":"It is, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"THESEUS","lines":["Go, bid the huntsmen wake them with their horns."],"id":470,"m":"Go and tell the huntsmen to wake them with their horns."},{"t":"sd","text":"Horns, and shout within. DEMETRIUS, LYSANDER, HERMIA and HELENA wake and start up","id":471},{"t":"sp","who":"THESEUS","cont":true,"lines":["Good morrow, friends. Saint Valentine is past.","Begin these wood-birds but to couple now?"],"id":472,"m":"Good morning, friends. Saint Valentine's Day is long gone. Are these woodland birds only now beginning to pair up?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LYSANDER","lines":["Pardon, my lord."],"id":473,"m":"Forgive us, my lord."},{"t":"sd","text":"He and the rest kneel to THESEUS","id":474},{"t":"sp","who":"THESEUS","lines":["I pray you all, stand up.","I know you two are rival enemies.","How comes this gentle concord in the world,","That hatred is so far from jealousy","To sleep by hate, and fear no enmity?"],"id":475,"m":"Please, all of you, stand up. I know you two are rivals and enemies. How has such gentle harmony come into the world that hatred is so far from suspicion that it sleeps next to its enemy without fear?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LYSANDER","lines":["My lord, I shall reply amazedly,","Half sleep, half waking; but as yet, I swear,","I cannot truly say how I came here.","But, as I think (for truly would I speak)","And now I do bethink me, so it is:","I came with Hermia hither. Our intent","Was to be gone from Athens, where we might be","Without the peril of the Athenian law."],"id":476,"m":"My lord, I'll answer you in confusion, half asleep, half awake. But I swear I can't really say how I got here. But I think (because I want to tell the truth), and now that I think about it, this is how it was: I came here with Hermia. We planned to leave Athens and go where we would be safe from the Athenian law."},{"t":"sp","who":"EGEUS","lines":["Enough, enough, my lord; you have enough.","I beg the law, the law upon his head.","They would have stol'n away, they would, Demetrius,","Thereby to have defeated you and me:","You of your wife, and me of my consent,","Of my consent that she should be your wife."],"id":477,"m":"Enough, enough, my lord, you've heard enough. I ask for the law, the law on his head! They meant to run away, they did, Demetrius, and so cheat both you and me: cheat you of your wife, and me of my consent, my consent that she should be your wife."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["My lord, fair Helen told me of their stealth,","Of this their purpose hither to this wood;","And I in fury hither follow'd them,","Fair Helena in fancy following me.","But, my good lord, I wot not by what power,","(But by some power it is) my love to Hermia,","Melted as the snow, seems to me now","As the remembrance of an idle gaud","Which in my childhood I did dote upon;","And all the faith, the virtue of my heart,","The object and the pleasure of mine eye,","Is only Helena. To her, my lord,","Was I betroth'd ere I saw Hermia.","But like a sickness did I loathe this food.","But, as in health, come to my natural taste,","Now I do wish it, love it, long for it,","And will for evermore be true to it."],"id":478,"m":"My lord, beautiful Helena told me about their escape, about their plan to come here to this wood, and I followed them here in a rage, with beautiful Helena following me out of love. But, my good lord, I don't know by what power (though it must be some power) my love for Hermia has melted like snow, and now seems like the memory of some worthless toy I was obsessed with when I was a child. All the faithfulness and strength of my heart, the object and the delight of my eyes, is Helena alone. I was engaged to her, my lord, before I saw Hermia. But, like someone who is ill, I hated this food. Now, as if I were well again, my natural taste has come back. Now I want it, love it, long for it, and I will be true to it for ever."},{"t":"sp","who":"THESEUS","lines":["Fair lovers, you are fortunately met.","Of this discourse we more will hear anon.","Egeus, I will overbear your will;","For in the temple, by and by with us,","These couples shall eternally be knit.","And, for the morning now is something worn,","Our purpos'd hunting shall be set aside.","Away with us to Athens. Three and three,","We'll hold a feast in great solemnity.","Come, Hippolyta."],"id":479,"m":"Beautiful lovers, this is a lucky meeting. We'll hear more of this story later. Egeus, I will overrule you, because in the temple, very soon, these couples will be joined for ever together with us. And since the morning is now half gone, we'll put off our planned hunt. Come with us to Athens. Three couples together, we'll hold a grand feast. Come, Hippolyta."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt THESEUS, HIPPOLYTA, EGEUS and Train","id":480},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["These things seem small and undistinguishable,","Like far-off mountains turnèd into clouds."],"id":481,"m":"These things seem small and hard to make out, like distant mountains that have turned into clouds."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIA","lines":["Methinks I see these things with parted eye,","When everything seems double."],"id":482,"m":"I feel as if I'm seeing these things with my eyes out of focus, when everything looks double."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["So methinks.","And I have found Demetrius like a jewel,","Mine own, and not mine own."],"id":483,"m":"So do I. And I have found Demetrius like a jewel someone has found: mine, and yet not really mine."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["Are you sure","That we are awake? It seems to me","That yet we sleep, we dream. Do not you think","The Duke was here, and bid us follow him?"],"id":484,"m":"Are you sure we're awake? It seems to me we're still asleep and dreaming. Do you think the Duke was here and told us to follow him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIA","lines":["Yea, and my father."],"id":485,"m":"Yes, and my father."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["And Hippolyta."],"id":486,"m":"And Hippolyta."},{"t":"sp","who":"LYSANDER","lines":["And he did bid us follow to the temple."],"id":487,"m":"And he told us to follow him to the temple."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["Why, then, we are awake: let's follow him,","And by the way let us recount our dreams."],"id":488,"m":"Well then, we're awake. Let's follow him, and on the way let's tell each other our dreams."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt","id":489},{"t":"sp","who":"BOTTOM","lines":["[Waking] When my cue comes, call me, and I will answer. My next is","'Most fair Pyramus.' Heigh-ho! Peter Quince! Flute, the bellows-mender!","Snout, the tinker! Starveling! God's my life! Stol'n hence, and left me","asleep! I have had a most rare vision. I have had a dream, past the wit","of man to say what dream it was. Man is but an ass if he go about to","expound this dream. Methought I was—there is no man can tell what.","Methought I was, and methought I had—but man is but a patched fool if","he will offer to say what methought I had. The eye of man hath not","heard, the ear of man hath not seen, man's hand is not able to taste,","his tongue to conceive, nor his heart to report, what my dream was. I","will get Peter Quince to write a ballad of this dream: it shall be","called 'Bottom's Dream', because it hath no bottom; and I will sing it","in the latter end of a play, before the Duke. Peradventure, to make it","the more gracious, I shall sing it at her death."],"id":490,"m":"When my cue comes, call me and I'll answer. My next line is 'Most fair Pyramus.' Hey there! Peter Quince! Flute, the bellows-mender! Snout, the tinker! Starveling! God help me! They've sneaked off and left me asleep! I've had a most amazing vision. I've had a dream that no man has the brains to explain. A man would be an ass if he tried to explain this dream. I thought I was... no one can say what. I thought I was, and I thought I had... but a man would be a patched-up fool if he tried to say what I thought I had. The eye of man has never heard, the ear of man has never seen, man's hand can't taste, his tongue can't imagine, and his heart can't tell what my dream was. I'll get Peter Quince to write a ballad about this dream. It will be called 'Bottom's Dream', because it has no bottom. And I'll sing it at the end of a play, in front of the Duke. Perhaps, to make it even better, I'll sing it when Thisbe dies."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit","id":491},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"II","loc":"Athens. A Room in Quince's House.","id":492},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter QUINCE, FLUTE, SNOUT and STARVELING","id":493},{"t":"sp","who":"QUINCE","lines":["Have you sent to Bottom's house? Is he come home yet?"],"id":494,"m":"Have you sent someone to Bottom's house? Has he come home yet?"},{"t":"sp","who":"STARVELING","lines":["He cannot be heard of. Out of doubt he is transported."],"id":495,"m":"No one's heard anything of him. No doubt he's been carried off."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUTE","lines":["If he come not, then the play is marred. It goes not forward, doth it?"],"id":496,"m":"If he doesn't come, the play's ruined. It can't go on, can it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"QUINCE","lines":["It is not possible. You have not a man in all Athens able to discharge","Pyramus but he."],"id":497,"m":"Impossible. There's no man in all Athens who could play Pyramus except him."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUTE","lines":["No, he hath simply the best wit of any handicraft man in Athens."],"id":498,"m":"No, he's simply got the best brains of any working man in Athens."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUINCE","lines":["Yea, and the best person too, and he is a very paramour for a sweet","voice."],"id":499,"m":"Yes, and he's the best-looking too, and he's a real lover-boy when it comes to a sweet voice."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUTE","lines":["You must say paragon. A paramour is, God bless us, a thing of naught."],"id":500,"m":"You mean a 'paragon'. A 'paramour' is, God help us, a wicked thing."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter SNUG","id":501},{"t":"sp","who":"SNUG","lines":["Masters, the Duke is coming from the temple, and there is two or three","lords and ladies more married. If our sport had gone forward, we had","all been made men."],"id":502,"m":"Gentlemen, the Duke is coming from the temple, and two or three more lords and ladies have been married too. If our play had gone ahead, our fortunes would all have been made."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLUTE","lines":["O sweet bully Bottom! Thus hath he lost sixpence a day during his life;","he could not have 'scaped sixpence a day. An the Duke had not given him","sixpence a day for playing Pyramus, I'll be hanged. He would have","deserved it: sixpence a day in Pyramus, or nothing."],"id":503,"m":"Oh, sweet, dear Bottom! That way he's lost a pension of sixpence a day for life. He couldn't have missed getting sixpence a day. If the Duke hadn't given him sixpence a day for playing Pyramus, I'll be hanged. He'd have deserved it: sixpence a day for Pyramus, or nothing."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter BOTTOM","id":504},{"t":"sp","who":"BOTTOM","lines":["Where are these lads? Where are these hearts?"],"id":505,"m":"Where are the lads? Where are my hearties?"},{"t":"sp","who":"QUINCE","lines":["Bottom! O most courageous day! O most happy hour!"],"id":506,"m":"Bottom! Oh, most wonderful day! Oh, happiest hour!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOTTOM","lines":["Masters, I am to discourse wonders: but ask me not what; for if I tell","you, I am not true Athenian. I will tell you everything, right as it","fell out."],"id":507,"m":"Gentlemen, I have wonders to tell you, but don't ask me what, because if I tell you, I'm no true Athenian. I'll tell you everything, exactly as it happened."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUINCE","lines":["Let us hear, sweet Bottom."],"id":508,"m":"Let's hear it, sweet Bottom."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOTTOM","lines":["Not a word of me. All that I will tell you is, that the Duke hath","dined. Get your apparel together, good strings to your beards, new","ribbons to your pumps; meet presently at the palace; every man look","o'er his part. For the short and the long is, our play is preferred. In","any case, let Thisbe have clean linen; and let not him that plays the","lion pare his nails, for they shall hang out for the lion's claws. And","most dear actors, eat no onions nor garlick, for we are to utter sweet","breath; and I do not doubt but to hear them say it is a sweet comedy.","No more words. Away! Go, away!"],"id":509,"m":"Not a word from me. All I'll tell you is that the Duke has had his dinner. Get your costumes together, good strings for your beards, new ribbons for your shoes. Meet at the palace straight away. Every man, go over his part. Because the long and the short of it is, our play has been shortlisted. Anyway, make sure Thisbe has clean underclothes, and don't let the man playing the lion cut his nails, because they'll stick out as the lion's claws. And, dearest actors, don't eat onions or garlic, because we have to breathe sweet breath, and I'm sure we'll hear them say it's a sweet comedy. No more talking. Off you go! Go, off you go!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt","id":510},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"I","loc":"Athens. An Apartment in the Palace of Theseus.","id":511},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter THESEUS, HIPPOLYTA, PHILOSTRATE, Lords and Attendants","id":512},{"t":"sp","who":"HIPPOLYTA","lines":["'Tis strange, my Theseus, that these lovers speak of."],"id":513,"m":"These things the lovers are talking about are strange, my Theseus."},{"t":"sp","who":"THESEUS","lines":["More strange than true. I never may believe","These antique fables, nor these fairy toys.","Lovers and madmen have such seething brains,","Such shaping fantasies, that apprehend","More than cool reason ever comprehends.","The lunatic, the lover, and the poet","Are of imagination all compact:","One sees more devils than vast hell can hold;","That is the madman: the lover, all as frantic,","Sees Helen's beauty in a brow of Egypt:","The poet's eye, in a fine frenzy rolling,","Doth glance from heaven to earth, from earth to heaven;","And as imagination bodies forth","The forms of things unknown, the poet's pen","Turns them to shapes, and gives to airy nothing","A local habitation and a name.","Such tricks hath strong imagination,","That if it would but apprehend some joy,","It comprehends some bringer of that joy.","Or in the night, imagining some fear,","How easy is a bush supposed a bear?"],"id":514,"m":"More strange than true. I can never believe these old legends or these silly fairy stories. Lovers and madmen have such boiling brains, such inventive imaginations, that they see more than calm reason can ever understand. The madman, the lover and the poet are all made entirely of imagination. One sees more devils than huge hell could hold: that's the madman. The lover, just as crazy, sees the beauty of Helen of Troy in a gypsy's face. The poet's eye, rolling in a fine frenzy, glances from heaven to earth and from earth to heaven, and as imagination gives body to things nobody knows, the poet's pen turns them into shapes and gives airy nothing a home and a name. Strong imagination plays such tricks that if it only feels some joy, it invents someone who brought that joy. Or at night, imagining something frightening, how easily a bush is taken for a bear!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HIPPOLYTA","lines":["But all the story of the night told over,","And all their minds transfigur'd so together,","More witnesseth than fancy's images,","And grows to something of great constancy;","But, howsoever, strange and admirable."],"id":515,"m":"But the whole story of the night, as they tell it, and the way all their minds were changed together, proves it is more than imagination's pictures, and adds up to something very consistent. But, however it happened, it is strange and wonderful."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter lovers: LYSANDER, DEMETRIUS, HERMIA and HELENA","id":516},{"t":"sp","who":"THESEUS","lines":["Here come the lovers, full of joy and mirth.","Joy, gentle friends, joy and fresh days of love","Accompany your hearts!"],"id":517,"m":"Here come the lovers, full of joy and happiness. Joy, gentle friends, joy and fresh days of love be with you!"},{"t":"sp","who":"LYSANDER","lines":["More than to us","Wait in your royal walks, your board, your bed!"],"id":518,"m":"May even more joy than ours be with you on your royal walks, at your table and in your bed!"},{"t":"sp","who":"THESEUS","lines":["Come now; what masques, what dances shall we have,","To wear away this long age of three hours","Between our after-supper and bed-time?","Where is our usual manager of mirth?","What revels are in hand? Is there no play","To ease the anguish of a torturing hour?","Call Philostrate."],"id":519,"m":"Come on now, what masques and dances shall we have to pass this endless age of three hours between after-supper and bedtime? Where is our usual master of entertainments? What shows are ready? Isn't there a play to ease the agony of a torturing hour? Call Philostrate."},{"t":"sp","who":"PHILOSTRATE","lines":["Here, mighty Theseus."],"id":520,"m":"Here, mighty Theseus."},{"t":"sp","who":"THESEUS","lines":["Say, what abridgment have you for this evening?","What masque? What music? How shall we beguile","The lazy time, if not with some delight?"],"id":521,"m":"Tell me, what entertainment have you got to shorten this evening? What masque? What music? How shall we fool the lazy hours, if not with something enjoyable?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PHILOSTRATE","lines":["There is a brief how many sports are ripe.","Make choice of which your Highness will see first."],"id":522,"m":"Here is a list of the entertainments that are ready. Choose which your Highness wants to see first."},{"t":"sd","text":"Giving a paper","id":523},{"t":"sp","who":"THESEUS","lines":["[Reads] 'The battle with the Centaurs, to be sung","By an Athenian eunuch to the harp.'","We'll none of that. That have I told my love","In glory of my kinsman Hercules.","'The riot of the tipsy Bacchanals,","Tearing the Thracian singer in their rage?'","That is an old device, and it was play'd","When I from Thebes came last a conqueror.","'The thrice three Muses mourning for the death","Of learning, late deceas'd in beggary.'","That is some satire, keen and critical,","Not sorting with a nuptial ceremony.","'A tedious brief scene of young Pyramus","And his love Thisbe; very tragical mirth.'","Merry and tragical? Tedious and brief?","That is hot ice and wondrous strange snow.","How shall we find the concord of this discord?"],"id":524,"m":"[He reads] 'The battle with the Centaurs, to be sung by an Athenian eunuch to the harp.' We won't have that. I've already told my love that story, in honour of my relative Hercules. 'The riot of the drunken followers of Bacchus, tearing Orpheus, the singer of Thrace, to pieces in their frenzy.' That's an old show. It was performed the last time I came back from Thebes as a conqueror. 'The nine Muses mourning the death of Learning, who recently died in poverty.' That's some satire, sharp and critical, not suitable for a wedding. 'A boring short scene of young Pyramus and his love Thisbe, very tragic comedy.' Comic and tragic? Boring and short? That's like hot ice and amazingly strange snow. How can we find harmony in this contradiction?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PHILOSTRATE","lines":["A play there is, my lord, some ten words long,","Which is as brief as I have known a play;","But by ten words, my lord, it is too long,","Which makes it tedious. For in all the play","There is not one word apt, one player fitted.","And tragical, my noble lord, it is.","For Pyramus therein doth kill himself,","Which, when I saw rehears'd, I must confess,","Made mine eyes water; but more merry tears","The passion of loud laughter never shed."],"id":525,"m":"There is a play, my lord, about ten words long, which is as short as any play I've known. But it is ten words too long, my lord, which makes it boring, because in the whole play there isn't one word that's right, or one actor suited to his part. And it is tragic, my noble lord, because Pyramus kills himself in it. When I saw it rehearsed, I must admit, it made my eyes water, but no one ever cried happier tears from laughing out loud."},{"t":"sp","who":"THESEUS","lines":["What are they that do play it?"],"id":526,"m":"Who are the actors?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PHILOSTRATE","lines":["Hard-handed men that work in Athens here,","Which never labour'd in their minds till now;","And now have toil'd their unbreath'd memories","With this same play against your nuptial."],"id":527,"m":"Working men with rough hands, who work here in Athens and have never worked with their brains until now. And now they have strained their untrained memories learning this play for your wedding."},{"t":"sp","who":"THESEUS","lines":["And we will hear it."],"id":528,"m":"Then we'll see it."},{"t":"sp","who":"PHILOSTRATE","lines":["No, my noble lord,","It is not for you: I have heard it over,","And it is nothing, nothing in the world;","Unless you can find sport in their intents,","Extremely stretch'd and conn'd with cruel pain","To do you service."],"id":529,"m":"No, my noble lord. It isn't good enough for you. I have heard it all the way through, and it is nothing, nothing at all, unless you can find amusement in their efforts, stretched to the limit and learned with terrible pain, to serve you."},{"t":"sp","who":"THESEUS","lines":["I will hear that play;","For never anything can be amiss","When simpleness and duty tender it.","Go, bring them in: and take your places, ladies."],"id":530,"m":"I will see that play, because nothing can ever be wrong when it is offered with simple honesty and a sense of duty. Go, bring them in. And take your seats, ladies."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit PHILOSTRATE","id":531},{"t":"sp","who":"HIPPOLYTA","lines":["I love not to see wretchedness o'ercharged,","And duty in his service perishing."],"id":532,"m":"I don't like to see poor people overburdened, or their dutiful efforts falling flat."},{"t":"sp","who":"THESEUS","lines":["Why, gentle sweet, you shall see no such thing."],"id":533,"m":"Why, gentle sweetheart, you won't see anything like that."},{"t":"sp","who":"HIPPOLYTA","lines":["He says they can do nothing in this kind."],"id":534,"m":"He says they can't act at all."},{"t":"sp","who":"THESEUS","lines":["The kinder we, to give them thanks for nothing.","Our sport shall be to take what they mistake:","And what poor duty cannot do, noble respect","Takes it in might, not merit.","Where I have come, great clerks have purposed","To greet me with premeditated welcomes;","Where I have seen them shiver and look pale,","Make periods in the midst of sentences,","Throttle their practis'd accent in their fears,","And, in conclusion, dumbly have broke off,","Not paying me a welcome. Trust me, sweet,","Out of this silence yet I pick'd a welcome;","And in the modesty of fearful duty","I read as much as from the rattling tongue","Of saucy and audacious eloquence.","Love, therefore, and tongue-tied simplicity","In least speak most to my capacity."],"id":535,"m":"Then we are all the kinder to thank them for nothing. Our fun will be to accept their mistakes kindly. When poor, dutiful people can't do something well, a noble mind values the effort, not the result. Wherever I have been, great scholars have planned to greet me with prepared speeches of welcome. I've seen them tremble and go pale, stop in the middle of sentences, choke on their rehearsed words in fear, and in the end break off speechless, without welcoming me at all. Believe me, sweetheart, I still found a welcome in their silence, and in the modesty of their nervous duty I read as much as in the rattling tongue of cheeky, confident eloquence. So love and tongue-tied simplicity, saying least, say most to me."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter PHILOSTRATE","id":536},{"t":"sp","who":"PHILOSTRATE","lines":["So please your grace, the Prologue is address'd."],"id":537,"m":"If it please your Grace, the Prologue is ready."},{"t":"sp","who":"THESEUS","lines":["Let him approach."],"id":538,"m":"Let him come forward."},{"t":"sd","text":"Flourish of trumpets. Enter the PROLOGUE","id":539},{"t":"sp","who":"PROLOGUE","lines":["If we offend, it is with our good will.","That you should think, we come not to offend,","But with good will. To show our simple skill,","That is the true beginning of our end.","Consider then, we come but in despite.","We do not come, as minding to content you,","Our true intent is. All for your delight","We are not here. That you should here repent you,","The actors are at hand, and, by their show,","You shall know all that you are like to know."],"id":540,"m":"If we offend, it is with our good will. That you should think we come not to offend, but with good will. To show our simple skill, that is the true beginning of our end. Think, then: we come only to spite you. We do not come intending to please you; that is our real aim. We are not here for your delight. That you should be sorry you came, the actors are ready, and by their show you'll learn all you are likely to learn. [Quince stops in all the wrong places, so every sentence says the opposite of what he means: that they come with good will, not to offend, and are here only to please.]"},{"t":"sp","who":"THESEUS","lines":["This fellow doth not stand upon points."],"id":541,"m":"This man doesn't pay attention to his full stops."},{"t":"sp","who":"LYSANDER","lines":["He hath rid his prologue like a rough colt; he knows not the stop. A","good moral, my lord: it is not enough to speak, but to speak true."],"id":542,"m":"He's ridden his prologue like a wild young horse: he doesn't know how to stop. That's a good lesson, my lord: it isn't enough to speak, you have to speak correctly."},{"t":"sp","who":"HIPPOLYTA","lines":["Indeed he hath played on this prologue like a child on a recorder; a","sound, but not in government."],"id":543,"m":"Yes, he's played this prologue like a child on a recorder: there's a sound, but no control."},{"t":"sp","who":"THESEUS","lines":["His speech was like a tangled chain; nothing impaired, but all","disordered. Who is next?"],"id":544,"m":"His speech was like a tangled chain: no part broken, but all in a mess. Who's next?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter PYRAMUS and THISBE, WALL, MOONSHINE and LION as in dumb show","id":545},{"t":"sp","who":"PROLOGUE","lines":["Gentles, perchance you wonder at this show;","But wonder on, till truth make all things plain.","This man is Pyramus, if you would know;","This beauteous lady Thisbe is certain.","This man, with lime and rough-cast, doth present","Wall, that vile wall which did these lovers sunder;","And through Wall's chink, poor souls, they are content","To whisper, at the which let no man wonder.","This man, with lantern, dog, and bush of thorn,","Presenteth Moonshine, for, if you will know,","By moonshine did these lovers think no scorn","To meet at Ninus' tomb, there, there to woo.","This grisly beast (which Lion hight by name)","The trusty Thisbe, coming first by night,","Did scare away, or rather did affright;","And as she fled, her mantle she did fall;","Which Lion vile with bloody mouth did stain.","Anon comes Pyramus, sweet youth, and tall,","And finds his trusty Thisbe's mantle slain;","Whereat with blade, with bloody blameful blade,","He bravely broach'd his boiling bloody breast;","And Thisbe, tarrying in mulberry shade,","His dagger drew, and died. For all the rest,","Let Lion, Moonshine, Wall, and lovers twain,","At large discourse while here they do remain."],"id":546,"m":"Ladies and gentlemen, maybe you're puzzled by this show, but keep wondering until the truth makes everything clear. This man is Pyramus, if you want to know. This beautiful lady is Thisbe, of course. This man, with plaster and gravel, represents Wall, that horrible wall which kept these lovers apart, and they are glad, poor things, to whisper through Wall's crack, which no one should be surprised at. This man, with a lantern, a dog and a bundle of thorn twigs, represents Moonshine, because, if you want to know, these lovers were not ashamed to meet by moonlight at Ninus's tomb, there, there to court each other. This horrible beast, who goes by the name of Lion, scared away, or rather frightened, faithful Thisbe when she arrived first at night. And as she ran away she dropped her cloak, which vile Lion stained with his bloody mouth. Soon Pyramus arrives, a sweet, tall young man, and finds his faithful Thisbe's cloak 'killed'. At this, with his blade, his bloody, blameworthy blade, he bravely broached his boiling, bloody breast. And Thisbe, waiting in the shade of the mulberry tree, drew his dagger and died. For all the rest, let Lion, Moonshine, Wall and the two lovers tell you everything while they're here."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt PROLOGUE, PYRAMUS, THISBE, LION and MOONSHINE","id":547},{"t":"sp","who":"THESEUS","lines":["I wonder if the lion be to speak."],"id":548,"m":"I wonder whether the lion will speak."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["No wonder, my lord. One lion may, when many asses do."],"id":549,"m":"No wonder, my lord. One lion can speak when so many asses do."},{"t":"sp","who":"WALL","lines":["In this same interlude it doth befall","That I, one Snout by name, present a wall:","And such a wall as I would have you think","That had in it a crannied hole or chink,","Through which the lovers, Pyramus and Thisbe,","Did whisper often very secretly.","This loam, this rough-cast, and this stone, doth show","That I am that same wall; the truth is so:","And this the cranny is, right and sinister,","Through which the fearful lovers are to whisper."],"id":550,"m":"In this same play it happens that I, Snout by name, play a wall, and the sort of wall, I'd like you to think, that had a cracked hole or chink in it, through which the lovers Pyramus and Thisbe often whispered very secretly. This clay, this plaster and this stone show that I am that same wall. That's the truth. And this is the crack, right and left, through which the frightened lovers are going to whisper."},{"t":"sp","who":"THESEUS","lines":["Would you desire lime and hair to speak better?"],"id":551,"m":"Could you want plaster and horsehair to speak any better?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["It is the wittiest partition that ever I heard discourse, my lord."],"id":552,"m":"It's the cleverest wall I ever heard give a speech, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"THESEUS","lines":["Pyramus draws near the wall; silence."],"id":553,"m":"Pyramus is coming to the wall. Quiet."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter PYRAMUS","id":554},{"t":"sp","who":"PYRAMUS","lines":["O grim-look'd night! O night with hue so black!","O night, which ever art when day is not!","O night, O night, alack, alack, alack,","I fear my Thisbe's promise is forgot!","And thou, O wall, O sweet, O lovely wall,","That stand'st between her father's ground and mine;","Thou wall, O wall, O sweet and lovely wall,","Show me thy chink, to blink through with mine eyne."],"id":555,"m":"Oh grim-looking night! Oh night so black in colour! Oh night, which always is when day is not! Oh night, oh night, alas, alas, alas, I fear my Thisbe has forgotten her promise! And you, oh wall, oh sweet, oh lovely wall, that stands between her father's land and mine, you wall, oh wall, oh sweet and lovely wall, show me your crack, so I can peep through with my eyes."},{"t":"sd","text":"WALL holds up his fingers","id":556},{"t":"sp","who":"PYRAMUS","cont":true,"lines":["Thanks, courteous wall: Jove shield thee well for this!","But what see I? No Thisbe do I see.","O wicked wall, through whom I see no bliss,","Curs'd be thy stones for thus deceiving me!"],"id":557,"m":"Thank you, polite wall. Jove protect you for this! But what do I see? No Thisbe do I see. Oh wicked wall, through which I see no happiness, curse your stones for tricking me like this!"},{"t":"sp","who":"THESEUS","lines":["The wall, methinks, being sensible, should curse again."],"id":558,"m":"I think the wall, since it can feel, should curse him back."},{"t":"sp","who":"PYRAMUS","lines":["No, in truth, sir, he should not. 'Deceiving me' is Thisbe's cue: she","is to enter now, and I am to spy her through the wall. You shall see it","will fall pat as I told you. Yonder she comes."],"id":559,"m":"No, honestly, sir, he shouldn't. 'Tricking me' is Thisbe's cue. She comes on now, and I have to spot her through the wall. You'll see, it'll happen exactly as I told you. Here she comes."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter THISBE","id":560},{"t":"sp","who":"THISBE","lines":["O wall, full often hast thou heard my moans,","For parting my fair Pyramus and me.","My cherry lips have often kiss'd thy stones,","Thy stones with lime and hair knit up in thee."],"id":561,"m":"Oh wall, you have very often heard me moan because you keep my handsome Pyramus and me apart. My cherry lips have often kissed your stones, your stones stuck together with plaster and hair."},{"t":"sp","who":"PYRAMUS","lines":["I see a voice; now will I to the chink,","To spy an I can hear my Thisbe's face.","Thisbe?"],"id":562,"m":"I see a voice! Now I'll go to the crack to see if I can hear my Thisbe's face. Thisbe?"},{"t":"sp","who":"THISBE","lines":["My love thou art, my love I think."],"id":563,"m":"You are my love, my love I think."},{"t":"sp","who":"PYRAMUS","lines":["Think what thou wilt, I am thy lover's grace;","And like Limander am I trusty still."],"id":564,"m":"Think what you like, I am your gracious lover, and like Limander I am still faithful."},{"t":"sp","who":"THISBE","lines":["And I like Helen, till the fates me kill."],"id":565,"m":"And I'm like Helen, until the Fates kill me."},{"t":"sp","who":"PYRAMUS","lines":["Not Shafalus to Procrus was so true."],"id":566,"m":"Shafalus was never so true to Procrus."},{"t":"sp","who":"THISBE","lines":["As Shafalus to Procrus, I to you."],"id":567,"m":"As Shafalus was to Procrus, so am I to you."},{"t":"sp","who":"PYRAMUS","lines":["O kiss me through the hole of this vile wall."],"id":568,"m":"Oh, kiss me through the hole in this vile wall!"},{"t":"sp","who":"THISBE","lines":["I kiss the wall's hole, not your lips at all."],"id":569,"m":"I'm kissing the wall's hole, not your lips at all."},{"t":"sp","who":"PYRAMUS","lines":["Wilt thou at Ninny's tomb meet me straightway?"],"id":570,"m":"Will you meet me at Ninny's tomb straight away?"},{"t":"sp","who":"THISBE","lines":["'Tide life, 'tide death, I come without delay."],"id":571,"m":"Come life or come death, I'll come without delay."},{"t":"sp","who":"WALL","lines":["Thus have I, Wall, my part discharged so;","And, being done, thus Wall away doth go."],"id":572,"m":"So I, Wall, have played my part, and now it's done, Wall goes away."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt WALL, PYRAMUS and THISBE","id":573},{"t":"sp","who":"THESEUS","lines":["Now is the mural down between the two neighbours."],"id":574,"m":"Now the wall between the two neighbours is down."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["No remedy, my lord, when walls are so wilful to hear without warning."],"id":575,"m":"There's no help for it, my lord, when walls are so keen to listen without warning."},{"t":"sp","who":"HIPPOLYTA","lines":["This is the silliest stuff that ever I heard."],"id":576,"m":"This is the silliest nonsense I've ever heard."},{"t":"sp","who":"THESEUS","lines":["The best in this kind are but shadows; and the worst are no worse, if","imagination amend them."],"id":577,"m":"The best plays of this kind are only shadows, and the worst are no worse, if imagination improves them."},{"t":"sp","who":"HIPPOLYTA","lines":["It must be your imagination then, and not theirs."],"id":578,"m":"Then it must be your imagination that does it, not theirs."},{"t":"sp","who":"THESEUS","lines":["If we imagine no worse of them than they of themselves, they may pass","for excellent men. Here come two noble beasts in, a man and a lion."],"id":579,"m":"If we think no worse of them than they think of themselves, they could pass for excellent men. Here come two noble beasts, a man and a lion."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter LION and MOONSHINE","id":580},{"t":"sp","who":"LION","lines":["You, ladies, you, whose gentle hearts do fear","The smallest monstrous mouse that creeps on floor,","May now, perchance, both quake and tremble here,","When lion rough in wildest rage doth roar.","Then know that I, one Snug the joiner, am","A lion fell, nor else no lion's dam;","For if I should as lion come in strife","Into this place, 'twere pity on my life."],"id":581,"m":"You ladies, whose gentle hearts are frightened of the smallest monstrous mouse that creeps across the floor, may now perhaps shake and tremble here, when a rough lion roars in wildest rage. So know that I, Snug the joiner, am a fierce lion only in costume, and no lioness either. Because if I came into this place as a real lion looking for a fight, I'd be sorry for my life."},{"t":"sp","who":"THESEUS","lines":["A very gentle beast, and of a good conscience."],"id":582,"m":"A very gentle beast, with a good conscience."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["The very best at a beast, my lord, that e'er I saw."],"id":583,"m":"The best at playing a beast I ever saw, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"LYSANDER","lines":["This lion is a very fox for his valour."],"id":584,"m":"This lion is a real fox when it comes to bravery."},{"t":"sp","who":"THESEUS","lines":["True; and a goose for his discretion."],"id":585,"m":"True, and a goose when it comes to good sense."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["Not so, my lord, for his valour cannot carry his discretion, and the","fox carries the goose."],"id":586,"m":"Not so, my lord, because his bravery can't carry off his good sense, and a fox carries off a goose."},{"t":"sp","who":"THESEUS","lines":["His discretion, I am sure, cannot carry his valour; for the goose","carries not the fox. It is well; leave it to his discretion, and let us","listen to the moon."],"id":587,"m":"I'm sure his good sense can't carry off his bravery, because a goose doesn't carry off a fox. Well, leave it to his good sense, and let's listen to the moon."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOONSHINE","lines":["This lanthorn doth the hornèd moon present."],"id":588,"m":"This lantern represents the horned moon."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["He should have worn the horns on his head."],"id":589,"m":"He should have worn the horns on his head."},{"t":"sp","who":"THESEUS","lines":["He is no crescent, and his horns are invisible within the","circumference."],"id":590,"m":"He isn't a crescent moon, and his horns are invisible inside his full circle."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOONSHINE","lines":["This lanthorn doth the hornèd moon present;","Myself the man i' the moon do seem to be."],"id":591,"m":"This lantern represents the horned moon. I seem to be the man in the moon."},{"t":"sp","who":"THESEUS","lines":["This is the greatest error of all the rest; the man should be put into","the lantern. How is it else the man i' the moon?"],"id":592,"m":"That's the biggest mistake of all. The man should be put inside the lantern. How else is he the man in the moon?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["He dares not come there for the candle, for you see, it is already in","snuff."],"id":593,"m":"He doesn't dare go in there because of the candle. You see, it's already burning badly."},{"t":"sp","who":"HIPPOLYTA","lines":["I am aweary of this moon. Would he would change!"],"id":594,"m":"I'm tired of this moon. I wish he would change!"},{"t":"sp","who":"THESEUS","lines":["It appears by his small light of discretion that he is in the wane; but","yet, in courtesy, in all reason, we must stay the time."],"id":595,"m":"Judging by his feeble light of good sense, he seems to be waning. But still, to be polite, and fair, we must wait it out."},{"t":"sp","who":"LYSANDER","lines":["Proceed, Moon."],"id":596,"m":"Carry on, Moon."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOON","lines":["All that I have to say, is to tell you that the lantern is the moon; I","the man i' the moon; this thorn-bush my thorn-bush; and this dog my","dog."],"id":597,"m":"All I have to say is to tell you that the lantern is the moon, I am the man in the moon, this thorn bush is my thorn bush, and this dog is my dog."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["Why, all these should be in the lantern, for all these are in the moon.","But silence; here comes Thisbe."],"id":598,"m":"Well, all these should be in the lantern, because they're all in the moon. But quiet, here comes Thisbe."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter THISBE","id":599},{"t":"sp","who":"THISBE","lines":["This is old Ninny's tomb. Where is my love?"],"id":600,"m":"This is old Ninny's tomb. Where is my love?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LION","lines":["Oh!"],"id":601,"m":"Oh!"},{"t":"sd","text":"The LION roars, THISBE runs off","id":602},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["Well roared, Lion."],"id":603,"m":"Well roared, Lion."},{"t":"sp","who":"THESEUS","lines":["Well run, Thisbe."],"id":604,"m":"Well run, Thisbe."},{"t":"sp","who":"HIPPOLYTA","lines":["Well shone, Moon. Truly, the moon shines with a good grace."],"id":605,"m":"Well shone, Moon. Honestly, the moon shines very nicely."},{"t":"sd","text":"The LION tears THISBE's mantle, and exit","id":606},{"t":"sp","who":"THESEUS","lines":["Well moused, Lion."],"id":607,"m":"Well mauled, Lion, like a cat with a mouse."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["And then came Pyramus."],"id":608,"m":"And then Pyramus came."},{"t":"sp","who":"LYSANDER","lines":["And so the lion vanished."],"id":609,"m":"And so the lion disappeared."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter PYRAMUS","id":610},{"t":"sp","who":"PYRAMUS","lines":["Sweet Moon, I thank thee for thy sunny beams;","I thank thee, Moon, for shining now so bright;","For, by thy gracious golden, glittering gleams,","I trust to take of truest Thisbe sight.","But stay! O spite!","But mark, poor knight,","What dreadful dole is here!","Eyes, do you see?","How can it be?","O dainty duck! O dear!","Thy mantle good,","What, stained with blood?","Approach, ye Furies fell!","O Fates, come, come;","Cut thread and thrum;","Quail, rush, conclude, and quell!"],"id":611,"m":"Sweet Moon, I thank you for your sunny beams. I thank you, Moon, for shining so brightly now, because by your gracious, golden, glittering gleams I hope to get a sight of my most faithful Thisbe. But wait! Oh, cruel! But look, poor knight, what dreadful grief is here! Eyes, do you see? How can it be? Oh dainty duck! Oh dear! Your good cloak, what, stained with blood? Come here, you cruel Furies! Oh Fates, come, come, cut the thread of my life, thread and fringe, crush, destroy, finish and kill!"},{"t":"sp","who":"THESEUS","lines":["This passion, and the death of a dear friend, would go near to make a","man look sad."],"id":612,"m":"This grief, together with the death of a dear friend, would almost make a man look sad."},{"t":"sp","who":"HIPPOLYTA","lines":["Beshrew my heart, but I pity the man."],"id":613,"m":"Curse my heart, but I feel sorry for the man."},{"t":"sp","who":"PYRAMUS","lines":["O wherefore, Nature, didst thou lions frame,","Since lion vile hath here deflower'd my dear?","Which is—no, no—which was the fairest dame","That liv'd, that lov'd, that lik'd, that look'd with cheer.","Come, tears, confound!","Out, sword, and wound","The pap of Pyramus;","Ay, that left pap,","Where heart doth hop:","Thus die I, thus, thus, thus.","Now am I dead,","Now am I fled;","My soul is in the sky.","Tongue, lose thy light!","Moon, take thy flight!","Now die, die, die, die, die."],"id":614,"m":"Oh, why, Nature, did you make lions, since a vile lion has 'deflowered' my dear here? She is (no, no) she was the most beautiful lady who ever lived, loved, liked, or looked cheerful. Come, tears, destroy me! Out, sword, and wound the breast of Pyramus. Yes, that left breast, where the heart hops. So I die, like this, this, this. Now I am dead, now I have fled, my soul is in the sky. Tongue, lose your light! Moon, fly away! Now die, die, die, die, die."},{"t":"sd","text":"Dies. Exit MOONSHINE","id":615},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["No die, but an ace, for him; for he is but one."],"id":616,"m":"Not a dice but an ace for him, because he's only one."},{"t":"sp","who":"LYSANDER","lines":["Less than an ace, man; for he is dead, he is nothing."],"id":617,"m":"Less than an ace, man, because he's dead. He's nothing."},{"t":"sp","who":"THESEUS","lines":["With the help of a surgeon he might yet recover and prove an ass."],"id":618,"m":"With the help of a surgeon he might still recover and turn out to be an ass."},{"t":"sp","who":"HIPPOLYTA","lines":["How chance Moonshine is gone before Thisbe comes back and finds her","lover?"],"id":619,"m":"How come Moonshine has gone before Thisbe comes back and finds her lover?"},{"t":"sp","who":"THESEUS","lines":["She will find him by starlight."],"id":620,"m":"She'll find him by starlight."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter THISBE","id":621},{"t":"sp","who":"THESEUS","cont":true,"lines":["Here she comes, and her passion ends the play."],"id":622,"m":"Here she comes, and her grief will end the play."},{"t":"sp","who":"HIPPOLYTA","lines":["Methinks she should not use a long one for such a Pyramus. I hope she","will be brief."],"id":623,"m":"I don't think she should make a long speech of grief for such a Pyramus. I hope she'll be brief."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["A mote will turn the balance, which Pyramus, which Thisbe, is the","better: he for a man, God warrant us; she for a woman, God bless us!"],"id":624,"m":"A speck of dust would tip the scales over which is better, Pyramus or Thisbe: he as a man, God help us, or she as a woman, God bless us!"},{"t":"sp","who":"LYSANDER","lines":["She hath spied him already with those sweet eyes."],"id":625,"m":"She's already spotted him with those sweet eyes."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["And thus she means, videlicet—"],"id":626,"m":"And so she moans, as follows..."},{"t":"sp","who":"THISBE","lines":["Asleep, my love?","What, dead, my dove?","O Pyramus, arise,","Speak, speak. Quite dumb?","Dead, dead? A tomb","Must cover thy sweet eyes.","These lily lips,","This cherry nose,","These yellow cowslip cheeks,","Are gone, are gone!","Lovers, make moan;","His eyes were green as leeks.","O Sisters Three,","Come, come to me,","With hands as pale as milk;","Lay them in gore,","Since you have shore","With shears his thread of silk.","Tongue, not a word:","Come, trusty sword,","Come, blade, my breast imbrue;","And farewell, friends.","Thus Thisbe ends.","Adieu, adieu, adieu."],"id":627,"m":"Asleep, my love? What, dead, my dove? Oh Pyramus, get up! Speak, speak. Quite silent? Dead, dead? A tomb must cover your sweet eyes. These lily-white lips, this cherry-red nose, these yellow cowslip cheeks are gone, are gone! Lovers, moan. His eyes were as green as leeks. Oh Three Sisters of Fate, come, come to me with hands as pale as milk. Dip them in blood, since you have cut his silken thread of life with your scissors. Tongue, not a word. Come, trusty sword. Come, blade, soak my breast in blood. And farewell, friends. This is how Thisbe ends. Goodbye, goodbye, goodbye."},{"t":"sd","text":"Dies","id":628},{"t":"sp","who":"THESEUS","lines":["Moonshine and Lion are left to bury the dead."],"id":629,"m":"Moonshine and Lion are left to bury the dead."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEMETRIUS","lines":["Ay, and Wall too."],"id":630,"m":"Yes, and Wall too."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOTTOM","lines":["No, I assure you; the wall is down that parted their fathers. Will it","please you to see the epilogue, or to hear a Bergomask dance between","two of our company?"],"id":631,"m":"No, I promise you, the wall that separated their fathers has come down. Would you like to see the epilogue, or hear a country dance performed by two of our company?"},{"t":"sp","who":"THESEUS","lines":["No epilogue, I pray you; for your play needs no excuse. Never excuse;","for when the players are all dead there need none to be blamed. Marry,","if he that writ it had played Pyramus, and hanged himself in Thisbe's","garter, it would have been a fine tragedy; and so it is, truly; and","very notably discharged. But come, your Bergomask; let your epilogue","alone."],"id":632,"m":"No epilogue, please, because your play needs no apology. Never apologise, because when the actors are all dead, no one needs to be blamed. Really, if the man who wrote it had played Pyramus and hanged himself with Thisbe's garter, it would have been a fine tragedy. And so it is, truly, and very remarkably performed. But come on, your country dance. Forget the epilogue."},{"t":"sd","text":"Here a dance of Clowns","id":633},{"t":"sp","who":"THESEUS","cont":true,"lines":["The iron tongue of midnight hath told twelve.","Lovers, to bed; 'tis almost fairy time.","I fear we shall outsleep the coming morn","As much as we this night have overwatch'd.","This palpable-gross play hath well beguil'd","The heavy gait of night. Sweet friends, to bed.","A fortnight hold we this solemnity","In nightly revels and new jollity."],"id":634,"m":"The iron tongue of the midnight bell has struck twelve. Lovers, to bed. It's almost fairy time. I'm afraid we'll sleep late into the morning, as long as we've stayed up tonight. This obviously crude play has helped the slow night pass very well. Sweet friends, to bed. For two weeks we'll keep up this celebration with parties every night and new festivities."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt","id":635},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter PUCK","id":636},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCK","lines":["Now the hungry lion roars,","And the wolf behowls the moon;","Whilst the heavy ploughman snores,","All with weary task fordone.","Now the wasted brands do glow,","Whilst the screech-owl, screeching loud,","Puts the wretch that lies in woe","In remembrance of a shroud.","Now it is the time of night","That the graves, all gaping wide,","Every one lets forth his sprite,","In the church-way paths to glide.","And we fairies, that do run","By the triple Hecate's team","From the presence of the sun,","Following darkness like a dream,","Now are frolic; not a mouse","Shall disturb this hallow'd house.","I am sent with broom before,","To sweep the dust behind the door."],"id":637,"m":"Now the hungry lion roars and the wolf howls at the moon, while the tired ploughman snores, worn out by his hard work. Now the burnt-out logs glow, while the screech owl, screeching loudly, makes the miserable person lying ill think of his burial shroud. Now it is the time of night when the graves all gape wide, and every one lets out its ghost to glide along the paths to the church. And we fairies, who run beside the chariot of Hecate, goddess of the moon and the underworld, away from the sun, following darkness like a dream, are now ready for fun. Not a mouse will disturb this blessed house. I've been sent ahead with a broom to sweep the dust from behind the door."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter OBERON and TITANIA with their Train","id":638},{"t":"sp","who":"OBERON","lines":["~Through the house give glimmering light,","~By the dead and drowsy fire.","~Every elf and fairy sprite","~Hop as light as bird from brier,","~And this ditty after me,","~Sing and dance it trippingly."],"id":639,"m":"Give a flickering light through the house from the dying, sleepy fire. Every elf and fairy spirit, hop as lightly as a bird from a briar, and sing this song after me and dance it nimbly."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITANIA","lines":["~First rehearse your song by rote,","~To each word a warbling note;","~Hand in hand, with fairy grace,","~Will we sing, and bless this place."],"id":640,"m":"First, practise your song by heart, with a trilling note for every word. Hand in hand, with fairy grace, we'll sing and bless this place."},{"t":"sd","text":"Song and Dance","id":641},{"t":"sp","who":"OBERON","lines":["~Now, until the break of day,","~Through this house each fairy stray.","~To the best bride-bed will we,","~Which by us shall blessèd be;","~And the issue there create","~Ever shall be fortunate.","~So shall all the couples three","~Ever true in loving be;","~And the blots of Nature's hand","~Shall not in their issue stand:","~Never mole, hare-lip, nor scar,","~Nor mark prodigious, such as are","~Despised in nativity,","~Shall upon their children be.","~With this field-dew consecrate,","~Every fairy take his gait,","~And each several chamber bless,","~Through this palace, with sweet peace;","~And the owner of it blest.","~Ever shall it in safety rest,","~Trip away. Make no stay;","~Meet me all by break of day."],"id":642,"m":"Now, until daybreak, let every fairy wander through this house. We will go to the best bridal bed, which we will bless, and the children made there will always be lucky. So all three couples will always be faithful in love, and Nature's flaws will not appear in their children. No mole, cleft lip or scar, no unnatural birthmark of the kind people hate to see in a newborn baby, will be on their children. With this blessed dew from the fields, let every fairy go on its way and bless each room in this palace with sweet peace, and bless the owner too. It will always rest in safety. Skip away. Don't stay. Meet me, all of you, by daybreak."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt OBERON, TITANIA and Train","id":643},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCK","lines":["If we shadows have offended,","Think but this, and all is mended,","That you have but slumber'd here","While these visions did appear.","And this weak and idle theme,","No more yielding but a dream,","Gentles, do not reprehend.","If you pardon, we will mend.","And, as I am an honest Puck,","If we have unearnèd luck","Now to 'scape the serpent's tongue,","We will make amends ere long;","Else the Puck a liar call.","So, good night unto you all.","Give me your hands, if we be friends,","And Robin shall restore amends."],"id":644,"m":"If we shadows have offended you, just think this, and everything is put right: that you have only been asleep here while these visions appeared. And this weak and silly story, which is no more than a dream, ladies and gentlemen, don't criticise it. If you forgive us, we'll do better. And as I am an honest Puck, if we are lucky enough, though we don't deserve it, to escape your hisses, we'll make it up to you soon, or else call Puck a liar. So, good night to you all. Give me your applause, if we're friends, and Robin will make amends."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit","id":645}];
const STUDY={"chars":[{"name":"Theseus","group":"The court of Athens","desc":"Duke of Athens, about to marry Hippolyta, the Amazon queen he defeated in war. He upholds the harsh law that gives Egeus power over his daughter, but in the morning after the night in the wood he overrules Egeus and lets the lovers marry. Practical and sceptical, he dismisses the lovers' story as imagination, yet he insists on watching the workmen's play and treats their clumsy effort kindly."},{"name":"Hippolyta","group":"The court of Athens","desc":"Queen of the Amazons, won by Theseus in battle and now his bride. She speaks little but thoughtfully: she believes the lovers' story is more than fancy because all four tell it the same way, and at the play she is the first to call it silly and the first to feel sorry for Pyramus."},{"name":"Egeus","group":"The court of Athens","desc":"Hermia's father. Furious that she loves Lysander, he demands the old Athenian law: she must marry Demetrius or die. He follows Theseus to the wood and demands the law again when the lovers are found, but Theseus overrules him and he is not heard from again."},{"name":"Philostrate","group":"The court of Athens","desc":"Theseus's master of the revels, in charge of entertainments. He has seen the workmen rehearse and advises Theseus not to choose their play, laughing at it as ten words too long."},{"name":"Hermia","group":"The lovers","desc":"Egeus's daughter, small, dark and quick-tempered. She defies her father and the Duke to stay true to Lysander and runs away with him. When the love-juice turns Lysander against her she is bewildered, then furious, ready to scratch Helena's eyes out for calling her short. She wakes in the morning with her love restored."},{"name":"Helena","group":"The lovers","desc":"Hermia's childhood friend, tall and fair, in love with Demetrius, who has abandoned her for Hermia. She betrays her friend's secret to win his thanks and follows him into the wood, begging to be treated like his spaniel. When both young men suddenly woo her she is sure they are mocking her. In the end Demetrius loves her for good."},{"name":"Lysander","group":"The lovers","desc":"Hermia's lover. He argues that he is every bit as good a match as Demetrius and plans their escape to his aunt's house. Puck mistakes him for Demetrius, and the love-juice makes him abandon Hermia for Helena, insulting her savagely. Oberon's cure restores his love for Hermia."},{"name":"Demetrius","group":"The lovers","desc":"The young man Egeus wants Hermia to marry. He once courted Helena and dropped her. He follows the runaways into the wood and treats Helena with contempt until Oberon has the love-juice put on his eyes. He is the only lover who is never cured: he stays in love with Helena and marries her."},{"name":"Oberon","group":"The fairies","desc":"King of the fairies. He quarrels with Titania over an Indian boy she refuses to give him as his henchman, and their quarrel has thrown the weather and seasons into chaos. He uses the love-in-idleness flower to make her fall for a monster and win the boy, and also tries to help Helena. Once he has what he wants he undoes the spells and blesses the marriages."},{"name":"Titania","group":"The fairies","desc":"Queen of the fairies. Loyal to the memory of her dead friend, she refuses to give up the friend's son to Oberon. Under the spell she falls in love with Bottom wearing an ass's head and pampers him in her bower. When she wakes she is horrified, makes peace with Oberon and joins him to bless the newly-weds."},{"name":"Puck","group":"The fairies","desc":"Robin Goodfellow, Oberon's jester and messenger, a mischievous spirit of English folk tales who plays tricks on villagers. He gives Bottom the ass's head, puts the love-juice on the wrong Athenian and leads the quarrelling young men round in the fog. He enjoys the chaos: 'Lord, what fools these mortals be!' He speaks the epilogue."},{"name":"Peaseblossom","group":"The fairies","desc":"One of Titania's fairies, told to serve Bottom. Named after the flower of the pea plant; Bottom asks to be remembered to his mother, Mistress Squash."},{"name":"Cobweb","group":"The fairies","desc":"One of Titania's fairies. Bottom jokes that he will use him on a cut finger (cobwebs were used to stop bleeding) and sends him to kill a bumblebee for its honey."},{"name":"Moth","group":"The fairies","desc":"One of Titania's fairies, sent to wait on Bottom. Probably pronounced 'mote', a tiny speck."},{"name":"Mustardseed","group":"The fairies","desc":"One of Titania's fairies. Bottom sympathises with him about all the roast beef his family has been eaten with."},{"name":"Fairy","group":"The fairies","desc":"A fairy in Titania's service who meets Puck at the start of Act 2 and recognises him as Robin Goodfellow."},{"name":"Bottom","group":"The workmen","desc":"Nick Bottom, a weaver, the star of the workmen's company. He wants to play every part, is full of confident advice and mixes up long words. Puck gives him an ass's head and Titania falls in love with him, and he takes it all calmly, asking the fairies to scratch his head. Back in Athens he plays Pyramus at the wedding feast and is sure his 'most rare vision' would make a ballad."},{"name":"Quince","group":"The workmen","desc":"Peter Quince, a carpenter, who organises the play and hands out the parts. Patient with Bottom, he worries about frightening the ladies. He speaks the Prologue at the wedding and gets all the punctuation wrong."},{"name":"Flute","group":"The workmen","desc":"Francis Flute, a bellows-mender, a young man who has to play Thisbe even though he has a beard coming. He says his whole part at once, cues and all, at rehearsal."},{"name":"Snout","group":"The workmen","desc":"Tom Snout, a tinker, cast as Pyramus's father but in the end plays Wall, with his fingers as the chink."},{"name":"Snug","group":"The workmen","desc":"Snug, a joiner, who plays the Lion and is worried about learning his lines, though the part is only roaring. He tells the ladies he is really Snug so as not to frighten them."},{"name":"Starveling","group":"The workmen","desc":"Robin Starveling, a tailor, cast as Thisbe's mother but in the end plays Moonshine, with a lantern, a thorn bush and a dog."},{"name":"Pyramus","group":"The play of Pyramus and Thisbe","desc":"The lover in the workmen's play, played by Bottom. He finds Thisbe's bloody cloak, thinks a lion has killed her and stabs himself at great length."},{"name":"Thisbe","group":"The play of Pyramus and Thisbe","desc":"The heroine of the workmen's play, played by Flute. She escapes the lion, finds Pyramus dead and kills herself with his sword. Her story is a comic mirror of Hermia's."},{"name":"Prologue","group":"The play of Pyramus and Thisbe","desc":"Quince, introducing the play. His punctuation turns his apology into an insult, and he then explains the whole plot before it happens."},{"name":"Wall","group":"The play of Pyramus and Thisbe","desc":"Snout in plaster and rough-cast, holding up his fingers as the chink the lovers whisper through."},{"name":"Lion","group":"The play of Pyramus and Thisbe","desc":"Snug, who reassures the ladies that he is only a joiner before he roars and chases Thisbe off."},{"name":"Moonshine","group":"The play of Pyramus and Thisbe","desc":"Starveling with a lantern, a thorn bush and a dog, the man in the moon. When the courtiers heckle him he gives up his verse and simply explains his props."}],"scenes":{"a1s1":{"sum":"In his palace, Theseus, Duke of Athens, counts the four days until his wedding to Hippolyta, the Amazon queen he conquered, and sends Philostrate to stir up merriment in the city. Egeus bursts in to complain that his daughter Hermia loves Lysander and refuses to marry Demetrius, the man he has chosen. Theseus tells Hermia that by Athenian law she must obey her father, die, or become a nun, and gives her until his wedding day to decide. Left alone, Hermia and Lysander agree to run away through the wood to his widowed aunt's house, seven leagues from Athens and beyond the reach of its law. They tell Helena, who loves Demetrius, and she decides to tell Demetrius, hoping for his thanks.","key":["The play opens with a conflict between love and law: a father's will, backed by the Duke, against a daughter's choice.","Lysander's 'The course of true love never did run smooth' sets up the whole play's pattern of obstacles to love.","Helena's soliloquy explains love's blindness: 'Love looks not with the eyes, but with the mind', and her betrayal of Hermia's secret drives the plot into the wood."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Athenian law allow Egeus to do if Hermia disobeys him?","o":["Send her to prison","Have her put to death","Disinherit her","Banish her from Athens"],"a":1},{"q":"Where do Hermia and Lysander plan to go?","o":["To the temple of Diana","To Lysander's widowed aunt's house","To Theseus's palace","To the sea"],"a":1},{"q":"Why does Helena decide to tell Demetrius about the escape?","o":["To get Hermia into trouble","To win Demetrius's thanks and see him","Because Egeus asks her to","To stop the wedding"],"a":1}]},"a1s2":{"sum":"At Peter Quince's house, a group of Athenian workmen meet to cast a play they hope to perform at the Duke's wedding: 'The most lamentable comedy and most cruel death of Pyramus and Thisbe'. Nick Bottom the weaver is cast as Pyramus, but he wants to play Thisbe and the Lion as well and shows off how he would do each part. Flute must play Thisbe despite his growing beard, and slow Snug gets the Lion's part, which is only roaring. They agree to rehearse in secret in the palace wood the next night.","key":["The workmen are a comic contrast to the court: honest, eager and hopelessly literal about acting.","Bottom's confidence and misused words ('aggravate my voice') make him the centre of the comedy.","The rehearsal in the wood will bring the workmen together with the lovers and the fairies."],"quiz":[{"q":"What part is Bottom given?","o":["Thisbe","The Lion","Pyramus","Wall"],"a":2},{"q":"Why doesn't Flute want to play Thisbe?","o":["He can't remember lines","He has a beard coming","He wants to play the Lion","He is afraid of the Duke"],"a":1},{"q":"Where will the workmen rehearse?","o":["At Quince's house","In the palace wood by moonlight","In the temple","At the Duke's palace"],"a":1}]},"a2s1":{"sum":"In the wood near Athens, Puck meets one of Titania's fairies and explains that Oberon and Titania are quarrelling over a changeling boy. The king and queen meet and accuse each other of jealousy and unfaithfulness: Titania says Oberon has come from India only because he once loved Hippolyta, and he throws her fondness for Theseus back at her. Oberon wants the boy as his henchman. Titania describes how their quarrel has disrupted nature: floods, rotting crops and confused seasons. She refuses to give up the boy, whose mother was her friend. To punish her, Oberon sends Puck for the flower love-in-idleness, whose juice makes a sleeper fall in love with the next creature they see. Invisible, Oberon watches Demetrius cruelly reject Helena, and orders Puck to put the juice on the eyes of the 'Athenian' youth so that he will love her.","key":["The fairy quarrel over the Indian boy mirrors the human quarrels over Hermia: love and power are tangled together.","Titania's speech links the fairies' disorder to disorder in nature, a sign of how powerful they are.","Oberon's instruction to find the man by his 'Athenian garments' sets up the mistake that drives the chaos of Act 3."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why are Oberon and Titania quarrelling?","o":["He has fallen in love with Hippolyta","She will not give him a changeling boy","She has banished Puck","He has stolen her crown"],"a":1},{"q":"What does the juice of love-in-idleness do?","o":["Puts people to sleep for ever","Makes a sleeper love the next creature they see","Turns people into animals","Makes people invisible"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Oberon order Puck to do for Helena?","o":["Take her home to Athens","Put the juice on the eyes of the Athenian man","Hide her from Demetrius","Make Hermia fall in love with Demetrius"],"a":1}]},"a2s2":{"sum":"Titania's fairies sing her to sleep, and Oberon squeezes the flower's juice on her eyelids, wishing that she will wake when some vile thing is near. Lysander and Hermia, lost and tired, lie down to sleep, though Hermia makes him lie a modest distance away. Puck sees Lysander's Athenian clothes, mistakes him for Demetrius and anoints his eyes. Helena, abandoned by Demetrius, wakes Lysander, who instantly falls in love with her. She thinks he is mocking her and runs off; he follows, leaving Hermia asleep. Hermia wakes from a nightmare of a snake eating her heart to find herself alone.","key":["The fairies' lullaby protects Titania from snakes and spiders, but cannot protect her from Oberon's spell.","Puck's mistake turns Lysander's love from Hermia to Helena in a single line: love is shown as arbitrary.","Hermia's dream of a serpent eating her heart while Lysander smiles is an image of the betrayal that has just happened."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does Hermia ask Lysander to lie further away?","o":["He snores","For the sake of modesty","She is angry with him","She wants to keep watch"],"a":1},{"q":"Why does Puck put the juice on Lysander's eyes?","o":["Oberon pointed him out","He mistakes him for Demetrius because of his Athenian clothes","Helena asks him to","He wants to cause trouble"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Hermia dream?","o":["That Lysander is drowned","That a serpent eats her heart while Lysander smiles","That she is married to Demetrius","That fairies steal her away"],"a":1}]},"a3s1":{"sum":"The workmen rehearse near Titania's sleeping place. Bottom worries that the sword and the Lion will frighten the ladies, so they plan a prologue to explain that nothing is real, and decide that people will play Wall and Moonshine. Puck watches, amused, and when Bottom steps out of the scene he gives him an ass's head. His friends flee in terror. Bottom sings to show he is not afraid, which wakes Titania, who falls instantly in love with him. She orders her fairies, Peaseblossom, Cobweb, Moth and Mustardseed, to serve him and leads him to her bower.","key":["The workmen's worries about illusion (will the audience think the lion is real?) make fun of theatre itself.","Bottom's transformation is literal: he becomes the ass his friends already half think him, yet he stays calm and polite.","Bottom's line 'reason and love keep little company together nowadays' sums up the whole play's view of love."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does Bottom want a prologue?","o":["To flatter the Duke","To tell the ladies the sword and lion are not real","To explain the plot","To introduce himself as the star"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Puck do to Bottom?","o":["Makes him invisible","Gives him an ass's head","Puts him to sleep","Turns him into a lion"],"a":1},{"q":"What wakes Titania?","o":["Puck's laughter","Bottom's singing","The workmen screaming","Oberon's horn"],"a":1}]},"a3s2":{"sum":"Puck tells Oberon that Titania loves an ass. When Demetrius enters chasing Hermia, Oberon realises Puck anointed the wrong man, puts the juice on Demetrius's eyes and sends Puck to bring Helena. Now both young men love Helena, who is sure all three are mocking her. Hermia arrives, Lysander rejects her cruelly, and the two women quarrel, Hermia furious at being called short and Helena afraid of her temper. The young men go off to fight over Helena. Oberon orders Puck to cover the night in fog and lead the rivals astray by imitating their voices until they fall asleep. All four lovers end up asleep close together, and Puck puts the cure on Lysander's eyes.","key":["The confusion peaks: the men's love switches completely, yet they speak of it in the same extravagant language as before.","The quarrel between Hermia and Helena destroys a childhood friendship that Helena describes as 'a double cherry'.","Puck enjoys the chaos ('Lord, what fools these mortals be!'), but Oberon makes sure everyone will wake to the right partner."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Oberon do when he sees Puck's mistake?","o":["Punishes Puck","Puts the juice on Demetrius's eyes","Wakes Titania","Sends the lovers home"],"a":1},{"q":"What makes Hermia most angry with Helena?","o":["She stole her letters","She calls her short and a puppet","She told her father","She laughed at her"],"a":1},{"q":"How does Puck stop the young men fighting?","o":["He hides their swords","He leads them astray in fog by imitating their voices","He turns them into animals","He tells Theseus"],"a":1}]},"a4s1":{"sum":"Titania pampers Bottom, who asks the fairies to scratch his head and wants hay and dried peas. They fall asleep in each other's arms. Oberon tells Puck that Titania has given him the changeling boy, so he releases her from the spell. She wakes, horrified at what she loved, and the fairy king and queen are reconciled. At dawn Theseus, Hippolyta and Egeus, out hunting, find the four lovers asleep. Lysander and Demetrius explain as well as they can; Demetrius now loves only Helena. Egeus demands the law, but Theseus overrules him: the two couples will marry with him that day. The dazed lovers follow, and Bottom wakes, amazed by his 'most rare vision'.","key":["Oberon gets the boy by humiliating Titania, a resolution some readers find troubling.","Theseus overrules Egeus and the law: the comic ending needs the father's power to give way.","Bottom's speech about his dream, muddling the senses, is both comic and strangely beautiful."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does Oberon release Titania from the spell?","o":["Puck begs him to","She has given him the changeling boy","Theseus orders it","The flower's juice wears off"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Theseus decide about Hermia?","o":["She must marry Demetrius","She will marry Lysander, overruling Egeus","She must become a nun","She must leave Athens"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Bottom plan to do about his dream?","o":["Forget it","Have Quince write a ballad of it called 'Bottom's Dream'","Tell Titania","Tell the Duke"],"a":1}]},"a4s2":{"sum":"Back in Athens, the workmen despair: without Bottom their play cannot go on, and they will lose a pension of sixpence a day from the Duke. Snug brings news that the Duke has married and that two more couples have married too. Bottom arrives, promising wonders but refusing to tell them, and hurries them off to the palace because their play has been chosen.","key":["The workmen's devotion to Bottom shows their warmth and their simple hopes.","Bottom keeps his night with the Fairy Queen to himself.","The scene moves the play back from the wood to Athens for the final act."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why can't the play go on without Bottom?","o":["He wrote it","No one else can play Pyramus","He has the costumes","The Duke asked for him"],"a":1},{"q":"What do the workmen think they have lost?","o":["Their tools","A pension of sixpence a day","Their wages","The Duke's favour"],"a":1},{"q":"What news does Bottom bring?","o":["The Duke has gone hunting","Their play has been preferred","Titania loves him","The wedding is off"],"a":1}]},"a5s1":{"sum":"At the palace after the three weddings, Theseus dismisses the lovers' story as imagination, like the fantasies of madmen and poets, though Hippolyta thinks it is too consistent to be invented. Theseus chooses the workmen's 'tedious brief' play of Pyramus and Thisbe because it is offered with simple duty. The play is a glorious disaster: Quince's prologue is mispunctuated, Wall, Moonshine and Lion explain themselves, Pyramus and Thisbe mangle their lines and kill themselves at length, and the courtiers heckle throughout. After a dance the couples go to bed at midnight. Puck, Oberon and Titania enter to bless the house and the three marriages, and Puck asks the audience to think of the play as a dream and to forgive it.","key":["Theseus's speech on 'the lunatic, the lover, and the poet' sets imagination against reason, though the audience has seen the fairies are real.","Pyramus and Thisbe is a comic version of what could have happened to the lovers: a tragedy of parents, walls and mistakes.","Puck's epilogue turns the audience into dreamers too: the whole play has been a 'weak and idle theme'."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does Theseus choose the workmen's play?","o":["It is the best play on the list","Simple and dutiful offerings are never wrong","Hippolyta asks for it","Philostrate recommends it"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Snout play?","o":["The Lion","Moonshine","Wall","Thisbe"],"a":2},{"q":"What do the fairies do at the end?","o":["Steal a baby","Bless the house and the marriages","Turn Theseus into an ass","Leave Athens for India"],"a":1}]}},"notes":{"2":"The play opens with the moon and with waiting. Theseus counts four days to the new moon; the moon will shine over the whole play.","6":"Theseus won Hippolyta by force in war. Notice how little she says here: their marriage began as a conquest.","10":"Egeus speaks of Hermia as property ('As she is mine I may dispose of her'). He blames Lysander's love-tokens, as if love were a kind of theft or witchcraft.","11":"Theseus's image of a daughter as wax imprinted by her father shows the power fathers had. Notice he calls Demetrius 'worthy' without giving a reason.","14":"The first of many lines about eyes. Hermia wants her father to see with her eyes; Theseus answers that her eyes must see with his judgement.","17":"The third choice, becoming a nun, introduces Diana, goddess of the moon and of chastity, and her 'cold fruitless moon'.","23":"Lysander's argument is reasonable: he is as rich and well-born as Demetrius. The only difference is Egeus's choice.","29":"The most famous line in the scene. Lysander and Hermia turn their trouble into a duet about all the things that keep lovers apart.","35":"Love as lightning: bright and quickly swallowed by darkness. The Pyramus and Thisbe play will show this tragedy in comic form.","38":"Hermia swears by Cupid and by Dido, who was abandoned by Aeneas. She jokes that men break more vows than women make, an irony given what Lysander will do.","42":"Helena plays on 'fair', meaning both beautiful and blonde. She wishes beauty were catching like an illness.","52":"From here the lovers speak in rhyming couplets, which make their love sound neat and conventional.","58":"Helena's soliloquy: love does not see with the eyes but with the mind, so Cupid is painted blind. It explains why Demetrius can love Hermia and why the love-juice works on the eyes.","64":"The workmen speak prose and mix up words. Their play is meant for the Duke's wedding night, so the three plots will meet at the end.","73":"Bottom shows off in the ranting style of old tyrant plays like 'Ercles' (Hercules). He thinks acting means shouting and weeping.","94":"Bottom's mistake: he means 'moderate' his voice but says 'aggravate'. His roaring 'as gently as any sucking dove' is a contradiction he does not notice.","108":"Puck explains the fairy quarrel: Titania has a changeling boy that Oberon wants. Fairies were believed to steal human babies and leave changelings.","110":"Puck describes himself as the folk spirit Robin Goodfellow, who plays tricks on village people. He is closer to English country life than to the court.","116":"Each fairy accuses the other of loving one of the mortals: Oberon loves Hippolyta, Titania loves Theseus. The fairy and human worlds are tied together.","118":"Titania's longest speech links the fairy quarrel to floods, ruined crops and confused seasons. Many in the 1590s had seen wet summers and failed harvests.","120":"Titania's reason for keeping the boy is loyalty to his dead mother, a mortal friend. Her story gives the quarrel a human, tender side.","128":"Oberon's 'fair vestal, thronèd by the west' was often taken as a compliment to Queen Elizabeth, the Virgin Queen, whom Cupid's arrow could not touch.","136":"Helena's spaniel speech is shocking: she asks to be beaten and kicked like a dog. Love here is humiliating as well as blind.","142":"Helena reverses classical myths: the woman chases and the man runs. In Ovid, Apollo chased Daphne until she turned into a tree.","144":"'We should be woo'd, and were not made to woo.' Helena knows she is breaking the rules of how women were expected to behave.","152":"Oberon's description of Titania's bank is full of real English wildflowers. Notice that his only way to identify Demetrius is his 'Athenian garments'.","159":"The fairies' lullaby is a song in the play. It keeps snakes and spiders away, but Oberon walks straight in.","167":"Oberon wants Titania to wake 'when some vile thing is near'. His spell is a punishment, not just a trick.","174":"Lysander puns on 'lie' (lie down, tell lies). Hermia's modesty, and her insistence on it, matter in a play about runaway lovers.","180":"Puck's charm is in short, four-beat lines with rhymes, the fairies' magic metre. He anoints the wrong man because of the clothes.","191":"Lysander claims that reason now guides his love. The audience knows it is the juice, which makes his claim to reason comic.","196":"Hermia's dream of a serpent eating her heart while Lysander smiles is a nightmare version of what has just happened.","205":"Bottom treats the audience as if they cannot tell a play from reality. The jokes about illusion make the real audience think about how plays work.","215":"Snug must show half his face through the lion's mask and say who he is. The workmen break the illusion to protect the ladies.","236":"Flute's 'most lovely Jew' and 'Ninny's tomb' (for Ninus) show the play's comic mangling of verse.","251":"'Translated' means transformed. Bottom's transformation is the only physical one in the play, and the most comic.","257":"Bottom, with an ass's head, speaks the wisest line about love in the play: 'reason and love keep little company together nowadays.'","260":"Titania offers to purge Bottom's 'mortal grossness' so he can move like a spirit. The queen of the fairies and a weaver: the highest and lowest meet.","286":"Puck's account of the workmen running away is full of animal comparisons (geese, choughs). He calls them 'rude mechanicals', the name they are often given.","312":"'Lord, what fools these mortals be!' Puck watches the lovers like a play put on for his amusement.","321":"Demetrius wakes and goes straight into extravagant love poetry. The language of love is the same whichever woman it is aimed at.","334":"Helena's 'double cherry' speech remembers a friendship as close as being one person. The men's quarrel has destroyed the women's bond.","362":"The quarrel turns physical comedy: tall Helena and short Hermia. Insults about height were probably written for two particular boy actors.","386":"Oberon promises that the night will seem 'a dream and fruitless vision'. The play keeps blurring dreams and reality.","387":"Puck mentions ghosts who must hide from daylight. Oberon answers that they are 'spirits of another sort', not creatures of death.","421":"Puck's charm ends with country proverbs: 'Jack shall have Jill.' The ending promises order restored, pair by pair.","435":"Bottom, as an ass, asks for scratching, hay and dried peas. He accepts being adored by a queen as if it were normal.","445":"Oberon gets the changeling boy while Titania is under the spell. He takes pity only once he has what he wants.","448":"'Methought I was enamour'd of an ass.' Titania describes the night as a dream, like the lovers and Bottom.","466":"Theseus enjoys the 'musical confusion' of his hounds. Harmony made from discord is an image for the whole play.","479":"Theseus overrules Egeus. The law that started the play is set aside without explanation.","481":"The lovers wake uncertain what was real. 'Mine own, and not mine own' captures the strangeness of Demetrius's change.","490":"Bottom muddles the senses ('The eye of man hath not heard'), echoing a Bible verse about the wonders God has prepared. His dream is ridiculous and yet a real vision.","514":"Theseus's famous speech: the lunatic, the lover and the poet are all made of imagination. The irony is that the audience has seen that the fairies are real, and Theseus is himself a character in a poet's play.","515":"Hippolyta disagrees: because all the lovers tell the same story, it must be more than fancy.","524":"Theseus rejects several entertainments before picking the 'tedious brief' play. Its contradictions ('hot ice') match the whole comedy's mix of opposites.","535":"Theseus explains why he will watch the play: he values the effort and the duty, not the skill.","540":"Quince's prologue is a joke about punctuation. Read with the pauses in the right place, it is a polite apology; read as he speaks it, it insults the audience.","550":"Wall explains himself. The workmen think that unless everything is spelled out, the audience will be confused.","577":"'The best in this kind are but shadows.' Theseus says all actors, good or bad, are only shadows that imagination must complete. Puck will call the actors 'shadows' in the epilogue.","614":"Pyramus's death speech is a parody of tragic rhetoric. Shakespeare had just written Romeo and Juliet, another story of lovers who die through a misunderstanding.","627":"Thisbe's lament uses comically wrong comparisons: 'lily lips', 'cherry nose', 'eyes green as leeks'.","634":"'Tis almost fairy time.' The humans leave and the fairies take over the palace, as they took over the wood.","642":"Oberon's blessing protects the couples' children from birth defects, which people feared might be caused by bad luck on the wedding night.","644":"Puck's epilogue speaks to the audience directly. If the play offended, we can treat it as a dream. He asks for applause ('Give me your hands')."},"gloss":{"nuptial":"wedding","apace":"quickly","methinks":"it seems to me","step-dame":"stepmother","dowager":"widow living on her late husband's estate","revenue":"income, inheritance","solemnities":"celebrations, ceremonies","pert":"lively","woo'd":"courted","revelling":"merrymaking","vexation":"anger, distress","bewitch'd":"put under a spell","feigning":"pretending; also soft, longing","gauds":"cheap trinkets","conceits":"fancy little gifts","knacks":"knick-knacks","nosegays":"small bunches of flowers","prevailment":"power to persuade","filch'd":"stolen","dispose":"do what one likes with","abjure":"give up by oath","livery":"uniform, clothing","mew'd":"shut up, caged","virgin patent":"right to remain unmarried","austerity":"strict self-denial","estate":"give as property","avouch":"declare","spotted":"stained, morally impure","beteem":"allow, give","misgraffèd":"badly matched (like a badly grafted tree)","collied":"coal-black","spleen":"sudden impulse","edict":"law, decree","leagues":"units of about three miles","lode-stars":"guiding stars","translated":"transformed","eyne":"eyes","dissolv'd":"melted","intelligence":"information","interlude":"short play","scrip":"script, list","lamentable":"sad","condole":"grieve, lament","Ercles":"Hercules","Phibbus":"Phoebus, the sun god","extempore":"improvised","aggravate":"(Bottom means) moderate","discharge":"perform","con":"learn by heart","obscenely":"(Bottom's mistake, perhaps for) seemly","dale":"valley","orbs":"fairy rings","pensioners":"royal bodyguards","lob":"clumsy lump","anon":"soon","passing":"very","changeling":"child stolen by fairies","perforce":"by force","square":"quarrel","shrewd":"sharp-tongued, mischievous","knavish":"naughty, roguish","quern":"hand-mill for grain","bootless":"uselessly","barm":"froth on ale","gossip's":"old woman's","crab":"crab apple","dewlap":"loose skin on the neck","forsworn":"sworn to give up","wanton":"unfaithful; also playful","buskin'd":"wearing hunting boots","forgeries":"lies, inventions","mead":"meadow","margent":"edge, shore","ringlets":"circle dances","contagious":"poisonous","pelting":"paltry, petty","continents":"banks","murrion":"dead from disease","nine-men's-morris":"outdoor board game cut in turf","hoary-headed":"white-haired","Hiems":"winter","chaplet":"garland","childing":"fruitful","wonted":"usual","liveries":"clothes","mazed":"bewildered","henchman":"page, attendant","vot'ress":"woman who has taken vows to serve","embarkèd traders":"merchant ships","promontory":"headland","dulcet":"sweet","vestal":"virgin","fancy-free":"free from love","love-in-idleness":"the wild pansy","leviathan":"whale","girdle":"belt","adamant":"magnet; hardest stone","impeach":"call into question","brakes":"thickets","griffin":"mythical beast, half eagle, half lion","hind":"female deer","wode":"mad","oxlips":"wild flowers like cowslips","woodbine":"honeysuckle; bindweed","eglantine":"sweet briar, wild rose","roundel":"dance in a circle","cankers":"grubs, caterpillars","reremice":"bats","Philomel":"the nightingale","blind-worms":"slow-worms","ounce":"lynx","pard":"leopard","troth":"truth; promise","beshrew":"curse","churl":"lout, rude man","darkling":"in the dark","sphery":"starry","o'erlook":"read through","surfeit":"excess, overeating","tiring-house":"dressing room","bully":"good friend, fine fellow","by'r lakin":"by our Lady","parlous":"dreadful","rough-cast":"plaster mixed with gravel","hempen homespuns":"peasants in rough cloth","juvenal":"youth","eke":"also","ousel":"blackbird","throstle":"thrush","gleek":"joke, jibe","apricocks":"apricots","dewberries":"blackberries","humble-bees":"bumblebees","nole":"head","patches":"fools, clowns","mechanicals":"workmen","thick-skin":"blockhead","choughs":"jackdaws","latch'd":"moistened","misprision":"mistake","fancy-sick":"lovesick","pageant":"show, spectacle","prepost'rously":"in the wrong order","Taurus":"a mountain range in Turkey","superpraise":"overpraise","aby":"pay for","oes":"circles, sparks","confederacy":"conspiracy","sampler":"piece of embroidery","incorporate":"in one body","Ethiope":"African (an insult about dark looks)","cankerblossom":"grub that destroys a flower bud","minimus":"tiny creature","knot-grass":"weed thought to stunt growth","coil":"turmoil","welkin":"sky","Acheron":"river of the underworld","Aurora's harbinger":"the morning star","recreant":"coward, traitor","coy":"caress","neaf":"fist","provender":"fodder","exposition":"(Bottom means) disposition","dotage":"foolish love","flouriets'":"little flowers'","vaward":"vanguard, early part","flew'd":"with hanging jowls","dewlap'd":"with folds of skin at the throat","Thessalian":"from Thessaly in Greece","conjunction":"together","jole":"jowl, cheek","paramour":"lover","paragon":"model of excellence","preferred":"put forward, chosen","antique":"old; also antic, grotesque","compact":"made up","abridgment":"pastime that shortens time","beguile":"cheat, pass","Bacchanals":"drunken followers of Bacchus","unbreath'd":"unpractised","clerks":"scholars","stand upon points":"take care over punctuation","sinister":"left","Limander":"(he means) Leander","Shafalus":"(he means) Cephalus","Procrus":"(he means) Procris","lanthorn":"lantern","in snuff":"needing trimming; offended","videlicet":"namely","imbrue":"stain with blood","Bergomask":"rustic dance","palpable-gross":"obviously crude","fordone":"worn out","Hecate":"goddess of the moon and the underworld","sprite":"spirit","prodigious":"monstrous, unnatural","reprehend":"criticise","serpent's tongue":"hissing"}};
const PARTS={"10":[[0,9,"I come here full of anger, to complain about my own child, my daughter Hermia. Step forward, Demetrius. My noble lord, this man has my permission to marry her. Step forward, Lysander. And, my gracious Duke, this man has put a spell on my child's heart. You, you, Lysander, you have given her poems and swapped love-tokens with her. You have sung at her window by moonlight in a fake, sweet voice, singing verses of fake love."],[10,23,"You have stolen her imagination with bracelets made of your hair, rings, cheap toys, trinkets, little gifts, flowers and sweets, which are powerful persuaders for a young girl who knows no better. With your tricks you have stolen my daughter's heart, and turned the obedience she owes me into stubbornness. So, my gracious Duke, if she will not agree here, in front of you, to marry Demetrius, I ask for the old right of every Athenian father. Since she belongs to me, I may do what I like with her: either give her to this gentleman or have her put to death, as our law clearly lays down for this case."]],"17":[[0,8,"Either you will be put to death, or you must give up the company of men for ever. So, beautiful Hermia, think hard about what you want. Remember how young you are, and test your feelings, to decide whether, if you refuse your father's choice, you can bear to wear a nun's robes, to be shut up for ever in a gloomy convent, to live your whole life as a childless sister, singing weak hymns to the cold, barren moon."],[9,13,"Those who can control their passions enough to live like that, as pure as pilgrims, are blessed three times over. But on earth a rose that is picked and made into perfume is happier than one that withers on its untouched stem, and grows, lives and dies alone."]],"23":[[0,6,"My lord, I come from as good a family as he does, and I am as rich. I love her more than he does. In every way my prospects are as good as his, if not better. And, more important than any of these boasts, beautiful Hermia loves me. So why shouldn't I claim what is mine?"],[7,11,"I'll say it to his face: Demetrius courted Nedar's daughter, Helena, and won her heart. And she, sweet lady, adores him, worships him like an idol, this stained and unfaithful man."]],"24":[[0,10,"I must admit I have heard as much, and meant to speak to Demetrius about it, but I was so busy with my own affairs that I forgot. But Demetrius, come, and Egeus, come too. You will come with me. I have some private advice for you both. As for you, beautiful Hermia, make sure you bring your wishes into line with your father's will, or else the law of Athens, which I have no power to soften, will condemn you to death or to a life alone."],[11,15,"Come, my Hippolyta. Are you all right, my love? Demetrius and Egeus, come along. I need your help with some arrangements for our wedding, and I want to talk to you about something that closely concerns you both."]],"37":[[0,7,"That is the right attitude. So listen to me, Hermia. I have an aunt, a rich widow with no children. Her house is twenty miles from Athens, and she treats me like her own son. There, sweet Hermia, I can marry you, and the harsh Athenian law cannot follow us there. So, if you love me,"],[8,12,"slip out of your father's house tomorrow night. In the wood three miles outside the town, where I once met you with Helena to celebrate a May morning, I will wait for you."]],"42":[[0,8,"Did you call me beautiful? Take that back. Demetrius loves your beauty. Lucky beauty! Your eyes are guiding stars, and your sweet voice is more musical than a lark's song is to a shepherd in spring, when the wheat is green and the hawthorn comes into bud. Illness is catching. If only good looks were catching too! I would catch yours, beautiful Hermia, before I left. My ear would catch your voice, my eye your eye, my tongue your sweet way of speaking."],[9,12,"If the world were mine, I'd give it all, except Demetrius, to be changed into you. Oh, teach me how you look at him, and what trick you use to control Demetrius's heart!"]],"53":[[0,5,"And in the wood, where you and I used to lie on beds of pale primroses telling each other our sweet secrets, Lysander and I will meet. From there we will turn our backs on Athens and look for new friends among strangers."],[6,9,"Goodbye, sweet friend of my childhood. Pray for us, and good luck in winning your Demetrius! Keep your word, Lysander. We must starve our eyes of the sight of each other, which is lovers' food, until midnight tomorrow."]],"58":[[0,11,"How much happier some people are than others! All over Athens I'm thought to be as beautiful as she is. But so what? Demetrius doesn't think so. He refuses to see what everyone except him can see. And just as he is wrong to be obsessed with Hermia's eyes, so am I wrong to admire everything about him. Love can turn things that are low and worthless into something fine and dignified. Love doesn't see with the eyes but with the mind, and that is why winged Cupid is painted blind. Love's mind has no judgement at all: wings and no eyes stand for reckless hurry."],[12,19,"That's why Love is said to be a child: because he is so often fooled in his choices. Just as naughty boys break their promises in games, so the boy Love tells lies everywhere. Before Demetrius saw Hermia's eyes, he showered me with promises that he was mine alone. But when that hail of promises felt some heat from Hermia, he melted, and his promises melted away too."],[20,25,"I will go and tell him that Hermia is running away. Then tomorrow night he will follow her to the wood. If he thanks me for this news, it will cost me dearly. But I mean to make my pain worth it by seeing him on the way there and back again."]],"73":[[0,11,"That will need some tears to do it properly. If I play it, the audience had better watch out for their eyes. I'll whip up storms of weeping; I'll lament in style. Now the rest. But what I'm really best at is a tyrant. I could play Hercules wonderfully, or a part where I rant and rave and bring the house down. 'The raging rocks and shivering shocks shall break the locks of prison gates, and Phoebus's chariot shall shine from far, and make and mar the foolish Fates.'"],[12,13,"That was magnificent! Now name the rest of the actors. That's the Hercules style, the tyrant style. A lover is more full of sorrow."]],"107":[[0,11,"Over hills, over valleys, through bushes, through briars, over parks, over fences, through floods, through fire, I wander everywhere, faster than the moon moves in its orbit. I serve the Fairy Queen and sprinkle dew on her fairy rings in the grass. The tall cowslips are her bodyguards. You can see spots on their golden coats: those are rubies, gifts from the fairies, and their sweet scent lives in those freckles."],[12,15,"I have to go and find some dewdrops here and hang a pearl in every cowslip's ear. Goodbye, you clumsy lump of a spirit. I'll be off. Our Queen and all her elves will be here any minute."]],"108":[[0,9,"The King is holding his party here tonight. Make sure the Queen doesn't come anywhere near him, because Oberon is extremely angry. She has a lovely boy as her servant, stolen from an Indian king; she never had such a sweet stolen child. Jealous Oberon wants the boy to be a knight in his own following, to roam the wild forests. But she refuses to give up the boy she loves, crowns him with flowers and makes him her whole delight."],[10,13,"Now whenever they meet, in a wood or a meadow, by a clear spring or under the shining stars, they quarrel, so that all their elves crawl into acorn cups in fear and hide there."]],"110":[[0,8,"You've got it right. I am that cheerful night wanderer. I tell jokes to Oberon and make him smile, when I trick a fat, well-fed stallion by neighing like a young female horse. Sometimes I hide in an old woman's drink, looking exactly like a roasted crab apple, and when she drinks I bob against her lips and make her spill the ale down her wrinkled neck."],[9,16,"The wisest old aunt, telling the saddest story, sometimes mistakes me for a three-legged stool. Then I slip out from under her bum, down she tumbles, shouts 'Ouch, my backside!' and starts coughing. Then the whole group hold their sides and laugh, and get merrier, and sneeze, and swear they never had a better time. But make way, fairy. Here comes Oberon."]],"118":[[0,6,"These are lies made up by jealousy. Ever since the start of midsummer, we have never met on a hill, in a valley, forest or meadow, by a stone-lined spring or a reedy stream, or on the sandy edge of the sea, to dance our circles to the whistling wind, without you spoiling our fun with your quarrelling."],[7,19,"So the winds, piping to us for nothing, have taken revenge by sucking up poisonous fogs from the sea. These have fallen on the land and made every little river so swollen that they have burst their banks. So the ox has pulled his plough for nothing, the ploughman has wasted his sweat, and the green corn has rotted before it could ripen. The sheep pens stand empty in the flooded fields, and crows grow fat on the dead, diseased flocks. The outdoor game boards are filled with mud, and the intricate paths in the lush grass can't be seen, because no one walks on them."],[20,36,"The humans are missing their winter festivities. No night is blessed with hymns or carols now. So the moon, which rules the tides, pale with anger, soaks the air with damp, and colds and fevers are everywhere. Because of this disorder, the seasons are changing. White frosts fall on the fresh red rose, and on old Winter's thin, icy head a sweet-smelling crown of summer buds is placed, as if to mock him. Spring, summer, fruitful autumn and angry winter swap their usual clothes, and the confused world can't tell which is which from what they produce. And this whole family of disasters comes from our argument, our quarrel. We are their parents and their origin."]],"120":[[0,8,"Forget it. You couldn't buy the child from me with all of fairyland. His mother was a follower of mine, and on many nights in the spicy Indian air she sat chatting beside me, and sat with me on the yellow sands by the sea, watching the merchant ships on the waves. We laughed to see the sails swell up as if they were pregnant, puffed out by the playful wind."],[9,16,"She would copy them, walking with a pretty, swaying step (her womb was carrying my young page then), and sail across the land to fetch me little presents, and come back as if from a voyage, loaded with goods. But she was mortal, and she died giving birth to that boy. For her sake I am bringing up her son, and for her sake I won't give him up."]],"128":[[0,9,"At that very moment I saw (though you couldn't) Cupid, fully armed, flying between the cold moon and the earth. He took careful aim at a beautiful virgin queen, enthroned in the west, and shot his love-arrow sharply from his bow, as if it could pierce a hundred thousand hearts. But I saw young Cupid's fiery arrow put out by the pure rays of the watery moon, and the royal virgin walked on, lost in innocent thoughts, free of love."],[10,19,"But I noticed where Cupid's arrow fell. It fell on a little western flower, which used to be milk-white but is now purple from love's wound. Girls call it love-in-idleness. Fetch me that flower, the herb I once showed you. Its juice, put on sleeping eyelids, will make a man or woman fall madly in love with the next living creature they see. Fetch me this herb, and be back before a whale can swim three miles."]],"152":[[0,10,"Please give it to me. I know a bank where wild thyme blooms, where oxlips and nodding violets grow, all roofed over with rich honeysuckle, sweet musk roses and wild roses. Titania sleeps there for part of the night, lulled to sleep among these flowers by dancing and pleasure. There the snake sheds its shiny skin, a garment wide enough to wrap a fairy in. I'll smear her eyes with the juice of this flower and fill her head with horrible fantasies."],[11,19,"Take some of it and search through this wood. A sweet Athenian lady is in love with a young man who scorns her. Put some on his eyes, but do it when the next thing he sees will be the lady. You'll recognise the man by his Athenian clothes. Do it carefully, so that he ends up more in love with her than she is with him. And make sure you meet me before the first cock crows."]],"180":[[0,9,"I've been through the whole forest, but I haven't found any Athenian on whose eyes I could test this flower's power to stir up love. Night and silence! Who's this? He's wearing Athenian clothes. This is the one my master said scorned the Athenian girl. And here is the girl, fast asleep on the damp, dirty ground."],[10,17,"Pretty thing, she didn't dare lie near this loveless, rude man. You lout, on your eyes I pour all the power this charm has. When you wake, let love keep sleep from ever settling on your eyelids again. So wake up when I've gone, because I must go back to Oberon now."]],"188":[[0,11,"Oh, I'm out of breath from this foolish chase! The more I beg, the less I get. Hermia is lucky, wherever she is, because she has beautiful, attractive eyes. How did her eyes become so bright? Not with salty tears. If they did, my eyes are washed more often than hers. No, no, I'm as ugly as a bear, because animals that meet me run away in fear. So it's no wonder Demetrius runs from me as if I were a monster. What lying, wicked mirror made me think I could compare with Hermia's starry eyes?"],[12,14,"But who's this? Lysander, on the ground! Dead or asleep? I can't see any blood or wound. Lysander, if you're alive, good sir, wake up."]],"191":[[0,5,"Happy with Hermia? No, I regret the boring minutes I've spent with her. It's not Hermia but Helena I love. Who wouldn't swap a raven for a dove? A man's desires are ruled by his reason, and reason says you are the better woman."],[6,11,"Things that grow aren't ripe until their season comes, so I, being young, wasn't ripe for reason until now. Now that I've reached the peak of human judgement, reason commands my desire and leads me to your eyes, where I read love's stories, written in love's richest book."]],"192":[[0,7,"Why was I born to be mocked so cruelly? What have I ever done to deserve such contempt from you? Isn't it enough, isn't it enough, young man, that I never have and never can earn one kind look from Demetrius, without you making fun of how I fall short? Honestly, you do me wrong, truly you do, to court me in such a sneering way."],[8,11,"But goodbye. I have to admit I thought you were a more genuine gentleman. Oh, that a lady rejected by one man should be insulted by another because of it!"]],"196":[[0,5,"Help me, Lysander, help me! Do your best to pull this crawling snake off my breast! Oh, have pity on me! What a dream that was! Lysander, look how I'm shaking with fear. I dreamed a snake was eating my heart, and you sat smiling while it attacked me."],[6,11,"Lysander! What, gone? Lysander! My lord! What, out of earshot? Gone? No sound, no word? Oh no, where are you? Speak, if you can hear me. Speak, for love's sake! I'm almost fainting with fear. No? Then I can see you're not nearby. I'll find either you or my death straight away."]],"286":[[0,11,"My mistress is in love with a monster. Near her private, sacred bower, while she was sleeping, a gang of fools, rough workmen who earn their bread in the Athenian market, met to rehearse a play they are planning for great Theseus's wedding day. The stupidest blockhead of that brainless lot, who played Pyramus in their show, left his scene and went into a thicket. When I caught him there, I fixed an ass's head on him."],[12,20,"Soon he had to answer his Thisbe, and out comes my actor. When they saw him, like wild geese that spot the hunter creeping up, or a big flock of grey-headed jackdaws rising and cawing at the sound of a gun and scattering wildly across the sky, his friends ran away at the sight of him. And when I stamped my foot, one after another fell over. One shouted 'Murder!' and called to Athens for help."],[21,28,"Their wits were so weak, and their fears so strong, that lifeless things began to attack them: briars and thorns snatched at their clothes, a sleeve here, a hat there. Everything grabs from a man who gives in. I led them on in this crazy fear, and left sweet Pyramus, transformed, behind. At that very moment, as it happened, Titania woke and immediately fell in love with an ass."]],"293":[[0,4,"For now I'm only scolding you, but I ought to treat you worse, because I'm afraid you've given me reason to curse you. If you've killed Lysander in his sleep, you're already ankle-deep in blood, so wade in deeper and kill me too."],[5,12,"The sun was never as faithful to the day as he was to me. Would he have crept away from Hermia while she was asleep? I'd sooner believe that a hole could be drilled through the earth, and that the moon could creep through it and annoy her brother the sun at noon on the other side of the world. You must have murdered him. That's how a murderer looks, so deathly pale, so grim."]],"322":[[0,9,"How cruel! What hell! I can see you're all determined to gang up on me for your entertainment. If you were decent and had any manners, you wouldn't hurt me like this. Isn't it enough to hate me, as I know you do, without joining forces to mock me as well? If you were real men, as you look like men, you wouldn't treat a gentle lady like this: promising, swearing and overpraising my looks, when I'm sure you hate me with all your hearts."],[10,16,"You're both rivals in love with Hermia, and now you're rivals in mocking Helena. A fine achievement, a manly deed, to bring tears to a poor girl's eyes with your scorn! No one of noble birth would insult a girl like that and push a poor soul's patience to breaking point, just to amuse yourselves."]],"334":[[0,10,"Look, she's part of this plot too! Now I see they've all three joined together to set up this cruel game to spite me. Hurtful Hermia, most ungrateful girl! Have you plotted, have you schemed with these men, to torment me with this foul mockery? Have all the secrets we two shared, the vows we made as sisters, the hours we spent together, when we scolded time for hurrying to part us, oh, have they all been forgotten? All our schooldays' friendship, our childhood innocence?"],[11,22,"Hermia, we were like two creative gods. With our needles we both made one flower, both sewing on one sampler, sitting on one cushion, both singing one song, both in the same key, as if our hands, our bodies, voices and minds were all one. So we grew up together like a double cherry, which looks like two but is joined though divided, two lovely berries growing on one stem. So we were two bodies with one heart, like the two halves of a coat of arms that belong to one family and are topped by one crest."],[23,27,"And will you tear our old love apart, to join with men in mocking your poor friend? It isn't friendly. It isn't the way a girl should behave. All women, as well as me, could blame you for it, though I am the only one who feels the hurt."]],"386":[[0,11,"You can see these lovers are looking for a place to fight. So hurry, Robin, and cloud over the night. Cover the starry sky straight away with a low fog as black as hell's river, and lead these angry rivals so far astray that neither can find the other. Sometimes imitate Lysander's voice and stir Demetrius up with cruel insults, and sometimes rant like Demetrius. Lead them away from each other like this until sleep, which looks like death, creeps over their foreheads with legs as heavy as lead and wings like a bat's."],[12,19,"Then squeeze this herb into Lysander's eye. Its juice has the power to take away all his error and make his eyes see as they used to. When they next wake, all this foolishness will seem like a dream, an empty vision, and the lovers will go back to Athens, joined in a bond that will last until death."],[20,23,"While I give you this job, I'll go to my queen and ask her for her Indian boy. And then I'll free her bewitched eyes from the sight of that monster, and everything will be peaceful."]],"445":[[0,10,"Welcome, good Robin. Can you see this sweet sight? I'm beginning to pity her foolish love now. Because when I met her just now behind the wood, looking for sweet presents for this hateful fool, I told her off and quarrelled with her. She had put a crown of fresh, sweet-smelling flowers round his hairy head, and the dew that used to swell on the buds like round, shining pearls was sitting in the eyes of the pretty little flowers like tears crying over their own disgrace."],[11,17,"When I had teased her as much as I liked, and she had gently begged me to be patient, I asked her for the stolen child. She gave him to me straight away, and sent her fairy to carry him to my bower in fairyland. And now that I have the boy, I'll undo this hateful fault in her eyes."],[18,24,"And, gentle Puck, take this transformed head off this Athenian peasant, so that when he wakes up with the others, they can all go back to Athens and think of this night's events as nothing more than the wild torment of a dream. But first I'll free the Fairy Queen."]],"478":[[0,10,"My lord, beautiful Helena told me about their escape, about their plan to come here to this wood, and I followed them here in a rage, with beautiful Helena following me out of love. But, my good lord, I don't know by what power (though it must be some power) my love for Hermia has melted like snow, and now seems like the memory of some worthless toy I was obsessed with when I was a child. All the faithfulness and strength of my heart, the object and the delight of my eyes,"],[11,16,"is Helena alone. I was engaged to her, my lord, before I saw Hermia. But, like someone who is ill, I hated this food. Now, as if I were well again, my natural taste has come back. Now I want it, love it, long for it, and I will be true to it for ever."]],"490":[[0,5,"When my cue comes, call me and I'll answer. My next line is 'Most fair Pyramus.' Hey there! Peter Quince! Flute, the bellows-mender! Snout, the tinker! Starveling! God help me! They've sneaked off and left me asleep! I've had a most amazing vision. I've had a dream that no man has the brains to explain. A man would be an ass if he tried to explain this dream. I thought I was... no one can say what."],[6,13,"I thought I was, and I thought I had... but a man would be a patched-up fool if he tried to say what I thought I had. The eye of man has never heard, the ear of man has never seen, man's hand can't taste, his tongue can't imagine, and his heart can't tell what my dream was. I'll get Peter Quince to write a ballad about this dream. It will be called 'Bottom's Dream', because it has no bottom. And I'll sing it at the end of a play, in front of the Duke. Perhaps, to make it even better, I'll sing it when Thisbe dies."]],"514":[[0,4,"More strange than true. I can never believe these old legends or these silly fairy stories. Lovers and madmen have such boiling brains, such inventive imaginations, that they see more than calm reason can ever understand."],[5,15,"The madman, the lover and the poet are all made entirely of imagination. One sees more devils than huge hell could hold: that's the madman. The lover, just as crazy, sees the beauty of Helen of Troy in a gypsy's face. The poet's eye, rolling in a fine frenzy, glances from heaven to earth and from earth to heaven, and as imagination gives body to things nobody knows, the poet's pen turns them into shapes and gives airy nothing a home and a name."],[16,20,"Strong imagination plays such tricks that if it only feels some joy, it invents someone who brought that joy. Or at night, imagining something frightening, how easily a bush is taken for a bear!"]],"524":[[0,7,"[He reads] 'The battle with the Centaurs, to be sung by an Athenian eunuch to the harp.' We won't have that. I've already told my love that story, in honour of my relative Hercules. 'The riot of the drunken followers of Bacchus, tearing Orpheus, the singer of Thrace, to pieces in their frenzy.' That's an old show. It was performed the last time I came back from Thebes as a conqueror."],[8,16,"'The nine Muses mourning the death of Learning, who recently died in poverty.' That's some satire, sharp and critical, not suitable for a wedding. 'A boring short scene of young Pyramus and his love Thisbe, very tragic comedy.' Comic and tragic? Boring and short? That's like hot ice and amazingly strange snow. How can we find harmony in this contradiction?"]],"535":[[0,3,"Then we are all the kinder to thank them for nothing. Our fun will be to accept their mistakes kindly. When poor, dutiful people can't do something well, a noble mind values the effort, not the result."],[4,16,"Wherever I have been, great scholars have planned to greet me with prepared speeches of welcome. I've seen them tremble and go pale, stop in the middle of sentences, choke on their rehearsed words in fear, and in the end break off speechless, without welcoming me at all. Believe me, sweetheart, I still found a welcome in their silence, and in the modesty of their nervous duty I read as much as in the rattling tongue of cheeky, confident eloquence. So love and tongue-tied simplicity, saying least, say most to me."]],"546":[[0,11,"Ladies and gentlemen, maybe you're puzzled by this show, but keep wondering until the truth makes everything clear. This man is Pyramus, if you want to know. This beautiful lady is Thisbe, of course. This man, with plaster and gravel, represents Wall, that horrible wall which kept these lovers apart, and they are glad, poor things, to whisper through Wall's crack, which no one should be surprised at. This man, with a lantern, a dog and a bundle of thorn twigs, represents Moonshine, because, if you want to know, these lovers were not ashamed to meet by moonlight at Ninus's tomb, there, there to court each other."],[12,21,"This horrible beast, who goes by the name of Lion, scared away, or rather frightened, faithful Thisbe when she arrived first at night. And as she ran away she dropped her cloak, which vile Lion stained with his bloody mouth. Soon Pyramus arrives, a sweet, tall young man, and finds his faithful Thisbe's cloak 'killed'. At this, with his blade, his bloody, blameworthy blade, he bravely broached his boiling, bloody breast. And Thisbe, waiting in the shade of the mulberry tree,"],[22,24,"drew his dagger and died. For all the rest, let Lion, Moonshine, Wall and the two lovers tell you everything while they're here."]],"611":[[0,3,"Sweet Moon, I thank you for your sunny beams. I thank you, Moon, for shining so brightly now, because by your gracious, golden, glittering gleams I hope to get a sight of my most faithful Thisbe."],[4,15,"But wait! Oh, cruel! But look, poor knight, what dreadful grief is here! Eyes, do you see? How can it be? Oh dainty duck! Oh dear! Your good cloak, what, stained with blood? Come here, you cruel Furies! Oh Fates, come, come, cut the thread of my life, thread and fringe, crush, destroy, finish and kill!"]],"614":[[0,3,"Oh, why, Nature, did you make lions, since a vile lion has 'deflowered' my dear here? She is (no, no) she was the most beautiful lady who ever lived, loved, liked, or looked cheerful."],[4,15,"Come, tears, destroy me! Out, sword, and wound the breast of Pyramus. Yes, that left breast, where the heart hops. So I die, like this, this, this. Now I am dead, now I have fled, my soul is in the sky. Tongue, lose your light! Moon, fly away! Now die, die, die, die, die."]],"627":[[0,11,"Asleep, my love? What, dead, my dove? Oh Pyramus, get up! Speak, speak. Quite silent? Dead, dead? A tomb must cover your sweet eyes. These lily-white lips, this cherry-red nose, these yellow cowslip cheeks are gone, are gone! Lovers, moan. His eyes were as green as leeks."],[12,23,"Oh Three Sisters of Fate, come, come to me with hands as pale as milk. Dip them in blood, since you have cut his silken thread of life with your scissors. Tongue, not a word. Come, trusty sword. Come, blade, soak my breast in blood. And farewell, friends. This is how Thisbe ends. Goodbye, goodbye, goodbye."]],"637":[[0,11,"Now the hungry lion roars and the wolf howls at the moon, while the tired ploughman snores, worn out by his hard work. Now the burnt-out logs glow, while the screech owl, screeching loudly, makes the miserable person lying ill think of his burial shroud. Now it is the time of night when the graves all gape wide, and every one lets out its ghost to glide along the paths to the church."],[12,19,"And we fairies, who run beside the chariot of Hecate, goddess of the moon and the underworld, away from the sun, following darkness like a dream, are now ready for fun. Not a mouse will disturb this blessed house. I've been sent ahead with a broom to sweep the dust from behind the door."]],"642":[[0,13,"Now, until daybreak, let every fairy wander through this house. We will go to the best bridal bed, which we will bless, and the children made there will always be lucky. So all three couples will always be faithful in love, and Nature's flaws will not appear in their children. No mole, cleft lip or scar, no unnatural birthmark of the kind people hate to see in a newborn baby, will be on their children."],[14,21,"With this blessed dew from the fields, let every fairy go on its way and bless each room in this palace with sweet peace, and bless the owner too. It will always rest in safety. Skip away. Don't stay. Meet me, all of you, by daybreak."]],"644":[[0,7,"If we shadows have offended you, just think this, and everything is put right: that you have only been asleep here while these visions appeared. And this weak and silly story, which is no more than a dream, ladies and gentlemen, don't criticise it. If you forgive us, we'll do better."],[8,15,"And as I am an honest Puck, if we are lucky enough, though we don't deserve it, to escape your hisses, we'll make it up to you soon, or else call Puck a liar. So, good night to you all. Give me your applause, if we're friends, and Robin will make amends."]]};
const QUOTES={"themes":{"love":["Love and desire","Love as blind, irrational and changeable, from Helena's devotion to the juice that switches hearts overnight."],"reason":["Love and reason","Whether love has anything to do with judgement, and how the lovers pretend it does."],"dreams":["Dreams and imagination","Visions, sleep, waking and the power of imagination, in lovers, madmen, poets and audiences."],"power":["Law, power and authority","Fathers, dukes and fairy kings: who has the right to control whom, and how that power is softened."],"gender":["Men and women","Daughters, wives and friends: how women resist, obey or are made to give way."],"nature":["Nature and the fairies","The wood, the moon, the seasons and the fairy world that shapes them."],"theatre":["Theatre and performance","Plays within plays, actors as shadows and an audience asked to imagine."],"change":["Transformation","Bottom's ass's head, switched loves, changed friendships: things 'translated'."]},"quotes":[{"id":"2","who":"THESEUS","text":"but oh, methinks, how slow This old moon wanes!","themes":["nature","love"],"note":"Theseus is impatient for his wedding; the moon marks time from the first line."},{"id":"6","who":"THESEUS","text":"Hippolyta, I woo'd thee with my sword, And won thy love doing thee injuries;","themes":["power","gender","love"],"note":"Theseus admits that he won his bride through war."},{"id":"10","who":"EGEUS","text":"As she is mine I may dispose of her;","themes":["power","gender"],"note":"Egeus treats his daughter as his property."},{"id":"11","who":"THESEUS","text":"To whom you are but as a form in wax By him imprinted, and within his power To leave the figure, or disfigure it.","themes":["power","gender","change"],"note":"Theseus's image of a daughter shaped by her father like wax."},{"id":"14","who":"HERMIA","text":"I would my father look'd but with my eyes.","themes":["love","reason"],"note":"Hermia wishes her father could see Lysander as she does."},{"id":"17","who":"THESEUS","text":"But earthlier happy is the rose distill'd Than that which, withering on the virgin thorn, Grows, lives, and dies, in single blessedness.","themes":["gender","nature","love"],"note":"Theseus argues that marriage is happier than a nun's life."},{"id":"18","who":"HERMIA","text":"Ere I will yield my virgin patent up Unto his lordship, whose unwishèd yoke My soul consents not to give sovereignty.","themes":["gender","power","love"],"note":"Hermia defies the Duke and her father."},{"id":"29","who":"LYSANDER","text":"The course of true love never did run smooth.","themes":["love"],"note":"Lysander comforts Hermia with the lessons of old stories."},{"id":"35","who":"LYSANDER","text":"So quick bright things come to confusion.","themes":["love","nature"],"note":"Love is as brief as a flash of lightning."},{"id":"58","who":"HELENA","text":"Love looks not with the eyes, but with the mind; And therefore is wing'd Cupid painted blind.","themes":["love","reason"],"note":"Helena explains why love cannot judge clearly."},{"id":"58","who":"HELENA","text":"Things base and vile, holding no quantity, Love can transpose to form and dignity.","themes":["love","change"],"note":"Love can turn worthless things into something fine."},{"id":"94","who":"BOTTOM","text":"I will roar you as gently as any sucking dove; I will roar you an 'twere any nightingale.","themes":["theatre"],"note":"Bottom promises not to frighten the ladies."},{"id":"107","who":"FAIRY","text":"Over hill, over dale, Thorough bush, thorough brier,","themes":["nature"],"note":"The fairy describes her travels in quick, light rhythm."},{"id":"110","who":"PUCK","text":"I am that merry wanderer of the night.","themes":["nature","change"],"note":"Puck introduces himself."},{"id":"113","who":"OBERON","text":"Ill met by moonlight, proud Titania.","themes":["nature","power"],"note":"Oberon's first words to his queen."},{"id":"118","who":"TITANIA","text":"The seasons alter: hoary-headed frosts Fall in the fresh lap of the crimson rose;","themes":["nature","change"],"note":"Titania describes the effect of their quarrel on the weather."},{"id":"118","who":"TITANIA","text":"And this same progeny of evils comes From our debate, from our dissension; We are their parents and original.","themes":["nature","power"],"note":"Titania admits the fairies are to blame."},{"id":"120","who":"TITANIA","text":"And for her sake do I rear up her boy, And for her sake I will not part with him.","themes":["love","gender","power"],"note":"Titania's loyalty to her dead friend."},{"id":"128","who":"OBERON","text":"And the imperial votress passed on, In maiden meditation, fancy-free.","themes":["love","power"],"note":"Cupid's arrow misses a virgin queen in the west."},{"id":"128","who":"OBERON","text":"Will make or man or woman madly dote Upon the next live creature that it sees.","themes":["love","change","reason"],"note":"Oberon explains the flower's power."},{"id":"129","who":"PUCK","text":"I'll put a girdle round about the earth In forty minutes.","themes":["nature"],"note":"Puck boasts of his speed."},{"id":"136","who":"HELENA","text":"I am your spaniel; and, Demetrius, The more you beat me, I will fawn on you.","themes":["love","gender"],"note":"Helena begs to be treated like a dog."},{"id":"144","who":"HELENA","text":"We cannot fight for love as men may do. We should be woo'd, and were not made to woo.","themes":["gender","love"],"note":"Helena knows she is breaking the rules for women."},{"id":"152","who":"OBERON","text":"I know a bank where the wild thyme blows, Where oxlips and the nodding violet grows,","themes":["nature"],"note":"Oberon describes Titania's sleeping place."},{"id":"152","who":"OBERON","text":"Thou shalt know the man By the Athenian garments he hath on.","themes":["change","love"],"note":"Oberon's vague instructions to Puck."},{"id":"167","who":"OBERON","text":"Wake when some vile thing is near.","themes":["power","change"],"note":"Oberon's curse on Titania."},{"id":"191","who":"LYSANDER","text":"Who will not change a raven for a dove?","themes":["love","change","reason"],"note":"Lysander, under the spell, compares dark Hermia to a raven."},{"id":"191","who":"LYSANDER","text":"The will of man is by his reason sway'd, And reason says you are the worthier maid.","themes":["reason","love"],"note":"Lysander claims his new love is rational."},{"id":"196","who":"HERMIA","text":"Methought a serpent eat my heart away, And you sat smiling at his cruel prey.","themes":["dreams","love"],"note":"Hermia's nightmare."},{"id":"226","who":"PUCK","text":"What hempen homespuns have we swaggering here, So near the cradle of the Fairy Queen?","themes":["theatre","nature"],"note":"Puck finds the workmen rehearsing."},{"id":"251","who":"QUINCE","text":"Bless thee, Bottom! bless thee! Thou art translated.","themes":["change"],"note":"Quince sees Bottom's ass's head."},{"id":"257","who":"BOTTOM","text":"And yet, to say the truth, reason and love keep little company together nowadays.","themes":["reason","love"],"note":"Bottom answers Titania's declaration of love."},{"id":"258","who":"TITANIA","text":"Thou art as wise as thou art beautiful.","themes":["love","change"],"note":"Titania praises Bottom."},{"id":"260","who":"TITANIA","text":"And I will purge thy mortal grossness so That thou shalt like an airy spirit go.","themes":["change","nature","love"],"note":"Titania offers to make Bottom a spirit."},{"id":"286","who":"PUCK","text":"My mistress with a monster is in love.","themes":["love","change"],"note":"Puck's report to Oberon."},{"id":"286","who":"PUCK","text":"A crew of patches, rude mechanicals, That work for bread upon Athenian stalls,","themes":["theatre","power"],"note":"Puck's scornful description of the workmen."},{"id":"312","who":"PUCK","text":"Lord, what fools these mortals be!","themes":["love","theatre"],"note":"Puck watches the lovers."},{"id":"321","who":"DEMETRIUS","text":"O Helen, goddess, nymph, perfect, divine! To what, my love, shall I compare thine eyne? Crystal is muddy.","themes":["love","reason"],"note":"Demetrius wakes in love with Helena."},{"id":"334","who":"HELENA","text":"So we grew together, Like to a double cherry, seeming parted, But yet a union in partition,","themes":["love","gender","change"],"note":"Helena remembers her friendship with Hermia."},{"id":"372","who":"HELENA","text":"And though she be but little, she is fierce.","themes":["gender","change"],"note":"Helena on Hermia's temper."},{"id":"362","who":"HERMIA","text":"How low am I, thou painted maypole? Speak,","themes":["gender","love"],"note":"Hermia turns on Helena."},{"id":"386","who":"OBERON","text":"When they next wake, all this derision Shall seem a dream and fruitless vision;","themes":["dreams","power"],"note":"Oberon plans how the night will be remembered."},{"id":"388","who":"OBERON","text":"But we are spirits of another sort:","themes":["nature","dreams"],"note":"Oberon separates the fairies from ghosts."},{"id":"421","who":"PUCK","text":"Jack shall have Jill; Nought shall go ill;","themes":["love","power"],"note":"Puck's charm promises a happy ending."},{"id":"435","who":"BOTTOM","text":"I am such a tender ass, if my hair do but tickle me, I must scratch.","themes":["change","love"],"note":"Bottom in Titania's bower."},{"id":"448","who":"TITANIA","text":"My Oberon, what visions have I seen! Methought I was enamour'd of an ass.","themes":["dreams","change","love"],"note":"Titania wakes from the spell."},{"id":"465","who":"HIPPOLYTA","text":"I never heard So musical a discord, such sweet thunder.","themes":["nature","theatre"],"note":"Hippolyta on the hunting hounds."},{"id":"479","who":"THESEUS","text":"Egeus, I will overbear your will;","themes":["power","love"],"note":"Theseus overrules Hermia's father."},{"id":"483","who":"HELENA","text":"And I have found Demetrius like a jewel, Mine own, and not mine own.","themes":["love","dreams","change"],"note":"Helena can hardly believe her luck."},{"id":"484","who":"DEMETRIUS","text":"Are you sure That we are awake? It seems to me That yet we sleep, we dream.","themes":["dreams"],"note":"Demetrius on waking."},{"id":"490","who":"BOTTOM","text":"I have had a most rare vision. I have had a dream, past the wit of man to say what dream it was.","themes":["dreams","change"],"note":"Bottom wakes."},{"id":"490","who":"BOTTOM","text":"The eye of man hath not heard, the ear of man hath not seen, man's hand is not able to taste, his tongue to conceive, nor his heart to report, what my dream was.","themes":["dreams","theatre"],"note":"Bottom mixes up the senses."},{"id":"490","who":"BOTTOM","text":"it shall be called 'Bottom's Dream', because it hath no bottom;","themes":["dreams","theatre"],"note":"Bottom names his ballad."},{"id":"514","who":"THESEUS","text":"More strange than true. I never may believe These antique fables, nor these fairy toys.","themes":["dreams","reason"],"note":"Theseus dismisses the lovers' story."},{"id":"514","who":"THESEUS","text":"The lunatic, the lover, and the poet Are of imagination all compact:","themes":["dreams","reason","love"],"note":"Theseus links madness, love and poetry."},{"id":"514","who":"THESEUS","text":"and gives to airy nothing A local habitation and a name.","themes":["dreams","theatre"],"note":"The poet gives imagination a real shape."},{"id":"514","who":"THESEUS","text":"Or in the night, imagining some fear, How easy is a bush supposed a bear?","themes":["dreams","reason"],"note":"Fear makes us see things."},{"id":"515","who":"HIPPOLYTA","text":"And grows to something of great constancy; But, howsoever, strange and admirable.","themes":["dreams","reason"],"note":"Hippolyta answers Theseus."},{"id":"524","who":"THESEUS","text":"Merry and tragical? Tedious and brief? That is hot ice and wondrous strange snow.","themes":["theatre"],"note":"Theseus reads the title of the workmen's play."},{"id":"530","who":"THESEUS","text":"For never anything can be amiss When simpleness and duty tender it.","themes":["theatre","power"],"note":"Theseus chooses the workmen's play."},{"id":"577","who":"THESEUS","text":"The best in this kind are but shadows; and the worst are no worse, if imagination amend them.","themes":["theatre","dreams"],"note":"Theseus on actors."},{"id":"576","who":"HIPPOLYTA","text":"This is the silliest stuff that ever I heard.","themes":["theatre"],"note":"Hippolyta's verdict on the play."},{"id":"614","who":"PYRAMUS","text":"Thus die I, thus, thus, thus. Now am I dead, Now am I fled; My soul is in the sky.","themes":["theatre","love"],"note":"Pyramus's long death."},{"id":"627","who":"THISBE","text":"His eyes were green as leeks.","themes":["theatre","love"],"note":"Thisbe mourns Pyramus."},{"id":"634","who":"THESEUS","text":"The iron tongue of midnight hath told twelve. Lovers, to bed; 'tis almost fairy time.","themes":["nature","love"],"note":"Theseus ends the day."},{"id":"642","who":"OBERON","text":"So shall all the couples three Ever true in loving be;","themes":["love","nature"],"note":"Oberon's blessing."},{"id":"644","who":"PUCK","text":"If we shadows have offended, Think but this, and all is mended,","themes":["theatre","dreams"],"note":"Puck's epilogue."},{"id":"644","who":"PUCK","text":"And this weak and idle theme, No more yielding but a dream,","themes":["theatre","dreams"],"note":"Puck apologises for the play."}]};
const EX={"intro":["Theseus, Duke of Athens, is about to marry Hippolyta, queen of the Amazons. Egeus demands that his daughter Hermia marry Demetrius; she loves Lysander, and the law says she must obey, die or become a nun. Her friend Helena loves Demetrius, who has abandoned her.","Hermia and Lysander run away into the wood at night, followed by Demetrius and then Helena. In the same wood a group of Athenian workmen are rehearsing a play for the Duke's wedding, and the fairy king and queen, Oberon and Titania, are quarrelling over a stolen boy.","Oberon uses a magic flower to make Titania fall in love with whatever she sees on waking, and sends his servant Puck to help Helena with the same juice. Puck's mistakes, and his trick of giving the weaver Bottom an ass's head, turn the night into chaos."],"timeline":[{"s":"a1s1","t":"Egeus demands the law against Hermia","id":10},{"s":"a1s1","t":"Theseus gives Hermia until his wedding day","id":19},{"s":"a1s1","t":"Lysander and Hermia plan to run away","id":37},{"s":"a1s1","t":"Helena decides to tell Demetrius","id":58},{"s":"a1s2","t":"The workmen cast Pyramus and Thisbe","id":70},{"s":"a2s1","t":"Puck explains the fight over the changeling boy","id":108},{"s":"a2s1","t":"Oberon and Titania quarrel","id":113},{"s":"a2s1","t":"Oberon sends Puck for the magic flower","id":128},{"s":"a2s1","t":"Demetrius rejects Helena; Oberon decides to help her","id":152},{"s":"a2s2","t":"Oberon puts the juice on Titania's eyes","id":167},{"s":"a2s2","t":"Puck anoints Lysander by mistake","id":180},{"s":"a2s2","t":"Lysander wakes in love with Helena","id":189},{"s":"a2s2","t":"Hermia wakes alone from a nightmare","id":196},{"s":"a3s1","t":"Puck gives Bottom an ass's head","id":251},{"s":"a3s1","t":"Titania wakes in love with Bottom","id":256},{"s":"a3s2","t":"Oberon anoints Demetrius","id":310},{"s":"a3s2","t":"Both men woo Helena; she thinks they mock her","id":322},{"s":"a3s2","t":"Hermia and Helena quarrel","id":362},{"s":"a3s2","t":"Puck leads the rivals astray and cures Lysander","id":421},{"s":"a4s1","t":"Oberon frees Titania and they make peace","id":447},{"s":"a4s1","t":"Theseus overrules Egeus; the couples will marry","id":479},{"s":"a4s1","t":"Bottom wakes from his 'most rare vision'","id":490},{"s":"a5s1","t":"Theseus chooses the workmen's play","id":530},{"s":"a5s1","t":"Pyramus and Thisbe die at great length","id":614},{"s":"a5s1","t":"The fairies bless the house; Puck's epilogue","id":644}],"irony":{"38":"Hermia jokes that men break more vows than women ever make; within a day Lysander will break his.","51":"Hermia says Helena will never be bothered by Demetrius again; soon two men will be chasing Helena.","58":"Helena says love can make 'things base and vile' seem beautiful; Titania will love an ass.","94":"Bottom fears frightening the ladies with a lion, while real fairy magic is about to frighten his friends with him.","152":"Oberon tells Puck to recognise the man by his Athenian clothes; there are two Athenian young men in the wood.","174":"Lysander swears his heart is knit to Hermia's for ever; within minutes it will belong to Helena.","191":"Lysander says reason now rules his love; the audience knows it is the love-juice.","257":"Bottom says reason and love keep little company; he is talking to a queen who loves him because of a spell.","258":"Titania calls Bottom wise and beautiful while he wears an ass's head.","321":"Demetrius praises Helena's eyes in the same extravagant terms he would once have used of Hermia's.","514":"Theseus dismisses fairy stories, but the audience has seen the fairies, and they will soon bless his house.","577":"Theseus calls actors shadows that need imagination, while he is himself an actor in a play."},"devices":{"moon":"The moon","eyes":"Eyes and seeing","dream":"Dreams and sleep","flowers":"Flowers and plants","beasts":"Animals and monsters","opposites":"Opposites and contradictions"},"dev":{"2":[["moon","The old moon wanes too slowly for Theseus, like a widow living on.","how slow This old moon wanes"]],"3":[["moon","The new moon as a silver bow bent in the sky.","like to a silver bow New bent in heaven"]],"14":[["eyes","Hermia wants her father to see with her eyes.","look'd but with my eyes"]],"17":[["moon","Diana's moon is cold and fruitless, the life of a nun.","the cold fruitless moon"],["flowers","A distilled rose (a married woman) is happier than one withering on the stem.","the rose distill'd"]],"35":[["opposites","Love is bright and quick, then swallowed by darkness.","quick bright things come to confusion"]],"52":[["moon","Phoebe, the moon, sees her face in the water.","when Phoebe doth behold Her silver visage"]],"58":[["eyes","Love sees with the mind, so Cupid is blind.","Love looks not with the eyes"]],"94":[["opposites","Bottom will roar as gently as a dove.","roar you as gently as any sucking dove"]],"118":[["flowers","Frost falls on the rose; summer buds crown Winter.","the fresh lap of the crimson rose"],["opposites","The seasons have swapped clothes.","change Their wonted liveries"]],"128":[["flowers","Cupid's arrow turns a white flower purple.","a little western flower"],["moon","The moon's chaste beams put out Cupid's fire.","the chaste beams of the watery moon"]],"136":[["beasts","Helena begs to be Demetrius's spaniel.","I am your spaniel"]],"142":[["beasts","Gentle creatures chase fierce ones.","The dove pursues the griffin"]],"167":[["beasts","Oberon lists the wild animals Titania might love.","ounce, or cat, or bear"]],"191":[["beasts","Hermia is now a raven, Helena a dove.","change a raven for a dove"]],"196":[["dream","Hermia's dream of a serpent eating her heart.","a serpent eat my heart away"],["beasts","The serpent stands for Lysander's betrayal.","this crawling serpent"]],"251":[["beasts","Bottom is 'translated' into an ass.","Thou art translated"]],"257":[["opposites","Reason and love do not keep company.","reason and love keep little company"]],"286":[["beasts","The workmen scatter like wild geese and jackdaws.","As wild geese that the creeping fowler eye"]],"321":[["eyes","Helena's eyes outshine crystal.","shall I compare thine eyne"]],"334":[["flowers","Hermia and Helena as a double cherry on one stem.","Like to a double cherry"]],"386":[["dream","The night will seem a dream.","a dream and fruitless vision"]],"442":[["flowers","Titania wraps Bottom like honeysuckle and ivy.","So doth the woodbine the sweet honeysuckle"]],"465":[["opposites","Barking hounds as sweet thunder.","So musical a discord, such sweet thunder"]],"490":[["dream","Bottom's dream defeats the senses.","I have had a most rare vision"]],"514":[["dream","Imagination shapes airy nothing.","imagination bodies forth"]],"524":[["opposites","A tragic comedy is like hot ice.","hot ice and wondrous strange snow"]],"591":[["moon","Moonshine plays the man in the moon with a lantern.","the man i' the moon"]],"644":[["dream","The play itself becomes the audience's dream.","No more yielding but a dream"]]},"stage":{"palace":"Theseus's palace","cottage":"A workman's house","wood":"The wood near Athens"},"why":{"0":"Introduces the moon, which governs the timing, mood and imagery of the whole play.","1":"Shows that even the play's model marriage began with conquest, which colours how we see male power.","2":"The clearest statement of the patriarchal law that sends the lovers into the wood.","3":"Shows the power of fathers in Athens, and that Theseus at first supports it.","4":"Begins the play's long thread about eyes, sight and how love sees.","5":"A picture of what is at stake for Hermia, and of the play's preference for marriage and fertility.","6":"Hermia's courage: she would rather die or be a nun than marry against her will.","7":"The most famous line of the play: it states the pattern of every plot in it.","8":"Anticipates the tragic version of the story, played for laughs in Pyramus and Thisbe.","9":"The key idea of the play: love is not based on what people really are.","10":"Comes true literally when Titania adores Bottom with an ass's head.","11":"Shows Bottom's comic confidence and the workmen's fear that the audience cannot tell art from life.","12":"The fairies' short, rhyming lines sound different from both the court's verse and the workmen's prose.","13":"Sets up Puck as a mischief-maker who will turn the night upside down.","14":"The fairy marriage is at war, mirroring the human conflicts.","15":"Shows that disorder in love causes disorder in nature.","16":"The fairies have real power over the human world, which makes their meddling matter.","17":"Gives Titania a sympathetic reason for refusing Oberon, which makes his revenge harder to like.","18":"Often read as a compliment to Queen Elizabeth I, the one person love's arrow could not touch.","19":"The magic that drives the plot treats love as a chemical accident.","20":"Shows the fairies' supernatural power and Puck's eager energy.","21":"Shows how humiliating unrequited love can be; many find it uncomfortable to watch.","22":"Draws attention to the different rules for men and women in love.","23":"Some of the play's most beautiful nature poetry, used to plan a cruel trick.","24":"Sets up the mistake: the two young men are almost interchangeable.","25":"Oberon wants to humiliate his wife, not just play a trick.","26":"His love has flipped completely, yet he claims reason guides him.","27":"Comic irony: the audience knows it is the juice, not reason.","28":"A dream that tells the truth about what has happened while she slept.","29":"Brings the workmen's world and the fairy world together.","30":"'Translated' means transformed: the play's most literal and comic transformation.","31":"The wisest comment on love in the play comes from a man with an ass's head.","32":"Love has made the Fairy Queen blind, exactly as Helena described.","33":"The meeting of the highest and the lowest, queen and weaver.","34":"Sums up Oberon's revenge in one line.","35":"The source of the name 'the mechanicals' and a sign of how the higher characters look down on them.","36":"The audience shares Puck's view of the lovers as a comic show.","37":"Love poetry turned into parody: the language is the same whatever its target.","38":"The women's friendship is the play's deepest bond, and the night breaks it.","39":"Comic insult that shows how far the friends have fallen out.","40":"The quarrel becomes physical comedy about height and looks.","41":"The fairies turn the night into a dream for the lovers, and the play does the same for the audience.","42":"Keeps the fairy world light and kind, not dark.","43":"The comic order is restored, couple by couple.","44":"Bottom stays completely himself in the strangest situation.","45":"Everyone who was enchanted remembers the night as a dream.","46":"Harmony out of discord is a picture of how the play's confusions end in order.","47":"The law that began the play is set aside so the comedy can end happily.","48":"She senses that Demetrius's love is real and yet strange, because it was made by magic.","49":"The boundary between dream and waking is blurred.","50":"Bottom's 'vision' is comic and yet sincere: he has touched something wonderful.","51":"A muddled echo of a Bible verse about wonders beyond human understanding.","52":"A pun: the dream is bottomless, beyond explanation.","53":"Dramatic irony: the audience has seen the fairies, so we know Theseus is wrong.","54":"The play's central statement about imagination, made by a sceptic in a poet's play.","55":"Describes exactly what Shakespeare has done in creating the fairies.","56":"A homely example that links to the lovers' fears in the dark wood.","57":"She trusts the lovers' shared story more than he does, and the audience knows she is right.","58":"The play-within-the-play is full of contradictions, like the comedy around it.","59":"Shows Theseus's generosity as a ruler towards his humble subjects.","60":"Asks the audience to use imagination, as Shakespeare's bare stage always did.","61":"The courtiers mock the play, though their own night was just as foolish.","62":"Parody of tragic deaths, and of what might have happened to Lysander and Hermia.","63":"Love poetry made ridiculous by the wrong comparisons.","64":"The human world gives way to the fairy world, which will bless the marriages.","65":"The fairies' power, which caused chaos, is now used to protect the marriages.","66":"The actors are 'shadows' and the play is a dream: the audience is invited to dream too.","67":"A modest ending that turns the whole play into the audience's dream."},"arc":{"Confusion":[3,2,6,7,8,10,6,2,3],"Harmony":[4,5,2,2,2,0,6,8,10]},"arcnote":"Confusion rises from the moment the lovers enter the wood and peaks in Act 3, Scene ii, when both men love Helena and the women quarrel. Harmony returns at dawn when Oberon undoes the spells and Theseus overrules Egeus, and is complete at the triple wedding and the fairies' blessing.","context":[["Date","Probably written between about 1594 and 1596, around the same time as Romeo and Juliet and Richard II. Pyramus and Thisbe, a tale of young lovers who die through a mistake, looks like a comic answer to Romeo and Juliet."],["Printing","First printed in 1600 in a quarto that says it had been 'sundry times publicly acted' by the Lord Chamberlain's Men, Shakespeare's company. It was reprinted in the First Folio of 1623."],["A wedding play?","Some scholars have suggested it was written for an aristocratic wedding, because it ends with three marriages and a blessing of the bridal beds. There is no firm evidence for any particular wedding."],["Sources","There is no single source. Theseus and Hippolyta come from Chaucer's Knight's Tale and Plutarch's Life of Theseus. Pyramus and Thisbe and Bottom's transformation draw on Ovid's Metamorphoses, which Shakespeare knew in Arthur Golding's translation of 1567. Oberon appears in the French romance Huon of Bordeaux; Titania is a name Ovid uses for Diana."],["Robin Goodfellow","Puck comes from English folk belief. A 'puck' was a mischievous spirit, and Robin Goodfellow was blamed for spoiled milk, failed butter and travellers lost at night. Reginald Scot's Discovery of Witchcraft (1584) mentions him as a figure servants were frightened with."],["Fairies","Elizabethans told stories of fairies who danced in rings, stole babies and left changelings. Shakespeare's fairies are smaller and gentler than most folk fairies, and later writers often copied his tiny, flower-named spirits."],["May Day and Midsummer","Theseus thinks the lovers rose early 'to observe the rite of May', when young people went into the woods at night. Midsummer Eve (23 June) was also linked with magic, love charms and madness. The title suggests a night of holiday licence."],["Fathers and daughters","In Elizabethan England fathers had great control over their daughters' marriages, especially among the wealthy, although the law did not let them put a daughter to death. Egeus's 'ancient privilege of Athens' exaggerates real paternal power."],["Queen Elizabeth","Elizabeth I, the unmarried 'Virgin Queen', was often praised as Diana or as a vestal. Oberon's 'fair vestal, thronèd by the west' whom Cupid's arrow cannot touch is usually read as a compliment to her."],["Amateur actors","Working men in towns put on religious and civic plays in Shakespeare's lifetime. The mechanicals' play makes fun of old-fashioned verse, clumsy staging and actors who fear the audience cannot tell a play from reality."],["Staging","Shakespeare's theatre had no lighting or scenery; plays were often performed in daylight. The audience had to imagine the moonlit wood, just as the mechanicals worry about bringing in moonlight. Boy actors played the women, which is why Flute must play Thisbe and why the jokes about Hermia's and Helena's heights may fit two particular boys."],["Later life","Samuel Pepys saw the play in 1662 and called it the most insipid, ridiculous play he had ever seen. Mendelssohn's music for it (1843) includes the Wedding March. Peter Brook's 1970 production, set in a white box with trapezes, changed how it is staged."]],"critics":{"dreams":[["William Hazlitt, Characters of Shakespear's Plays (1817)","Paraphrased: the play is a world of pure poetry and fancy, which is lost when it is put on a real stage; the fairies cannot be acted, only imagined."],["Frank Kermode, 'The Mature Comedies' (1961)","Paraphrased: the play is a serious exploration of love, vision and imagination, and Bottom's garbled echo of St Paul suggests that his foolish dream contains a real glimpse of the wonderful."]],"love":[["C. L. Barber, Shakespeare's Festive Comedy (1959)","Paraphrased: the play follows the pattern of a holiday: release into the misrule of the night wood, then a return to everyday order with clearer understanding."],["Harold Bloom, Shakespeare: The Invention of the Human (1998)","Paraphrased: Bottom is the play's real hero, warm, unflappable and good-natured, and his calm acceptance of Titania's love is more admirable than the lovers' frenzy."]],"gender":[["Louis Montrose, 'Shaping Fantasies' (1983)","Paraphrased: the play repeatedly shows women's independence (the Amazon queen, Titania, Hermia) being brought under male control, and it does this under a queen who ruled alone."]],"change":[["Jan Kott, Shakespeare Our Contemporary (1964)","Paraphrased: the play is darker and more erotic than its reputation; the night in the wood releases animal desire, and Titania's love for the ass is a nightmare as much as a joke."]],"theatre":[["Samuel Pepys, Diary (29 September 1662)","Paraphrased: he found it the most insipid, ridiculous play he had ever seen, though he enjoyed the dancing and the pretty women."]]},"insults":[{"id":21,"f":"Do you marry him","who":"Lysander","n":"Lysander mocks Demetrius: since Egeus loves him so much, let Egeus marry him."},{"id":23,"f":"this spotted and inconstant man","who":"Lysander (of Demetrius)","n":"Lysander's charge against Demetrius is about faithlessness, a fault Lysander will soon share."},{"id":107,"f":"thou lob of spirits","who":"Fairy","n":"The fairy calls Puck a clumsy lump, teasing the most famous spirit in England."},{"id":115,"f":"Tarry, rash wanton","who":"Oberon","n":"Oberon calls his queen headstrong and unfaithful: fairy marriage as a battle."},{"id":137,"f":"I am sick when I do look on thee","who":"Demetrius","n":"Demetrius's contempt for Helena is total."},{"id":180,"f":"this lack-love, this kill-courtesy","who":"Puck","n":"Puck invents insults for the man he thinks has scorned Helena; it is the wrong man."},{"id":226,"f":"hempen homespuns","who":"Puck","n":"Puck mocks the workmen's rough clothes and rustic swagger."},{"id":286,"f":"rude mechanicals","who":"Puck","n":"Rude means rough and uneducated, mechanicals means manual workers: a snobbish label that stuck."},{"id":297,"f":"Out, dog! Out, cur!","who":"Hermia","n":"Hermia, thinking Demetrius has killed Lysander, turns to animal insults."},{"id":348,"f":"Away, you Ethiope!","who":"Lysander","n":"Lysander mocks Hermia's dark looks; the insult depends on the period's prejudice about fair and dark skin."},{"id":352,"f":"Out, tawny Tartar, out!","who":"Lysander","n":"Another insult about Hermia's colouring, and he calls her a hated potion."},{"id":360,"f":"You juggler! You cankerblossom! You thief of love!","who":"Hermia","n":"Hermia accuses Helena of trickery and of eating away at her love like a grub."},{"id":361,"f":"you counterfeit, you puppet, you!","who":"Helena","n":"Helena means Hermia is a fake. Hermia hears 'puppet' as a joke about her height."},{"id":362,"f":"thou painted maypole","who":"Hermia","n":"Hermia attacks Helena's height and suggests she wears make-up."},{"id":374,"f":"You minimus, of hind'ring knot-grass made; You bead, you acorn","who":"Lysander","n":"Lysander piles up insults about Hermia's size."},{"id":399,"f":"Come, recreant, come, thou child!","who":"Puck (as Lysander)","n":"Puck imitates the young men's insults to keep them apart."}],"pronouns":[{"id":10,"f":"Thou, thou, Lysander, thou hast given her rhymes","n":"Egeus spits 'thou' at Lysander: anger and contempt."},{"id":11,"f":"What say you, Hermia? Be advis'd, fair maid.","n":"Theseus uses the polite 'you' to Hermia, formal and distant as a judge."},{"id":136,"f":"I am your spaniel","n":"Helena always says 'you' to Demetrius, respectful and humble; he answers with 'thee' and 'thou' to push her away."},{"id":174,"f":"I mean that my heart unto yours is knit","n":"Lysander and Hermia use 'you' to each other here, the polite form of courtship."},{"id":256,"f":"I pray thee, gentle mortal, sing again.","n":"Titania uses the loving 'thee' to Bottom; he politely answers 'you' and 'mistress'."},{"id":358,"f":"Am not I Hermia? Are not you Lysander?","n":"Hermia, bewildered, still uses 'you' to the man who now calls her 'thou' with scorn."},{"id":362,"f":"How low am I, thou painted maypole?","n":"Hermia switches to 'thou' when she loses her temper with Helena."}],"predict":{"a1s1":["Hermia obeys her father","Hermia and Lysander run away","Hermia becomes a nun"],"a1s2":["The workmen give up the play","They rehearse in the wood","Bottom gets every part"],"a2s1":["Titania gives up the boy","Oberon uses the flower on Titania","The fairies leave the wood"],"a2s2":["Puck anoints the right man","Lysander falls in love with Helena","Demetrius falls in love with Hermia"],"a3s1":["The workmen finish rehearsing","Titania falls in love with Bottom","Oberon catches Puck"],"a3s2":["The young men fight a duel","Oberon puts everything right while they sleep","Hermia goes home to her father"],"a4s1":["Egeus wins and Hermia must obey","Theseus lets the couples marry","The lovers forget each other"],"a4s2":["The play is cancelled","The workmen perform at the palace","Bottom tells them about the fairies"]},"contrast":[{"t":"Hermia and Helena","pts":["Hermia is short, dark and fiery; Helena is tall, fair and timid.","Hermia is loved and defies her father; Helena is unloved and humiliates herself.","Both stay faithful to one man while the men's love switches around them."],"ids":[18,136]},{"t":"Lysander and Demetrius","pts":["Lysander himself says they are equal in birth and wealth.","Puck cannot tell them apart; only their clothes identify them.","Lysander is cured by the end; Demetrius stays under the spell for life."],"ids":[23,478]},{"t":"Theseus and Oberon","pts":["Both are rulers about to be, or already, married to a strong woman.","Theseus wins Hippolyta with a sword; Oberon defeats Titania with a trick.","Both end the play by blessing marriages and restoring order."],"ids":[6,445]},{"t":"The lovers and Pyramus and Thisbe","pts":["Both stories have young lovers kept apart by fathers and a 'wall'.","Both lovers arrange to meet by night outside the city.","In the wood mistakes are mended; in the play-within-the-play they end in death."],"ids":[29,614]},{"t":"Athens and the wood","pts":["Athens is the world of law, fathers and daylight.","The wood is the world of night, dreams, fairies and desire.","The characters go into the wood confused and come back to Athens ready to marry."],"ids":[10,152]}],"keyfacts":[["Full title","A Midsummer Night's Dream"],["Written","About 1594 to 1596; first printed in 1600"],["Genre","Romantic comedy, with a play-within-the-play"],["Setting","Athens and a wood nearby, in legendary times, over two nights around May Day"],["Plots","Four: the wedding of Theseus and Hippolyta, the four young lovers, the fairy quarrel, and the workmen's play"],["Main conflict","Hermia's love for Lysander against her father's will and the law; the fairy king and queen's quarrel over a changeling boy"],["Antagonist","No real villain: the trouble comes from a harsh law, a fairy quarrel and Puck's mistakes"],["Turning point","Puck anoints the wrong Athenian (2.2), setting off the night's confusion"],["Climax","Act 3, Scene ii: both men love Helena, the women quarrel and the men go off to fight"],["Resolution","Oberon undoes the spells, Theseus overrules Egeus and the three couples marry"],["Language","Blank verse for the court, rhyming couplets for the lovers, short rhyming lines for fairy magic, prose for the workmen"],["Narrator","Puck speaks the epilogue directly to the audience"],["Length","About 2,100 lines, one of Shakespeare's shorter plays"]],"analysis":{"THESEUS":["Theseus frames the play as the ruler of Athens. He opens it longing for his wedding and closes it sending the couples to bed. He won Hippolyta in war ('I woo'd thee with my sword') and promises to marry her in a different key, with feasting and celebration.","In Act 1 he upholds the law, telling Hermia that her father is like a god to her. He offers her the 'mildest' outcome of a nun's life but does not change the law. When he meets the lovers in the morning, however, he simply overrules Egeus, a sign that comedy needs mercy and that the night has changed something.","In Act 5 he is a sceptic, dismissing the lovers' story as the work of imagination, like madness and poetry. The audience knows he is wrong. Yet he is generous to the workmen, valuing their 'simpleness and duty' and asking imagination to 'amend' their shadows: the same imagination he mocked."],"HERMIA":["Hermia is the play's most defiant woman. She tells the Duke she would rather die or live as a nun than marry Demetrius, and she plans the escape with Lysander. Even in the wood she insists on modesty, asking Lysander to lie further away.","Her dream of a serpent eating her heart while Lysander smiles comes true in a sense: she wakes alone, abandoned. When she finds Lysander loving Helena she is first bewildered ('Am not I Hermia? Are not you Lysander?') and then furious.","Her temper is comic: 'though she be but little, she is fierce'. She takes 'puppet' as an insult about her height and threatens to scratch Helena's eyes. At the end she wakes seeing things 'with parted eye', with her love and her father's consent restored."],"HELENA":["Helena is the play's lonely lover. She loves Demetrius, who once courted her and has dropped her for Hermia, and she understands love's blindness better than anyone: 'Love looks not with the eyes, but with the mind.'","She betrays Hermia's secret to win a moment with Demetrius, and in the wood she begs to be his spaniel, beaten and kicked. She knows that women are expected to be wooed, not to woo, and she breaks the rule anyway.","When both men suddenly love her, she cannot believe it and is sure she is being mocked. Her 'double cherry' speech mourns her lost friendship with Hermia. At the end she has Demetrius 'like a jewel, mine own, and not mine own', won by magic but hers all the same."],"LYSANDER":["Lysander begins as the reasonable lover. He points out that he is Demetrius's equal and that Hermia loves him, and he delivers the play's most famous line, 'The course of true love never did run smooth.'","Under the juice his love switches to Helena in an instant, and he claims it is reason that guides him. His cruelty to Hermia ('Away, you Ethiope!', 'You minimus') is shocking and comic at once, because he cannot see what has happened to him.","Once Puck cures him, he wakes confused and honest, telling Theseus that he cannot truly say how he came there. He is the lover who goes back to where he started."],"DEMETRIUS":["Demetrius is Egeus's choice for Hermia, but Lysander tells us he courted Helena first and won her heart. He treats Helena with contempt in the wood and even threatens her.","Oberon has the juice put on his eyes, and he wakes in love with Helena, praising her in extravagant language. He and Lysander then compete over her as they once competed over Hermia.","He is the only character who is never released from the spell. He says his love for Hermia melted 'as the snow' and that loving Helena is like returning to his natural taste. The happy ending depends on magic that is never undone."],"OBERON":["Oberon is the king of the fairies and the controller of the plot. He quarrels with Titania over the changeling boy and is not above humiliating her to win: his spell is meant to make her love 'some vile thing'.","He also pities Helena and tries to help, but his instructions are vague and he blames Puck for the mistake. Once he sees the confusion he works to put it right, ordering the fog and the cure.","When Titania gives him the boy he releases her, and the fairy king and queen bless the newly married couples. He is powerful and sometimes cruel, but his power ends in harmony."],"TITANIA":["Titania is the queen of the fairies and Oberon's equal in power. Her great speech describes the floods and confused seasons their quarrel has caused, and she takes responsibility: 'We are their parents and original.'","She keeps the Indian boy out of loyalty to his mother, her friend, who died giving birth to him. This makes Oberon's trick against her seem harsher.","Under the spell she adores Bottom, feeding him and calling him wise and beautiful, comic and slightly disturbing. When she wakes she simply says she dreamed she loved an ass, and joins Oberon to bless the marriages."],"PUCK":["Puck, or Robin Goodfellow, is Oberon's jester and messenger, a spirit of English folk tales who plays tricks on villagers. He is quick ('I'll put a girdle round about the earth in forty minutes') and delighted by chaos.","His mistake with the love-juice is honest, but he enjoys the results: 'Lord, what fools these mortals be!' He gives Bottom the ass's head out of pure mischief and leads the rivals around in the fog by imitating their voices.","He has the last word, speaking directly to the audience. He asks us to think of the play as a dream and promises to make amends, turning the audience into dreamers too."],"BOTTOM":["Nick Bottom the weaver wants to play every part in the workmen's play and has advice for everyone. He confuses long words ('aggravate', 'obscenely', 'exposition') and is completely confident in his own talent.","Turned into an ass, he is the only mortal to meet the fairies, and he takes being loved by a queen in his stride, asking for his head to be scratched and for hay. He also speaks the play's wisest line about love and reason.","His speech on waking is both ridiculous and strangely moving, as he tries and fails to describe his 'most rare vision'. In Act 5 he plays Pyramus with total commitment, dying several times over. Many readers find him the most lovable character in the play."]},"motifs":[["The moon","The moon times the wedding, lights the lovers' escape, stands for chaste Diana, and is played by Starveling with a lantern. It rules the night of madness and dreams.",[0,18,64]],["Eyes and seeing","Hermia wants her father to see with her eyes; Helena says love does not see with the eyes; the love-juice works on eyelids; Demetrius praises Helena's 'eyne'.",[4,9,19]],["Dreams and sleep","Characters fall asleep and wake changed. Hermia's nightmare, Titania's 'visions', Bottom's 'rare vision' and Puck's epilogue all blur dream and reality.",[28,45,50,66]],["Transformation","Bottom is 'translated'; loves switch overnight; a white flower turns purple; friends become enemies. The play draws on Ovid's book of changes, the Metamorphoses.",[30,10,26]],["Discord and harmony","The fairy quarrel upsets the seasons; the lovers quarrel; the workmen's play is 'hot ice'. In the end Hippolyta's 'musical discord' becomes the play's harmony.",[15,46,58]],["Theatre and illusion","The workmen fear the audience will mistake a play for reality; Theseus calls actors shadows; Puck asks the audience to imagine it all as a dream.",[11,60,55]],["Opposites side by side","The play keeps pairing contrasts: tall Helena and short Hermia, the fairy queen and the ass-headed weaver, rough workmen and a courtly audience, 'hot ice' and 'wondrous strange snow'.",[39,32,58]]],"symbols2":[["Love-in-idleness","The magic flower, turned purple by Cupid's arrow. It makes love an accident of whatever the eye sees first."],["The ass's head","Bottom's transformation shows love's blindness at its most extreme, and turns a common insult (calling someone an ass) into a literal joke."],["The changeling boy","The child Oberon and Titania fight over. He never speaks or appears in the text; he stands for power, possession and loyalty."],["The wood","A place outside the law of Athens where night, desire and magic take over, and from which everyone returns changed."],["Wall","The workmen's human wall with a chink for a mouth is a comic version of all the barriers between lovers: fathers, law and quarrels."],["The moon","Chastity (Diana), changeableness, madness ('lunacy') and the passing of time, all at once."]],"bigq":[["Is the ending really happy?",["All three couples marry and the fairies bless them","Demetrius is still under the spell for life","Titania is humiliated into giving up the boy","Hermia only escapes the law because Theseus changes his mind"],[47,48,17]],["Is the play about love or about power?",["Fathers, dukes and fairy kings control women's choices","Theseus won Hippolyta by force","Oberon wins the boy by a trick","Love escapes the law in the wood, but only by magic"],[2,1,25]],["Is love in the play rational?",["Lysander claims reason guides his new love","Helena says love sees with the mind, not the eyes","Bottom says reason and love keep little company","Hermia and Helena stay faithful whatever happens"],[27,9,31]],["Who is right about imagination, Theseus or Hippolyta?",["Theseus calls the lovers' story fancy","The audience has seen the fairies are real","Hippolyta trusts the lovers' shared story","Theseus later asks imagination to amend the actors"],[53,57,60]],["Is Bottom a fool or the play's hero?",["He mixes up words and wants every part","He speaks the wisest line about love","He stays calm and kind with the Fairy Queen","His dream speech is comic and moving at once"],[11,31,50]],["Why include Pyramus and Thisbe?",["It is a comic version of what could have happened to the lovers","It mocks bad acting and old-fashioned verse","It makes the audience think about how plays work","The courtiers' mockery reflects back on their own foolish night"],[58,62,60]]],"symbols":{"The moon":"\\bmoon","Eyes":"\\beyes?\\b|\\beyne\\b","Dreams and sleep":"\\bdream|\\bsleep|\\bslept\\b","Love":"\\blov(e|es|ed|er|ers|ing)\\b","Flowers":"\\bflowers?\\b|\\brose\\b|\\bbuds?\\b|\\bviolets?\\b","The ass":"\\bass\\b|\\bass's\\b|\\bass-head\\b"},"stagerules":[["cottage|quince","cottage"],["wood","wood"],["palace|apartment|room","palace"]],"solo":{"58":1,"188":1,"196":1,"490":1,"644":1},"iago":[["a2s1","Oberon learns of the flower love-in-idleness and sends Puck to fetch it."],["a2s1","He plans to make Titania love a beast until she gives up the changeling boy."],["a2s1","Seeing Demetrius scorn Helena, he gives Puck some juice for the 'Athenian' youth."],["a2s2","Oberon squeezes the juice on Titania's eyes."],["a2s2","Puck anoints Lysander by mistake; Lysander loves Helena."],["a3s1","Puck gives Bottom an ass's head; Titania wakes and loves him."],["a3s2","Oberon anoints Demetrius; both men now love Helena."],["a3s2","Oberon orders fog to keep the rivals apart until they sleep."],["a3s2","Puck puts the cure on Lysander's eyes."],["a4s1","Titania gives up the boy; Oberon frees her and Puck removes the ass's head."],["a4s1","The lovers wake paired correctly; Demetrius stays enchanted."],["a5s1","The fairies bless the three marriages."]],"rel":{"1":[["EGEUS","HERMIA","hate"],["HERMIA","LYSANDER","love"],["LYSANDER","HERMIA","love"],["DEMETRIUS","HERMIA","love"],["HELENA","DEMETRIUS","love"],["HELENA","HERMIA","deceive"],["THESEUS","HIPPOLYTA","love"]],"2":[["OBERON","TITANIA","hate"],["TITANIA","OBERON","hate"],["OBERON","TITANIA","deceive"],["DEMETRIUS","HELENA","hate"],["HELENA","DEMETRIUS","love"],["PUCK","OBERON","trust"],["LYSANDER","HELENA","love"]],"3":[["TITANIA","BOTTOM","love"],["LYSANDER","HELENA","love"],["DEMETRIUS","HELENA","love"],["LYSANDER","HERMIA","hate"],["HERMIA","HELENA","hate"],["LYSANDER","DEMETRIUS","hate"],["PUCK","LYSANDER","deceive"]],"4":[["OBERON","TITANIA","love"],["TITANIA","OBERON","love"],["DEMETRIUS","HELENA","love"],["LYSANDER","HERMIA","love"],["THESEUS","EGEUS","hate"],["QUINCE","BOTTOM","trust"]],"5":[["THESEUS","HIPPOLYTA","love"],["LYSANDER","HERMIA","love"],["DEMETRIUS","HELENA","love"],["THESEUS","QUINCE","trust"],["OBERON","THESEUS","trust"],["TITANIA","OBERON","love"]]},"examq":[["How does Shakespeare present love in A Midsummer Night's Dream?",[7,9,19,31,38,48]],["How does Shakespeare present the character of Bottom?",[11,30,31,44,50,62]],["Explore how Shakespeare presents conflict between parents and children, and law and love.",[2,3,6,47,5]],["How does Shakespeare use the fairies and the supernatural?",[16,19,25,36,65]],["How does Shakespeare present dreams and imagination?",[28,41,45,54,66]],["How does Shakespeare present Helena?",[9,21,22,38,48]]],"check":["Answered the exact question in the first sentence","Made a clear argument (a thesis) and kept to it","Used short, embedded quotations","Analysed key words and images, not just the meaning","Linked to context (fathers' power, Elizabeth, May Day, Ovid, the theatre)","Tracked change across the whole play: Athens, the wood, Athens","Considered the audience and dramatic effect, especially comedy","Mentioned an alternative reading or critic","Wrote a conclusion that answers the question","Checked spelling of names: Hermia, Helena, Lysander, Demetrius, Titania, Theseus"],"place":{"a1s1":"Athens","a1s2":"Athens","a2s1":"The wood","a2s2":"The wood","a3s1":"The wood","a3s2":"The wood","a4s1":"The wood","a4s2":"Athens","a5s1":"Athens"},"models":[[9,"Love is presented as blind and irrational, unable to judge what it loves.","Helena's couplet \"Love looks not with the eyes, but with the mind; And therefore is wing'd Cupid painted blind\" uses the familiar image of blind Cupid, and the neat rhyme makes it sound like a proverb.","The audience recognise the idea at once, and then watch it come literally true when the juice on the eyes makes the lovers change partners.","The image of blind Cupid was everywhere in Renaissance art and poetry, and the play's love-juice comes from Cupid's arrow.","Some readers argue the play also shows faithful love: Hermia and Helena never change, which suggests only male love is shown as fickle."],[31,"Bottom is presented as foolish on the surface but often wiser than the people around him.","Speaking to Titania, Bottom says \"reason and love keep little company together nowadays\", a simple prose remark in the middle of her extravagant verse.","The audience laugh at the ass's head, yet realise he has summed up the whole night's action better than the lovers can.","The wise fool was a common figure in Elizabethan drama, and Shakespeare's company included famous clowns who played such parts.","Some critics see Bottom simply as a figure of fun who never understands what happens to him."],[2,"Shakespeare presents the law of Athens as giving fathers almost total power over daughters.","Egeus says \"As she is mine I may dispose of her\", using the language of ownership: 'mine' and 'dispose' treat Hermia as property.","The audience is likely to side with Hermia, and Egeus's harshness makes her escape into the wood seem justified.","Elizabethan fathers had real power over their daughters' marriages, though not of life and death; the play exaggerates it.","Theseus finally overrules Egeus, which some see as the play rejecting the law and others as simply the Duke's power replacing the father's."],[16,"The fairies are presented as powerful beings whose quarrels affect the whole natural world.","Titania says of the floods and ruined seasons: \"We are their parents and original\", personifying the disasters as children born of the fairy quarrel.","The audience see that the fairies are not just pretty decorations; their disorder is serious and needs to be healed.","The play was written in the mid-1590s, when England suffered cold, wet summers and bad harvests.","Other readers stress the fairies' comedy and smallness, seeing them as playful rather than threatening."],[54,"Imagination is presented as both dangerous and creative.","Theseus links \"The lunatic, the lover, and the poet\" as all made of imagination, and his list moves from madness to art.","The audience see the irony: they have watched the fairies, so the sceptical Duke is wrong.","Shakespeare's theatre had no scenery, so plays always depended on the audience's imagination, as the epilogue reminds us.","Hippolyta's reply that the story 'grows to something of great constancy' offers another view: shared imagination becomes a kind of truth."],[21,"Helena is presented as a woman humiliated by unrequited love.","She begs Demetrius: \"I am your spaniel; and, Demetrius, The more you beat me, I will fawn on you\", a metaphor that reduces her to a loyal, beaten dog.","The audience may feel pity and discomfort at once, and her desperation makes her later disbelief easier to understand.","Women were expected to be wooed, not to woo, and Helena herself says she is breaking that rule.","Some productions play the speech for comedy, others as painful, showing that her character can be read either way."]],"lang":{"un":[[10,"Full of vexation come I","I come full of vexation"],[286,"My mistress with a monster is in love","My mistress is in love with a monster"],[18,"So will I grow, so live, so die, my lord","So I will grow, so live, so die, my lord"],[513,"'Tis strange, my Theseus, that these lovers speak of","That these lovers speak of is strange, my Theseus"]],"pun":[[174,"For lying so, Hermia, I do not lie","lie","pun","Lie means both lie down and tell a lie: Lysander promises he means no harm by lying beside her."],[490,"because it hath no bottom","bottom","pun","Bottom's name is also the word for the depth of something: his dream is bottomless."],[251,"Thou art translated","translated","wordplay","Translated usually means put into another language; Quince uses it to mean transformed."],[94,"I will aggravate my voice so","aggravate","malapropism","Bottom means moderate. His mistakes with long words are called malapropisms."],[42,"Demetrius loves your fair. O happy fair!","fair","pun","Fair means beauty and also light-coloured; Helena uses it as a noun for Hermia's beauty."]],"st":[[29,"The course of true love never did run smooth",[["course","Like a river, love has a path that twists."],["true","Even real love meets obstacles."],["never","A universal rule, not just this couple."]]],[257,"reason and love keep little company together nowadays",[["reason","Judgement, which love ignores."],["love","The force that drives the whole play."],["nowadays","A comic, everyday word for a timeless truth."]]],[644,"If we shadows have offended",[["we","Puck speaks for all the actors."],["shadows","Actors are illusions, like dreams."],["offended","A modest apology that invites applause."]]]]},"dir":[[136,"I am your spaniel","How should Helena play this?",[["Desperate and serious","Painful to watch; we pity her."],["Clinging to his leg","Physical comedy; we laugh at her."],["Angry, almost sarcastic","She knows how absurd she sounds."]]],[256,"I pray thee, gentle mortal, sing again","How does Titania approach Bottom?",[["Dreamy and tender","Her love seems real, which is funnier."],["Hungry and physical","The scene becomes darker and more erotic."],["Regal and commanding","She is still a queen, even in love."]]],[312,"Lord, what fools these mortals be!","How does Puck say this?",[["To Oberon, laughing","He enjoys the chaos he caused."],["Straight to the audience","We become fellow spectators."],["Fondly, almost sad","He feels for the foolish humans."]]],[362,"How low am I, thou painted maypole?","How far does the quarrel go?",[["Hermia physically attacks","Slapstick comedy at its height."],["The men hold her back","It becomes a joke about the men's helplessness."],["Real tears on both sides","The end of a friendship, painful under the jokes."]]],[448,"Methought I was enamour'd of an ass","How does Titania react on waking?",[["Laughing at herself","The quarrel is over and both forgive."],["Horrified and ashamed","Oberon's trick looks cruel."],["Coldly, to Oberon","She knows he did this to her."]]],[514,"The lunatic, the lover, and the poet","How does Theseus deliver this?",[["Mocking, to the court","He is the voice of common sense."],["Thoughtfully, half believing","The speech becomes a tribute to imagination."],["While fairies watch unseen","The audience see that he is wrong."]]]],"ev":{"ctx":[["524","614"],["113","312"],["642","479","524"],["2","251","524"],["109","129","644"],["107","118","642"],["468","2","634"],["10","11","17"],["128","447"],["65","286","577"],["577","209","226"],["490","251"]],"cr":{"dreams":[["128","129","644"],["490","484","514"]],"love":[["58","468","479"],["257","435","490"]],"gender":[["6","17","114"]],"change":[["258","260","152"]],"theatre":[["524","577"]]}}};
